(function () {
  'use strict';
  const REVISION = 'ALFRED-CETA-ACADEMIC-FINAL-20261028';
  const GAP_PATCHES = [{"finding":"CV3-001","module":"M01","page":"L01-0-05","paragraphs":[["PPE decisions and the cited standard · Concept","Electrical protective equipment is selected from a qualified task-specific hazard assessment, not from circuit voltage alone. Distinguish an arc-flash risk assessment, the documented arc-flash boundary, shock approach boundaries, and the applicable protective work procedures. ETA cites NFPA 70E Table 130.5(C); table numbers and their contents can change between editions. First identify the edition and the exact referenced table, verify its scope, and use the employer-approved procedure rather than copying a table value from memory. De-energizing and establishing an electrically safe work condition are preferred to depending on PPE."],["PPE decisions and the cited standard · Worked reasoning","Paper-only scenario: an arc-flash label lists incident energy and a boundary, but the job description and the installation condition have changed. Step 1: do not infer permission from the label or gloves alone. Step 2: confirm whether the task, equipment condition and label/assessment are still applicable. Step 3: a qualified person consults the controlled edition and performs the required assessment, then specifies tools, boundaries, work permit and PPE. Stop if any required information is missing."],["PPE decisions and the cited standard · Independent check","Check: explain why a 480 V label is insufficient to choose an arc-rated ensemble and why a table from another NFPA 70E edition may not apply. Answer: available fault energy, clearing time, task, condition, method, and applicable edition can change the outcome; voltage alone is not a complete arc-flash assessment. Do not attempt energized work from this exercise."]]},{"finding":"CV3-002","module":"M11","page":"L08-0-03","paragraphs":[["Silicon versus germanium forward-bias calculations · Concept","A forward-biased junction does not have a universal fixed voltage drop. For introductory hand calculations, a constant-drop approximation is often useful: about 0.7 V for an ordinary silicon diode and about 0.3 V for a germanium diode at a representative current and temperature. Real current follows a nonlinear I–V curve; temperature, current, manufacturer and diode type change forward voltage. State the model and assumptions before calculating."],["Silicon versus germanium forward-bias calculations · Worked reasoning","Paper circuit: 5.0 V DC source, 470 Ω series resistor and one forward-oriented diode. Approximate silicon case: resistor voltage 5.0−0.7=4.3 V; I=4.3/470=0.00915 A≈9.15 mA; P_diode=0.7×0.00915≈6.4 mW and P_resistor=4.3×0.00915≈39 mW. Germanium approximation: resistor voltage 5.0−0.3=4.7 V; I=4.7/470=0.0100 A=10 mA; P_diode=0.3×0.010=3.0 mW, P_resistor≈47 mW. Check that source power equals diode plus resistor power in each case."],["Silicon versus germanium forward-bias calculations · Independent check","Independent check: replace the 470 Ω resistor with 1.0 kΩ; using the same assumed forward drops, calculate current and diode power for silicon and germanium. Expected: silicon 4.3 mA and 3.01 mW; germanium 4.7 mA and 1.41 mW. In an actual repair, read the applicable device datasheet rather than assuming a material-wide drop."]]},{"finding":"CV3-003","module":"M15","page":"L29-0-02","paragraphs":[["Why solder work creates health hazards · Concept","Heating flux can release rosin or other flux-decomposition fumes and airborne irritants; these can irritate eyes/airways and may cause occupational asthma in sensitized workers. Lead poisoning is a separate chronic metal-toxicity risk: lead may enter through contaminated hands, food or work surfaces and can affect the nervous system, kidneys, blood formation and reproduction. Normal solder fume is not accurately described as only vaporized lead. Lead-free solder still uses flux and can still produce harmful fumes. Use effective local extraction, suitable hygiene, approved handling and facility exposure controls."],["Why solder work creates health hazards · Worked reasoning","Case: a worker uses lead-free solder and says extraction can be switched off because no lead is present. Separate the hazards: (1) flux fume remains; (2) lead-free removes one source of lead contamination, not all irritation/particle risks; (3) a shared bench may still have residues from leaded materials. Confirm the materials and process controls through the facility risk assessment and SDS, then maintain extraction and no food/drink or hand-to-mouth contact."],["Why solder work creates health hazards · Independent check","Check: name one fume-related and one lead-related health outcome and the likely exposure-control route. Example answer: flux inhalation can worsen respiratory symptoms; lead uptake through contaminated hands can injure the nervous system. Provide ventilation at the source, safe handling and hygiene; seek approved occupational-health guidance for suspected exposure."]]},{"finding":"CV3-004","module":"M15","page":"L12-0-04","paragraphs":[["SMT, BGA and gold-contact handling · Concept","SMT components require package-specific handling, correct orientation and controlled thermal profiles. Some moisture-sensitive devices have labelled moisture sensitivity level (MSL), package storage requirements and maximum time out of dry packaging (floor life); track exposure and use qualified recovery/baking instructions when the specified window is exceeded. Ball-grid arrays (BGAs) hide solder joints under the package, so rework depends on qualified temperature profile, alignment, cleaning and inspection—not visual inspection of the top of the package alone. Edge gold-finger contacts should be kept clean and protected from abrasion, skin oils and aggressive cleaners; hold boards by approved edges and follow the connector maker cleaning procedure."],["SMT, BGA and gold-contact handling · Worked reasoning","Paper receiving case: a sealed BGA moisture-barrier bag was opened on a previous shift and exposure time was not logged. Do not send it directly to reflow because it looks dry. Locate the package MSL label and manufacturer handling specification, reconstruct or conservatively disposition exposure history, and route uncertain material for approved storage/bake or quality review. Inspect mating gold-finger contacts for wear/contamination without scraping off the plating."],["SMT, BGA and gold-contact handling · Independent check","Check: why can a BGA joint be defective even when its visible top surface looks fine? Hidden solder balls may bridge, crack or remain open. Explain why floor-life decisions and plating protection must follow the part and process documentation rather than improvised heating or abrasion."]]},{"finding":"CV3-005","module":"M02","page":"L04-0-03","paragraphs":[["NIST spelling and SI unit names · Concept","In unit names, a prefix is attached to the unit name without a space: kilo + ohm is spelled kilohm, and mega + ohm is spelled megohm. The “o” of kilo/mega is dropped before ohm under the established spelling conventions; spellings such as “kiloohm” and “megaohm” are nonstandard. Unit symbols retain their own case-sensitive rules: kΩ for kilohm, MΩ for megohm, mΩ for milliohm. Use the official NIST SI style guidance when checking a named exception; names and symbols are not interchangeable strings."],["NIST spelling and SI unit names · Worked reasoning","Write 4.7 kΩ as “4.7 kilohms” and 2 MΩ as “2 megohms.” Quantitatively, 4.7 kΩ = 4,700 Ω and 2 MΩ = 2,000,000 Ω. Contrast 2 mΩ = 0.002 Ω: changing uppercase M to lowercase m changes the factor by 10^9, so symbol case is a measurement issue, not typography alone."],["NIST spelling and SI unit names · Independent check","Check: spell the names of 10 kΩ and 3 MΩ, then convert them to ohms. Answer: ten kilohms = 10,000 Ω; three megohms = 3,000,000 Ω. Explain why “3 mΩ” would be unsafe to treat as equivalent to “3 MΩ.”"]]},{"finding":"CV3-006","module":"M02","page":"L04-0-03","paragraphs":[["Multiplying powers with negative exponents · Concept","For equal nonzero base b, multiplying b^a by b^c adds exponents: b^a × b^c = b^(a+c). A negative exponent means a reciprocal, not a negative quantity: 10^(−2) = 1/100 = 0.01. This rule links scientific notation, unit prefixes and resistor/current conversions."],["Multiplying powers with negative exponents · Worked reasoning","Worked: 10^3 × 10^(−2) = 10^(3−2) = 10^1 = 10. Verify independently: 1000×0.01=10. A second electronics case: (2×10^3 Ω)(3×10^(−3) A)=(2×3)×10^(3−3) V=6 V. Keep the numerical coefficients, exponent arithmetic and final SI unit separate."],["Multiplying powers with negative exponents · Independent check","Check: compute (4×10^(−6) A)(5×10^3 Ω). Answer: 20×10^(−3) V = 0.020 V = 20 mV. If a result seems off by a thousand, write each exponent as a decimal check before blaming the circuit."]]},{"finding":"CV3-007","module":"M16","page":"L11-0-03","paragraphs":[["Distributive Boolean algebra · Concept","In Boolean algebra, adjacency is AND, plus is OR, and the distributive law is A(B+C)=AB+AC. Interpret it as: A must be true AND at least one of B or C must be true; equivalently either the A-and-B branch or A-and-C branch must be true. This is a logical identity for binary inputs, not ordinary addition of numeric currents."],["Distributive Boolean algebra · Worked reasoning","Prove the identity with two representative rows and the rule. If A=0, both sides are 0 regardless of B,C. If A=1, left side becomes (B OR C); right side becomes B OR C. Therefore the sides agree for all 2×2×2 combinations. Draw two AND gates feeding an OR gate for the expanded expression, and a B/C OR gate feeding an AND gate for the factored expression."],["Distributive Boolean algebra · Independent check","Check: factor XY+XZ and simplify 0(B+C)+1(B+C). Answer: X(Y+Z) and B+C. A schematic implementing either form should be tested with all eight A/B/C input combinations to distinguish a true equivalence from an accidental match on one row."]]},{"finding":"CV3-008","module":"M13","page":"L09-1-05","paragraphs":[["One source, several regulated voltage rails · Concept","A multiple-output supply can create different rails with multiple separately controlled converter stages or different transformer secondary windings/taps followed by rectification and regulation. Outputs are not automatically isolated: +12 V and +5 V converters may share a common return, whereas appropriately designed isolated secondary outputs can have floating returns. Regulation, load limits, reference choice, common-mode behavior and safety isolation must be checked per output."],["One source, several regulated voltage rails · Worked reasoning","Paper power tree: a 12 V DC source feeds two regulators, one 5 V/0.40 A logic rail and one 3.3 V/0.20 A sensor rail, both sharing ground. Output powers are 2.0 W and 0.66 W; combined delivered output is 2.66 W before conversion losses. If the two hypothetical converters each operate at 80% efficiency, expected input power is 2.66/0.8 = 3.325 W, or about 0.277 A from 12 V. This does not guarantee real rail independence or permit tying together two outputs."],["One source, several regulated voltage rails · Independent check","Check: why is measuring +5 V relative to the 3.3 V rail misleading when both share ground? You would measure 1.7 V, not the rail’s nominal ground-referenced value. Show a safe paper fault tree for “5 V good, 3.3 V missing” that tests the branch regulator and load instead of the source blindly."]]},{"finding":"CV3-009","module":"M19","page":"L17-0-02","paragraphs":[["Zigbee compared with wired interfaces · Concept","Zigbee is a low-power wireless networking technology built around IEEE 802.15.4 radio/MAC foundations and higher-layer networking/application functions, used in many sensor/automation products. It is not a pin-to-pin MCU serial protocol like I²C or SPI and is not a replacement for a direct UART wire. Devices participate in an organized wireless network, typically with a coordinator and, in supported mesh arrangements, routers and end devices. Actual band, reach, latency, security and interoperability depend on the device profile and regional rules."],["Zigbee compared with wired interfaces · Worked reasoning","Paper decision: two ICs on the same PCB exchange synchronous register data. SPI or I²C may fit because the devices share a short electrical connection. Battery sensors distributed across a building have no shared data cable; a qualified Zigbee network may fit if power and supported topology are suitable. Neither option is automatically secure or compatible merely from the word “serial.”"],["Zigbee compared with wired interfaces · Independent check","Check: identify one wire-level issue when connecting an I²C device (pull-ups/voltage and shared reference) and one Zigbee network issue (joining credentials, range/interference, routing, supported profile). Explain why a logic analyzer on SDA cannot directly decode a Zigbee radio channel."]]},{"finding":"CV3-010","module":"M17","page":"L11-0-02","paragraphs":[["SR, D, JK and T flip-flops · Concept","A flip-flop stores a bit but its input rule depends on its type. D flip-flop: on the active clock edge Q_next=D. T flip-flop: T=0 holds, T=1 toggles. JK flip-flop: J,K=00 hold, 10 set, 01 reset, 11 toggle. A basic SR latch has set/reset inputs and a forbidden/undefined simultaneous assertion condition for its particular implementation; never assume all SR circuits use identical active polarity. Edge-triggered flip-flops differ from level-sensitive latches."],["SR, D, JK and T flip-flops · Worked reasoning","Worked state comparison: start Q=0. On one clock edge, D=1 gives Q_next=1; T=1 gives Q_next=1; JK=11 also toggles to Q_next=1. At the next edge with the same inputs, D remains 1 but T and JK=11 toggle back to 0. This separates storing a sampled value from toggling state."],["SR, D, JK and T flip-flops · Independent check","Check: write Q_next after one edge starting Q=1 for D=0, T=0, JK=01 and JK=11. Answers: 0, 1, 0, 0. Explain why a counter using T flip-flops often relies on controlled toggling rather than constantly loading a logic 1."]]},{"finding":"CV3-011","module":"M17","page":"L11-0-05","paragraphs":[["Showing digits versus letters on displays · Concept","A seven-segment display has individually controlled segments a through g; a BCD-to-seven-segment driver commonly maps 0000–1001 to decimal digits 0–9. Some letters such as A, b, C, d, E and F can be approximated with segments, but many letters are ambiguous or illegible. An alphanumeric 14/16-segment display or a dot-matrix display needs additional segments/pixels and a character-to-pattern map; a character LCD often includes a character-generator ROM or custom glyph memory."],["Showing digits versus letters on displays · Worked reasoning","Worked encoding: a hex display reads a 4-bit value 1010 (decimal 10) and should show A. A BCD-only 0–9 decoder may not define input 1010, so do not assume it displays A. Select a hexadecimal-compatible segment decoder or explicitly map each seven-segment output in the driver logic. For a word such as “HELLO”, choose a dot-matrix or alphanumeric character display and map glyph patterns rather than expecting a decimal BCD driver to support all letters."],["Showing digits versus letters on displays · Independent check","Check: distinguish digit selection, glyph selection and multiplex scanning. The character code selects a desired pattern; driver transistors/logic energize segments; scanning time-shares devices fast enough for persistence. Why is a BCD-only numeric decoder insufficient for arbitrary ASCII text?"]]},{"finding":"CV3-012","module":"M21","page":"L27-0-03","paragraphs":[["Computer installation and first-boot setup · Concept","A reproducible computer setup begins by confirming the system requirements and authorized OS image, collecting a backup/recovery path, recording device/firmware identity, checking installation media integrity, selecting supported storage/partition options, then installing OS, trusted drivers, updates and intended applications. Complete first-boot identity, security settings and network configuration, then verify device manager/status, storage, software versions, permissions, backup/recovery and a representative application test. These are conceptual supervised procedures; avoid reinstalling or wiping a live machine for a practice exercise."],["Computer installation and first-boot setup · Worked reasoning","Paper scenario: a test laptop has a newly fitted SSD and a documented corporate image. First verify serial and image compatibility; then show the order media verification → imaging → first boot → chipset/network drivers → security patches → domain or approved identity setup → tested I/O → baseline record. A working desktop alone is insufficient if a measurement instrument driver or network policy is missing."],["Computer installation and first-boot setup · Independent check","Check: write a five-line deployment acceptance record that includes installed OS/image revision, driver state, approved network access, expected application launch and rollback/recovery evidence. Explain why a generic Internet ISO is not automatically the correct source for controlled equipment."]]},{"finding":"CV3-013","module":"M21","page":"L17-0-04","paragraphs":[["Choose the correct software utility · Concept","Different utilities act on different fault layers. A filesystem check inspects logical volume metadata; a SMART/storage-health utility reports drive indicators but does not fix the filesystem; an anti-malware utility detects/remediates known malicious software but does not replace backup; a backup/restore utility copies or restores protected data; a network diagnostic tool (for example ping, IP configuration or name lookup) tests specific connectivity layers. Disk imaging and defragmentation have different goals and risks; SSD maintenance uses device/OS-supported procedures."],["Choose the correct software utility · Worked reasoning","Worked triage: after an unsafe shutdown, a volume mounts inconsistently, but storage health reports no clear hardware fault. Back up accessible critical data using approved procedures before repair attempts; distinguish a logical filesystem problem from a failing storage medium, then choose authorized checks. Running a malware scan or changing Wi-Fi settings is not a targeted filesystem repair. Preserve evidence and avoid destructive utilities without authorization."],["Choose the correct software utility · Independent check","Check: match these symptoms to initial evidence tools: domain-name failure with IP connection working → DNS/name lookup; suspected ransomware → incident-response-approved security scan/isolation; accidental deletion → restore from verified backup; SMART failure → storage replacement/escalation. A successful tool run does not prove another layer is healthy."]]},{"finding":"CV3-014","module":"M21","page":"L17-0-05","paragraphs":[["Voltage surge versus malicious software · Concept","A power surge is an electrical transient from sources such as switching, faults or lightning-related coupling; protective measures depend on installation design and may include correctly rated surge protective devices, earthing/bonding, suitable UPS and approved electrical work. Malware is a software/security threat propagated through malicious files, links, compromised accounts or vulnerabilities; mitigations include updates, least privilege, MFA, trusted downloads, endpoint protection and tested offline/versioned backups. A surge protector cannot remove a virus, and anti-malware software cannot stop a damaging transient."],["Voltage surge versus malicious software · Worked reasoning","Paper incident A: equipment fails immediately after a storm and shows physical damage. Preserve the unit safely, identify electrical inspection and power-quality evidence; do not defeat protection to retry it. Incident B: files are encrypted and login sessions unusual. Isolate affected network access under incident procedures, preserve logs and invoke approved cyber response. Keep the hazard mechanisms separate even if both cases interrupt work."],["Voltage surge versus malicious software · Independent check","Check: give two preventive controls for each threat and one remaining limitation. For example, a surge device has a defined energy rating/installation constraint; MFA does not eliminate phishing or all account compromise. Backups support recovery from both categories but do not prevent the original incident."]]},{"finding":"CV3-015","module":"M21","page":"L17-0-02","paragraphs":[["Internet access, security and applications · Concept","An Internet connection is a chain: physical/wireless link → local addressing and gateway → name resolution → routing/provider → remote service/application. To connect an authorized computer, select the approved network, authenticate, verify its IP address/mask/gateway/DNS, then test local reachability and an approved external service. An IP link does not prove DNS or the target website works. Common Internet applications include web (HTTP/HTTPS), email, remote meetings/VoIP, file transfer/cloud services and updates; each has distinct requirements and security risks."],["Internet access, security and applications · Worked reasoning","Worked diagnosis: an authorized workstation reaches the default gateway and a numeric public IP, but a hostname fails. Local link and some upstream routing are likely working; check the documented DNS server, lookups and filtering before replacing cables. Protect the process with strong unique credentials, multifactor authentication, approved updates and phishing awareness, and use encrypted connections for sensitive traffic."],["Internet access, security and applications · Independent check","Check: order these checks for a “no website” complaint: cable/Wi-Fi link, IP configuration, gateway, DNS, remote service/application. Explain why email or video calling may still fail when ordinary web pages load; different ports, authentication and service endpoints may be involved."]]},{"finding":"CV3-016","module":"M24","page":"L19-0-07","paragraphs":[["Microphone families and application choices · Concept","A moving-coil dynamic microphone generates a small electrical voltage by motion of a coil in a magnetic field; it is typically rugged and does not inherently require phantom power. A condenser microphone changes capacitance with diaphragm motion and requires electronic bias/impedance conversion; many professional versions accept phantom power through appropriate balanced interfaces, but power needs vary by model. Electret condenser capsules retain a built-in charge yet often need bias for their FET. Directional patterns (omnidirectional, cardioid, etc.) affect rejection and placement independently of the transducer type."],["Microphone families and application choices · Worked reasoning","Paper choice: a loud workshop interview with high background noise favors a rugged directional microphone close to the talker, with correct gain staging and connector/power compatibility. A quiet acoustic measurement might favor a calibrated measurement microphone. Neither microphone type is guaranteed better in every condition; choose by frequency response, SPL tolerance, polarization, power and environment."],["Microphone families and application choices · Independent check","Check: why may connecting a condenser mic to an unpowered mic input yield no signal, while a dynamic mic may work? Explain why phantom compatibility must be checked before switching it on, especially with adapters or unbalanced wiring."]]},{"finding":"CV3-017","module":"M24","page":"L19-0-14","paragraphs":[["How a moving-coil loudspeaker is built · Concept","A common dynamic loudspeaker has a fixed magnet and pole-piece gap, a voice coil on a former, a diaphragm/cone attached to that coil, a flexible surround, a spider that centers the coil and supplies suspension, and terminals. Alternating current through the voice coil produces a changing force in the magnet field; coil/diaphragm motion moves air and creates sound pressure. The spider and surround constrain motion while allowing travel. Nominal impedance, power/thermal limit and excursion limits govern amplifier compatibility."],["How a moving-coil loudspeaker is built · Worked reasoning","Paper fault: audio is distorted and rubbing is audible even at low level. A damaged surround or shifted coil may cause the diaphragm/coil to scrape the gap; an amplifier with adequate voltage headroom cannot correct this mechanical fault. Compare the measured signal and speaker behavior, then refer to approved inspection/service procedure; do not infer the cause from a label alone."],["How a moving-coil loudspeaker is built · Independent check","Check: identify which part generates electromagnetic force (voice coil), maintains the magnetic gap (magnet/pole structure), centers moving parts (spider) and pushes air (cone/diaphragm). Why might a speaker with nominal 4 Ω be unsuitable for an amplifier rated for 8 Ω minimum?"]]},{"finding":"CV3-018","module":"M24","page":"L19-0-08","paragraphs":[["Audio and video output levels · Concept","Signal levels are defined with units and load/termination. Typical analog consumer audio line level is often referenced to −10 dBV, approximately 0.316 V RMS; professional nominal analog line level is often +4 dBu, approximately 1.228 V RMS using 0 dBu = 0.775 V RMS. Microphone output is usually far smaller, often millivolts, varying greatly with sound pressure/sensitivity. Speaker output is a power stage and can span volts to tens of volts depending on load/power. Composite analog video traditionally uses about 1 V peak-to-peak into the specified 75 Ω termination. HDMI/USB digital outputs require interface-specific logic/link compliance, not an invented universal analog signal voltage."],["Audio and video output levels · Worked reasoning","Worked: the voltage ratio between 1.228 V RMS and 0.316 V RMS is about 3.89; for equal impedance, 20 log10(3.89) ≈ 11.8 dB. Do not confuse dBu (voltage reference) and dBV or interpret speaker volts without impedance and waveform. An oscilloscope open-circuit 1 Vpp reading on a 75 Ω video interface may differ from the intended terminated measurement."],["Audio and video output levels · Independent check","Check: identify microphone, consumer line, professional line and speaker-level signals by orders of magnitude, then explain why loading matters. In an actual system consult the specific connector/interface and equipment specifications; “line level” is not a universal exact voltage."]]},{"finding":"CV3-019","module":"M25","page":"L19-0-09","paragraphs":[["Compare seven-segment, LCD and OLED displays · Concept","Seven-segment LEDs are simple and legible for digits with straightforward drivers, but poor for arbitrary text and high-resolution graphics; each illuminated LED segment requires managed current. LCDs modulate light rather than emitting it directly and commonly need a backlight in transmissive displays; they can have efficient static image use but viewing-angle and response characteristics vary by panel type. OLED pixels emit their own light, enabling deep blacks and no full-panel backlight, but organic-emitter aging, brightness limits and static-image retention/burn-in are design concerns. Compare a specific device instead of asserting a technology is always best."],["Compare seven-segment, LCD and OLED displays · Worked reasoning","Paper selection: a low-cost instrument showing only three digits could use a multiplexed seven-segment module; a handheld menu interface might use an LCD; a high-contrast graphical panel might use OLED if static UI lifetime and power profile are considered. Analyze readability, temperature, lifetime, power at expected content, driver complexity and repair availability."],["Compare seven-segment, LCD and OLED displays · Independent check","Check: for a continuously displayed fixed logo, identify a likely OLED lifetime concern; for an LCD in darkness, identify the need for an appropriate backlight; for seven-segment, explain the limitation in writing long alphabetic words. Give one legitimate advantage and one limitation for each."]]},{"finding":"CV3-020","module":"M25","page":"L19-0-10","paragraphs":[["Photodiode, phototransistor and LDR symbols · Concept","The ordinary diode schematic symbol denotes a PN junction; a photodiode adds two small arrows pointing inward toward its junction to denote incident light. A phototransistor has the BJT emitter/collector structure with two light arrows pointing into the base region; the base lead may be omitted in some drawings. A light-dependent resistor (LDR/photoresistor) uses a resistor symbol, often inside a circle, with light arrows pointing toward it. By contrast, an LED uses small arrows directed *outward* from its diode to show emitted light. Exact drawing conventions vary by IEC/ANSI symbol library; use the legend and datasheet for unfamiliar marks."],["Photodiode, phototransistor and LDR symbols · Worked reasoning","Three-paper-symbol identification: (1) diode with inward arrows is photodiode; (2) transistor with inward arrows is phototransistor; (3) resistor body with inward arrows is LDR. The arrows indicate response to illumination, not current direction. In a circuit, a photodiode can produce photocurrent; a phototransistor provides light-controlled gain; an LDR changes resistance with illumination. A photovoltaic cell can power a load under light; it is not automatically interchangeable with these detectors."],["Photodiode, phototransistor and LDR symbols · Independent check","Check: if a symbol has a diode junction plus outward arrows, it denotes an LED rather than a photodiode. Explain how the symbol body differentiates a photodiode from an LDR, then choose the light sensor for fast optical data detection versus a slow ambient-light control and justify the choice."]]},{"finding":"CV3-021","module":"M26","page":"L20-0-09","paragraphs":[["Static fields and propagating radio waves · Concept","An electrostatic electric field exists around a static charge distribution, and a static magnetic field can exist around steady current. Radio-frequency radiation is associated with time-varying electric and magnetic fields coupled through Maxwell’s equations; in the far field of a plane wave, E and H are mutually perpendicular and transverse to the propagation direction. A static E field by itself is not a traveling RF signal. Near an antenna, reactive and radiating fields can coexist and simple far-field relations may not apply."],["Static fields and propagating radio waves · Worked reasoning","Paper comparison: a charged but isolated metal plate produces an electric field without intentionally transmitting a modulated message. In a transmitting antenna, time-varying current/charge generate changing fields; sufficiently far away a propagating wave transports energy. For a plane wave traveling along +x, electric field can point along y and magnetic field along z. Reversing polarization orientation affects receive-antenna coupling."],["Static fields and propagating radio waves · Independent check","Check: why is “radio waves are just static electric fields” incorrect? Give the field orientation for a far-field plane wave and name one reason to avoid treating near-antenna exposure with ideal plane-wave assumptions."]]},{"finding":"CV3-022","module":"M26","page":"L20-0-01","paragraphs":[["Analog modulation, radio services and cellular standards · Concept","AM varies carrier amplitude and FM varies instantaneous frequency; analog television historically combined picture and sound with service-dependent modulation, while digital TV uses digitally modulated channels. Land-mobile radio (LMR) describes a two-way communication *service/use case*, not one universal modulation. CDMA names a spread-spectrum multiple-access approach also used in particular cellular generations; GSM is a 2G cellular system family, LTE a 4G-era packet-based cellular standard, and 5G NR a later radio-access technology. These labels live at different conceptual layers: modulation of symbols, resource sharing/multiple access, and an end-to-end air-interface/network standard. Treat legacy versus current regional use as implementation-dependent."],["Analog modulation, radio services and cellular standards · Worked reasoning","Paper sorting exercise: “FM” belongs in the modulation column; “CDMA” in the access/multiplexing column; “GSM/LTE/5G NR” in the cellular-standard family column; “LMR” in the service category. A system might use different modulation and coding depending on generation, band and radio conditions. Do not conclude that saying “5G” specifies the exact carrier frequency or one simple FM modulation."],["Analog modulation, radio services and cellular standards · Independent check","Check: compare a broadcast FM receiver, an LMR push-to-talk radio, an LTE handset and a digital TV receiver by directionality, service objective and network/receiver needs. Explain why a modulation method does not, by itself, define encryption, roaming or compatible standards."]]},{"finding":"CV3-023","module":"M27","page":"L20-0-07","paragraphs":[["Aviation, land-mobile and maritime radio · Concept","Two-way radio services are organized by operating purpose, channels, coverage, equipment and regulation. Aviation voice communication often serves pilots and air-traffic/ground operations using internationally coordinated VHF aeronautical service channels and application-specific modulation. Maritime VHF serves vessel-to-vessel and vessel-to-shore safety/operational communications using maritime channel plans and distress procedures. Land-mobile radio (LMR) serves dispatch, public safety and business portable/mobile users, often by direct radio-to-radio or repeater-assisted push-to-talk. Real frequencies, channel plans and legal authorization are jurisdiction-dependent; do not improvise transmissions on safety-service channels."],["Aviation, land-mobile and maritime radio · Worked reasoning","Directionality matters separately from service: simplex is strictly one-way information transfer on the defined link; half-duplex supports both directions at different times, like a typical shared push-to-talk channel; full-duplex allows simultaneous two-way conversation. A repeater can extend coverage but may require channel offsets, access signaling and isolation. For example, a short-range direct path working does not prove the configured repeater input is reachable."],["Aviation, land-mobile and maritime radio · Independent check","Check: for a harbor distress call, air-traffic instruction and factory dispatch, identify maritime, aviation and land-mobile service respectively. Then classify typical push-to-talk as half-duplex (not simplex in the strict information-flow sense) and explain why repeater access configuration matters."]]},{"finding":"CV3-024","module":"M27","page":"L20-0-08","paragraphs":[["Apply actual standards to the correct radio system · Concept","“Wireless telephone, video and data” is not governed by one universal ANSI/TIA radio standard. Match standards to *equipment and interface*. ANSI/TIA-102 is a family related to Project 25 (P25) public-safety land-mobile radio, including voice/data air-interface and interoperability specifications; it should not be represented as the standard for ordinary LTE/5G handset operation. Cellular radio-access standardization is predominantly in the 3GPP ecosystem (e.g. LTE and 5G NR). IEEE 802.11 governs Wi-Fi PHY/MAC families; ANSI/TIA-568 concerns structured premises cabling rather than a wireless air interface. Verify the relevant document edition for any actual equipment."],["Apply actual standards to the correct radio system · Worked reasoning","Paper application: a county P25 handheld interoperates with a P25 base system—inspect the applicable ANSI/TIA-102 family/interface requirements. A cellular handset connects to a 5G NR tower—identify 3GPP radio/network standard requirements instead. A camera feeds Ethernet through a structured cable system—then applicable TIA cabling standards may describe the physical plant, not the cellular radio link."],["Apply actual standards to the correct radio system · Independent check","Check: give one concrete example of an applicable ANSI/TIA standard family (ANSI/TIA-102 for P25 LMR) and one example where it is *not* the correct radio-interface authority (5G NR). Explain why standards for cabling, towers, modulation and cellular protocols must not be conflated."]]},{"finding":"CV3-025","module":"M27","page":"L20-0-08","paragraphs":[["Satellite uplink, payload, and downlink · Concept","A satellite link contains a transmitting Earth terminal, an uplink through free space, spacecraft receive antenna/front end, a relay or regenerative payload, a downlink transmitter and receiving ground equipment. A bent-pipe transponder typically receives, frequency-converts/amplifies and retransmits a signal; a regenerative payload can demodulate/process information before retransmission. Satellite position and orbit affect footprint, delay, relative motion/Doppler and pointing/tracking. Geostationary satellites appear nearly stationary from suitable ground locations but have long propagation paths; LEO satellites move across the sky and often require tracking and handovers."],["Satellite uplink, payload, and downlink · Worked reasoning","Worked diagram in words: ground A → UPLINK → satellite payload → DOWNLINK → ground B. If the one-way propagation path spans roughly 36,000 km up and 36,000 km down, free-space propagation alone is at least 72,000/300,000 ≈ 0.24 s, excluding encoding, queues and other geometry. This illustrative distance is not a complete round-trip calculation or a universal GEO path length; actual geometry and service routing matter."],["Satellite uplink, payload, and downlink · Independent check","Check: distinguish uplink from downlink, identify what a transponder does, and explain why an LEO terminal may need tracking. What happens if a station has an intact transmitter but its dish is mispointed? Link budget/received power can fail even if RF output exists."]]},{"finding":"CV3-026","module":"M27","page":"L19-0-02","paragraphs":[["How a POTS subscriber loop works · Concept","Plain old telephone service (POTS) traditionally connects a telephone set through a two-conductor local loop named TIP and RING. At rest (on-hook) the line normally carries a DC supply potential from the exchange but little loop current; taking the receiver off-hook closes the loop through the telephone set and exchange detects loop current to provide dial tone/call setup. Ringing can superimpose a high AC alerting voltage. Speech may travel in both directions on the same two-wire pair using hybrid circuitry and network switching. Exact voltages, polarity and signaling vary with equipment and region; telephone lines are not safe low-voltage hobby test points."],["How a POTS subscriber loop works · Worked reasoning","Paper wire-center case: a pair in a cable bundle is documented on a numbered terminal block and cross-connect jumper. Use the installation pair-color code and wiremap to trace TIP/RING end to end; do not choose a spare pair based on color alone. A qualified splice joins the correct conductor to its matched conductor with approved connector/environmental sealing and strain relief, maintaining pair integrity and identifying the work. Reversed/crossed pairs, split pairs, bridged taps and bad splices can have different symptoms; continuity alone is not full service certification."],["How a POTS subscriber loop works · Independent check","Check: state what changes between on-hook and off-hook (loop-current path) and why ringing is a hazard. For crossed wires at a distribution frame, name the documents/instruments a qualified technician would use to verify pairing and restore service without working on an unknown live loop."]]},{"finding":"CV3-027","module":"M28","page":"L28-0-04","paragraphs":[["How to obtain the right service literature · Concept","A service manual can be obtained through more than one legitimate channel: the equipment maker’s authorized support/document portal, a manufacturer technical-support representative, an authorized distributor or service-partner portal, a controlled employer document-management system, or licensed/official standards and maintenance subscriptions when required. Access permissions, licensing and regional release restrictions may apply. For each source, confirm the exact model/serial or board revision, document number, revision/date, safety updates and whether the instruction supersedes an older copy."],["How to obtain the right service literature · Worked reasoning","Paper case: the local PDF describes PCB revision A but the actual board is revision C. Search the OEM document catalogue by model/revision, request the current revision from manufacturer support if the portal is restricted, and compare controlled distributor/service-partner material only if authorized. Record document identity and source in the repair ticket; do not rely on an anonymous forum reupload as an authoritative repair procedure."],["How to obtain the right service literature · Independent check","Check: list three legitimate procurement channels and one limitation of each; explain why “same product name” is insufficient to accept a manual without revision verification."]]},{"finding":"CV3-028","module":"M28","page":"L28-0-04","paragraphs":[["Parts location and approved cross-reference · Concept","Part selection begins with the exact equipment identity and revision, the schematic reference designator (e.g. U17), the assembly drawing/BOM entry, and the manufacturer part number (MPN) and package. Next locate the approved spare/supersession list and inventory bin or approved supplier record. A substitute must meet electrical ratings, functional behavior, pinout, package/thermal constraints, firmware/qualification needs and service authorization; having the same package outline is insufficient. Record lot/traceability and source so a later technician can reproduce the decision."],["Parts location and approved cross-reference · Worked reasoning","Paper example: board revision C has U17 listed as “3.3 V LDO, MPN REG-A, SOT-223, pin order IN–GND–OUT.” A supplier suggests REG-B in the same package but with IN–OUT–GND. Step 1 locate U17 on the assembly drawing; 2 match BOM revision; 3 read both datasheets; 4 reject REG-B because pinout is incompatible absent an engineered redesign; 5 seek an OEM-approved equivalent or supersession and confirm it is in authorized inventory before repair."],["Parts location and approved cross-reference · Independent check","Check: trace “U17” through drawing → BOM → MPN → OEM approved replacement → supplier/bin → verified receipt and final repair record. Which two checks might reject a component that is physically the same size? Pinout and electrical/thermal ratings (among others)."]]},{"finding":"CV3-029","module":"M28","page":"L28-0-07","paragraphs":[["Individual and department productivity by period · Concept","Define productivity before dividing. For one technician, a common throughput measure is accepted jobs / productive labor-hours during a stated time window; first-pass yield is accepted on first attempt / attempted jobs, a different metric. For a department, total accepted jobs divided by total qualifying department labor-hours yields aggregated throughput. Never average individual rates without weighting for different hours, and compare complexity/case mix rather than ranking people on a single ratio."],["Individual and department productivity by period · Worked reasoning","Weekly example: technician A completes 12 accepted jobs in 24 labor-hours (0.50 job/hour); technician B completes 8 jobs in 32 hours (0.25 job/hour). Department throughput is (12+8)/(24+32)=20/56≈0.357 job/hour, **not** the unweighted mean (0.50+0.25)/2=0.375. If first-pass yields are A=9/12=75% and B=6/8=75%, department first-pass yield is (9+6)/(12+8)=15/20=75%—using counts, not time."],["Individual and department productivity by period · Independent check","Check: on the next day A completes 3 jobs in 6 hours and B completes 2 jobs in 4 hours. What is combined daily throughput? (3+2)/(6+4)=0.5 job/hour. Explain why that daily metric should not be compared directly with a monthly average without controlling for case mix and reporting definitions."]]}];
  const ITEM_PATCHES = [{"id":"ALF52-A-001","expectedPrompt":"A shop is evaluating RF work near an antenna. Which information is needed before deciding whether exposure is within the applicable maximum permissible exposure limit?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Measured or calculated field exposure, frequency, applicable time averaging and limit","Only the color of the antenna radome","The audio volume of the receiver","Use transmitter nameplate power and the antenna's physical height alone to establish safe RF exposure without evaluating frequency or averaging time."],"newChoices":["Measured or calculated field exposure, frequency, applicable time averaging and limit","The transmitter rated output power and antenna gain, without comparing the actual measured value to a frequency-specific limit.","The measured value and distance from antenna, but not frequency, exposure units or the permitted averaging interval.","A comparison to a limit for another frequency band, assuming that the same time-averaging interval always applies."],"reason":"Require recognizing all constraints of a legitimate MPE comparison; avoid unrelated color/audio alternatives.","newPrompt":"A technician has a measured RF exposure value from a transmitter site. What other information is required to judge whether the measured exposure complies with the applicable MPE requirement?"},{"id":"ALF52-A-003","expectedPrompt":"A technician documents the sequence power-up, self-test, then fault branch. What makes a decision diamond useful in the flowchart?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["It states the physical size of a test point","It provides the replacement resistor part number","It directs two or more paths based on an explicit condition","It guarantees a fault cannot recur"],"newChoices":["Place the diamond before the voltage measurement so it can select a branch without a measured condition.","Place the diamond on the 5 V supply symbol and branch based on component location, not test result.","It directs two or more paths based on an explicit condition","Use a process rectangle instead of a conditional decision, then direct both branches to the same recovery step."],"reason":"All alternatives concern plausible chart design errors and decision placement.","newPrompt":"A flowchart models a self-test. The program must run a recovery branch only when the measured 5 V rail is outside its specified tolerance. Which use of a decision diamond is correct?"},{"id":"ALF52-A-005","expectedPrompt":"A relay coil is energized by DC and attracts an iron armature. What produces the force?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Magnetic field associated with current in the coil","An optical signal that heats the contacts","Charge trapped permanently on the relay housing","Mechanical pressure produced by the supply label"],"newChoices":["Magnetic field associated with current in the coil","The coil voltage creates an electrostatic field at the open contacts that directly pulls the iron armature in.","The armature is pulled primarily by induced eddy currents that persist after the DC field reaches steady state.","The spring becomes magnetized by the supply and attracts the core independently of the coil current."],"reason":"Challenge confusion between static electrostatics, transients and electromagnetism.","newPrompt":"A relay coil is driven by steady DC and an iron armature moves toward its pole pieces. Which physical interaction produces the pull-in force?"},{"id":"ALF52-A-006","expectedPrompt":"An industrial controlled rectifier needs a three-terminal PNPN semiconductor that can latch on after a gate pulse. Which device fits?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["Ordinary two-terminal silicon signal diode","Metal-film resistor","Crystal resonator","Silicon-controlled rectifier (SCR)"],"newChoices":["TRIAC, a bidirectional thyristor intended to conduct in either AC polarity under its rated triggering conditions.","DIAC, a two-terminal bidirectional breakover trigger with no separate control gate.","Enhancement MOSFET, whose conductive channel depends on appropriate sustained gate-to-source bias.","Silicon-controlled rectifier (SCR)"],"reason":"Test SCR versus TRIAC, DIAC, and MOSFET operating mechanisms without word-matching the key.","newPrompt":"A DC switching circuit requires a unidirectional PNPN device with an anode, cathode and gate that can remain on after a brief trigger until its anode current falls below holding current. Select the device."},{"id":"ALF52-A-010","expectedPrompt":"A microcontroller GPIO input is limited to 3.3 V and a sensor output can reach 5 V. What must be evaluated before connecting them?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["The length of the application filename","Matching the two boards' ground potentials is sufficient to connect a 5 V push-pull output directly to an input whose absolute maximum is 3.3 V.","The number of LEDs on the power supply","Input absolute maximum rating and a suitable level-shifting or conditioning method"],"newChoices":["Confirm 5 V counts as a digital HIGH, and rely on the controller internal pull-up to keep the pin within its absolute maximum rating.","Share ground and choose a stronger 5 V output driver; a common reference by itself prevents overvoltage at the input.","Install a series resistor of arbitrary value without checking clamping current or input ratings; then connect the full 5 V swing.","Input absolute maximum rating and a suitable level-shifting or conditioning method"],"reason":"Replace unrelated distractors with believable but unsafe interface assumptions.","newPrompt":"A 5 V push-pull sensor must drive a microcontroller GPIO rated for a maximum of 3.3 V. Before wiring the interface, what is the principal design check?"},{"id":"ALF52-A-011","expectedPrompt":"A verified vendor service update is downloaded. What is the best practice before installing it on controlled equipment?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["Run any similarly named binary found online","Disable all backups permanently","Confirm model, source authenticity, revision, checksum or signature and approved backup/recovery steps","Verify the download matches the product brand but install the newest revision on all models without checking firmware signatures or rollback support."],"newChoices":["Select a package from an authorized source by brand family only and assume all hardware revisions use the newest binary.","Use a locally cached file from last year if it has a plausible filename, without verifying whether it has been superseded.","Confirm model, source authenticity, revision, checksum or signature and approved backup/recovery steps","Select a manufacturer-hosted package for the correct model but skip the published release notes and recovery plan before installation."],"reason":"Directly assess procurement and download compatibility, correcting prior incomplete ETA 16.9 match.","newPrompt":"A technician must obtain and install an approved service software update for a controlled device. Which download-and-preparation procedure is defensible?"},{"id":"ALF52-A-013","expectedPrompt":"A camera's sensor receives more photons during a longer exposure, with other settings fixed. Which change is expected?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Larger signal charge until the pixel saturates","A guaranteed decrease in signal independent of scene","An increase in lens focal length","Conversion of the sensor into a microphone"],"newChoices":["Larger signal charge until the pixel saturates","Signal charge decreases to about half because photons are averaged over twice the collection interval.","Signal charge remains approximately constant because changing integration time affects only the digital frame rate.","Collected charge is unchanged, while sensor sensitivity (photons per electron) doubles exactly with exposure time."],"reason":"Contrast accumulated charge, sensitivity, digital frame rate and integration.","newPrompt":"A camera uses a charge-integrating image sensor. With scene illumination and gain fixed, the exposure time increases from 1 ms to 2 ms without reaching pixel saturation. What is the expected signal trend?"},{"id":"ALF52-A-014","expectedPrompt":"Two mobile radios must exchange traffic without fixed infrastructure. Which feature is most directly relevant?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["Whether their cases have identical colors","Two mobile radios only require a common audio volume setting to communicate, since their programmed channels and modulation do not affect interoperability.","Whether both have touchscreens","Compatible frequencies, modulation and duplex/simplex channel configuration"],"newChoices":["Maritime VHF service organized primarily for ship-to-ship and ship-to-shore operations.","Aeronautical VHF voice service organized primarily for pilot and air-traffic communications.","One-way television broadcast reception using a passive receiver without two-way dispatch capability.","Land-mobile radio (LMR) using suitable authorized direct or repeater-assisted two-way channels."],"reason":"Address the official 20.1 service-types objective, not just generic channel-configuration compatibility.","newPrompt":"A city public-works department needs portable-to-vehicle and portable-to-dispatch voice communications, sometimes through a repeater. Which two-way radio service is most directly aligned with that use?"},{"id":"ALF52-A-019","expectedPrompt":"A PLL locks a voltage-controlled oscillator to a reference. What is the role of the phase detector?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["It stores the audio output mechanically","It rectifies mains to DC in every PLL","It produces an error related to phase or frequency difference between reference and feedback","The phase detector directly measures antenna VSWR and adjusts oscillator output amplitude until the transmitted and received RF power match."],"newChoices":["An output proportional solely to the VCO supply current, regardless of the reference and feedback timing.","A filtered DC control voltage supplied directly by the phase detector without any role for the loop filter or charge-pump topology.","It produces an error related to phase or frequency difference between reference and feedback","A measurement of the RF antenna mismatch that retunes output power but does not compare VCO feedback to the reference."],"reason":"Introduce plausible confusion among phase detector, charge pump, filter and VCO control.","newPrompt":"A PLL locks its VCO to a reference. Which signal is generated by the phase detector/phase-frequency detector for the loop to correct a tracking error?"},{"id":"ALF52-A-020","expectedPrompt":"A logic-level MOSFET drives a motor. What requirement is still essential even if the gate threshold appears low?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["Treat the MOSFET threshold voltage as the recommended full-load gate drive, then size the motor wiring using only its no-load current rating.","Check on-resistance at the actual gate drive plus drain voltage, current, heat and inductive suppression","Ignore motor stall current","Connect the motor to the gate pin as a load"],"newChoices":["Treat the MOSFET threshold voltage as the recommended full-load gate drive, then size the motor wiring using only its no-load current rating.","Check on-resistance at the actual gate drive plus drain voltage, current, heat and inductive suppression","Use the typical on-resistance quoted at VGS=10 V as the exact on-resistance at a 3.3 V gate drive, with no separate check.","Rate the MOSFET for nominal motor current only, assuming the switching transition and stalled motor conditions cannot raise dissipation."],"reason":"Repair weak distractors and link power-loss, thermal and voltage checks to explicit MOSFET dissipation instruction.","sourcePageId":"L08-1-00","sourceModuleId":"M12"},{"id":"ALF52-A-021","expectedPrompt":"Why might a broadband amplifier need different design compromises than a narrowband amplifier?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["It must control gain, matching and stability across a wider frequency range","It always has infinite gain at DC","It never requires thermal management","A broadband amplifier is optimized around one narrow center frequency, so its gain and stability at frequencies outside that spot are irrelevant."],"newChoices":["It must control gain, matching and stability across a wider frequency range","Choosing one very high-Q tuned input network to maximize narrow frequency selectivity while accepting severe gain loss elsewhere.","Specifying the DC bias point alone as sufficient evidence of stable gain and matching over the entire 100 kHz–10 MHz band.","Maximizing gain at 1 MHz without checking reactive feedback or stability toward either edge of the required passband."],"reason":"Make alternatives relevant to gain-bandwidth, impedance and stability tradeoffs.","newPrompt":"An amplifier is specified for flat usable gain over 100 kHz–10 MHz rather than around one tuned center frequency. Which design concern becomes especially important?"},{"id":"ALF52-A-024","expectedPrompt":"Why is a compiled embedded-language program usually linked before loading?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["Linking changes the PCB trace width","Object files and libraries must have references resolved into an executable image","The linker translates the processor's ADC peripheral into a DAC instruction and sets oscillator speed, while compiler objects are already executable.","A linker measures clock frequency on a scope"],"newChoices":["Convert each object file back into C so the MCU can interpret it when it starts.","Object files and libraries must have references resolved into an executable image","Resolve external references in the compiler only; the linker merely checks source-code formatting.","Join object modules inside the flash programmer after it writes them to unrelated memory addresses."],"reason":"Distinguish compiler, linker and flash-loader jobs rather than eliminating unrelated PCB/test-equipment distractors; linking remains a narrow 15.7 sample.","newPrompt":"An embedded C compiler has produced object files for separate source modules. Before the firmware can be loaded onto its target MCU, what is a typical function of the linker?"},{"id":"ALF52-A-025","expectedPrompt":"A flexible equipment cord experiences repeated bending. Which construction is most appropriate?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Suitably rated stranded conductors with appropriate insulation and strain relief","Bare solid conductors without support","Use one solid coax center conductor as the full flexible supply lead, because its stiffness prevents fatigue during frequent bending.","Any cable with the same jacket color"],"newChoices":["Suitably rated stranded conductors with appropriate insulation and strain relief","A solid-core conductor of adequate ampacity under a flexible jacket, relying on the jacket alone to prevent repeated copper flexing.","Stranded wire with rated insulation but a rigid, unsupported solder joint at the point of continuous bending.","A high-flex stranded cable whose current capacity and temperature rating fall below the equipment requirements."],"reason":"Replace implausible bare-wire/jacket-color distractors with realistic tradeoffs involving flex rating, termination fatigue and ampacity.","newPrompt":"An equipment cord must carry its rated current while undergoing repeated motion near a strain-relieved connector. Which construction best addresses electrical ratings and flex fatigue?"},{"id":"ALF52-A-026","expectedPrompt":"A powered speaker produces audible output from digital media. Which major chain is plausible?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["Antenna only, no electrical source","A microphone and passive resistor only","Connect the digital storage device directly to a passive speaker cone; the speaker will automatically decode and amplify the stored audio stream.","Source and decoder, DAC or digital amplifier, power stage and loudspeaker"],"newChoices":["Digital file → analog-only power amplifier → loudspeaker, with no digital decoder or compatible conversion stage.","Digital file → decoder → line-level DAC → passive loudspeaker, without a power stage capable of driving the load.","Digital file → decoder → microphone preamplifier → loudspeaker, using the microphone preamp as the output power stage.","Source and decoder, DAC or digital amplifier, power stage and loudspeaker"],"reason":"Assess signal-chain functional roles using plausible but missing/inappropriate decoder, DAC and output power configurations.","newPrompt":"A powered loudspeaker plays a compressed digital audio file. Which chain provides both decoding and adequate electrical drive to the speaker?"},{"id":"ALF52-A-027","expectedPrompt":"A receiver is tuned off the desired station. Which change most directly centers the selected channel?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["Increase the RF front-end gain to make the off-channel signal stronger without correcting frequency selection","Reduce the IF filter bandwidth while leaving the receiver’s tuning point displaced from the desired channel","Adjusting its specified tuning/local-oscillator and filter alignment using appropriate test procedures","Change the antenna matching network to increase signal transfer without addressing the receiver’s tuned frequency"],"newChoices":["Raise RF gain until the desired station sounds louder, without verifying the oscillator or filter center frequency.","Choose a narrower IF filter without correcting its offset from the intended passband center.","Adjusting its specified tuning/local-oscillator and filter alignment using appropriate test procedures","Change antenna impedance matching to improve received power while leaving measured oscillator/IF errors unchanged."],"reason":"Separate ordinary station selection from calibrated service realignment and link to the existing explicit alignment caution.","newPrompt":"A receiver is set to the correct frequency, but calibrated test signals reveal that its local-oscillator/IF response remains off-center. Under the manufacturer’s approved procedure, what adjustment addresses that confirmed alignment fault?","sourceModuleId":"M26","sourcePageId":"L20-0-11"},{"id":"ALF52-A-032","expectedPrompt":"For a solenoid with constant current, adding turns generally changes what?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["Electron charge from negative to positive","Ampere-turns and magnetic field strength, within magnetic and thermal limits","The speed of light in free space","Increasing coil turns automatically changes the battery chemistry and fixes the magnetic field irrespective of coil resistance or supply current."],"newChoices":["At fixed current, the magnetizing field falls to half because doubling turn count halves ampere-turns.","Ampere-turns and magnetic field strength, within magnetic and thermal limits","The field stays unchanged because increased inductance cancels magnetizing force under constant DC current.","Magnetizing force depends only on wire diameter, so adding turns affects resistance but never the magnetic field."],"reason":"Contrast coil inductance, resistance, turn count and magnetizing force at explicitly held constant current; add NI worked instruction at M07.","newPrompt":"Two ideal air-core solenoids have the same length, cross-section and current, but coil B has twice as many turns as coil A. Before core saturation or winding-heat constraints, what qualitative field relationship follows?"},{"id":"ALF52-A-034","expectedPrompt":"An SCR is latched conducting in a DC circuit. Which statement is generally correct?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["Turn off the SCR gate drive and assume the anode current ceases immediately, even when the DC load current remains above the holding level.","It is identical to a fixed resistor","Its anode and cathode can be ignored","Removing gate drive alone may not stop conduction until current falls below holding current"],"newChoices":["Unlike the MOSFET, a latched SCR normally turns off as soon as its gate drive is removed, even above holding current.","Both devices require reverse gate voltage before either can stop conducting DC load current.","The SCR turns off automatically once its trigger pulse ends, while the MOSFET must be commutated below holding current.","Removing gate drive alone may not stop conduction until current falls below holding current"],"reason":"Align the test with 3.6.2 comparison verb and make all distractors engage SCR versus MOSFET gate/holding-current behavior.","newPrompt":"A technician compares a conventional MOSFET DC switch with an SCR that has already latched on in a DC load circuit. Which statement describes an important SCR turn-off difference?"},{"id":"ALF52-A-035","expectedPrompt":"A switch-mode supply has a switching transistor, magnetic element and output feedback. Which explains regulation?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["Regulation requires an always-on series resistor only","Output voltage is set by front panel color","Duty cycle or switching behavior changes under feedback to control output energy","Set every switching regulator's oscillator to line frequency and regulate output solely by changing a resistor, without changing pulse width or feedback."],"newChoices":["Reduce switch on-time because output droop is always caused by excessive transferred energy.","Hold duty cycle fixed and change only the passive output filter capacitor during normal feedback regulation.","Duty cycle or switching behavior changes under feedback to control output energy","Ignore the output feedback signal and adjust only the input rectifier frequency to restore output voltage."],"reason":"Test negative-feedback corrective action with competing control-loop errors and point remediation to the actual feedback lesson.","newPrompt":"A buck SMPS supplies a load that suddenly increases. Its feedback senses output droop against the voltage reference. What adjustment can its controller make to restore regulation?","sourceModuleId":"M13","sourcePageId":"L09-1-02"},{"expectedPrompt":"Solder ejects tiny droplets during rework. Which precaution directly reduces injury and contamination risk?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["Blow across molten solder toward nearby people","Use eye protection, controlled tip temperature, and suitable technique and work positioning","Raise the tip temperature as high as possible to shorten contact time, keeping the board close to the face so the solder joint remains visible.","Shake the iron above populated circuitry"],"reason":"The answer and wording are sound; change only remediation destination from generic ESD to the existing lesson specifically explaining solder splatter causes and precautions (5.3).","sourceModuleId":"M15","sourcePageId":"L29-0-02","id":"ALF52-A-036","newChoices":["Blow across molten solder toward nearby people","Use eye protection, controlled tip temperature, and suitable technique and work positioning","Raise the tip temperature as high as possible to shorten contact time, keeping the board close to the face so the solder joint remains visible.","Shake the iron above populated circuitry"]},{"expectedPrompt":"A microcontroller needs to repeat an LED blink ten times. Which programming structure is most direct?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["A variable declaration alone repeats the output toggle ten times after compilation, without a loop, timer state or executable control flow.","A hardware fuse replacement for each blink","A compiler warning suppressed without code","A bounded for-loop with an output toggle and timed delay or timer"],"newPrompt":"The function blink_once() performs one complete LED on-off blink. Which C-style counted loop calls it exactly ten times?","newChoices":["for (int i = 0; i <= 10; i++) { blink_once(); }","for (int i = 1; i < 10; i++) { blink_once(); }","for (int i = 0; i < 10; i += 2) { blink_once(); }","for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) { blink_once(); }"],"reason":"Four same-domain loops test 11/9/5/10 iterations and counter boundaries rather than unrelated fuses or compiler warnings; linked counted-loop lesson receives an off-by-one worked trace.","sourceModuleId":"M20","sourcePageId":"L14-0-03","id":"ALF52-A-038"},{"expectedPrompt":"Which cable test best detects a swapped pair at an RJ45 jack?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["A thermal camera alone","An analog voltmeter measuring the jacket","Correctly configured wire-map or pair-continuity tester","An antenna SWR meter only"],"newPrompt":"An Ethernet twisted-pair cable conducts end-to-end, but the two connectors might have different pair assignments. Which test most directly identifies a swapped pair at the RJ45 termination?","newChoices":["An end-to-end continuity beep that reports only whether a circuit closes, without conductor-to-pin mapping.","A cable-length TDR reading used only to locate opens, with no pin-by-pin wiring results.","A wire-map test that reports the actual conductor/pin assignment at each end.","An insertion-loss measurement at the rated frequency without wire-map or pair-identification output."],"reason":"Replace cross-domain distractors with realistic cable tests and distinguish wire mapping from mere continuity, TDR and insertion-loss evaluation; tests a bounded copper subset of 7.10.","sourceModuleId":"M22","sourcePageId":"L19-0-06","id":"ALF52-A-039"},{"expectedPrompt":"A radio link gets weak behind a hill even though the transmitter still runs. Which effect is a plausible cause?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Obstruction, diffraction limits and path loss affecting propagation","Ohm's law stopping at the hill","Audio gain turning into antenna gain automatically","The earth absorbing all frequency-independent signals identically"],"newPrompt":"A fixed-frequency radio link has stable transmitter output power and unchanged antenna and feedline settings. Reception becomes weak when the receiver is moved behind a ridge. What is the strongest first explanation?","newChoices":["Obstruction, diffraction limits and path loss affecting propagation","The ridge must have changed the transmitter output-stage impedance, despite the unchanged transmitter and feedline measurements.","The receiver local oscillator necessarily shifts in frequency as soon as the ridge blocks the path, even with a locked frequency reference.","Automatic gain control must be lowering receiver gain because the received RF level fell, regardless of the AGC feedback direction."],"reason":"Plausible but incorrect equipment/control explanations force separating propagation loss from oscillator, matching and AGC faults.","sourceModuleId":"M26","sourcePageId":"L20-0-03","id":"ALF52-A-041"},{"expectedPrompt":"A service manual is unavailable locally. What is the soundest way to procure one?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["Use a random unrelated model's pinout","Use service documentation for the nearest product family without confirming the hardware revision because connectors from one brand share pinouts.","Bypass safety warnings on a forum diagram","Search the manufacturer's authorized support/service portal for the exact model and revision"],"newPrompt":"A local binder has only a previous board-revision manual; the manufacturer portal currently lists a service manual for the exact hardware revision. Before servicing the unit, which procurement decision is best?","newChoices":["Use the controlled internal archive manual for the earlier board revision without checking whether the changed connectors affect service steps.","Request a manual from an authorized distributor using only the product-family name and proceed before matching the actual board revision.","Use a licensed copy of an older service procedure without checking whether it has been superseded by a safety or revision notice.","Search the manufacturer's authorized support/service portal for the exact model and revision"],"reason":"Use a specific scenario to compare legitimate acquisition channels against their model/revision applicability; CV3-027 already teaches authorized portal, technical support, distributor and controlled archive alternatives.","sourceModuleId":"M28","sourcePageId":"L28-0-04","id":"ALF52-A-042"},{"expectedPrompt":"A 100-ohm resistor has 1.5 A through it. What voltage appears across it ideally?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["66.7 V","1.5 V","150 V","0.015 V"],"reason":"The V=IR arithmetic and numerical distractors are valid; correct primary objective from conceptual 2.6 to calculating with Ohm law (2.13.1) and link directly to worked Ohm-law lesson in M03. Unchanged key and text.","expectedObjectiveIds":["2.6"],"expectedFocusObjectiveId":"2.6","newObjectiveIds":["2.13.1"],"newFocusObjectiveId":"2.13.1","sourceModuleId":"M03","sourcePageId":"L01-0-01","id":"ALF52-A-043","newChoices":["66.7 V","1.5 V","150 V","0.015 V"]},{"expectedPrompt":"On a DMM, which control chooses whether the input is measuring voltage or current?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Function/range selector, paired with the proper jack configuration","Choose the meter's hold button to switch between voltage and current measurement; moving the probes between jacks is unnecessary.","The hold button as a substitute for function","The serial number label"],"newPrompt":"A DMM has separate voltage and fused current inputs. To change safely from measuring voltage to measuring current, which setup choice is required?","newChoices":["Function/range selector, paired with the proper jack configuration","Move the red probe to a current jack while leaving the function selector in voltage mode; the jack alone selects the mode.","Turn the selector to current but leave the red probe in the voltage jack; the selector alone reroutes the meter input.","Use the HOLD button to change the measurement function while retaining the voltage range and probe configuration."],"expectedObjectiveIds":["8.12.1"],"expectedFocusObjectiveId":"8.12.1","newObjectiveIds":["8.3"],"newFocusObjectiveId":"8.3","reason":"Correct inappropriate oscilloscope-control objective (8.12.1) to DMM protection, safety and usage (8.3), with three realistic mode/jack/hold confusions; link to M05 meter-safety fundamentals.","sourceModuleId":"M05","sourcePageId":"L03-0-00","id":"ALF52-A-045"},{"expectedPrompt":"An accelerometer uses piezoelectric material under compression. What is converted?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["Light to radio waves directly","An AC waveform to a permanent magnet","Mechanical stress to electrical charge or voltage","Heat only to digital memory addresses"],"newPrompt":"A piezoelectric vibration sensor is mechanically compressed and a charge amplifier reports an electrical signal. Which transduction process explains the direct effect?","newChoices":["Applied strain alters the resistance of a biased foil element; the electrical change is due solely to the external bias source.","Motion of a permanent magnet through a fixed coil induces a current by changing linked magnetic flux.","Mechanical stress to electrical charge or voltage","An applied electric field causes a crystal to change shape; this is the converse effect rather than the observed sensing direction."],"reason":"Contrast direct piezoelectric sensing against strain-gauge, inductive and converse-piezo mechanisms and add stress-charge worked instruction to assigned crystal lesson.","sourceModuleId":"M10","sourcePageId":"L11-0-06","id":"ALF52-A-047"},{"expectedPrompt":"A transistor's beta is 100 at one operating point. What is a valid interpretation?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["Its current gain is guaranteed exactly 100 over all temperature and load","Collector current to base current ratio at that operating point is about 100","Emitter current is always zero","The device is an enhancement MOSFET"],"newPrompt":"In forward-active operation, a BJT has measured base current 20 μA and collector current 2.0 mA. Which interpretation of its DC current gain β is correct?","newChoices":["β ≈ 0.010 because it is the base-to-collector current ratio IB/IC.","Collector current to base current ratio at that operating point is about 100","β ≈ 1.01 because it is the emitter-to-collector current ratio IE/IC.","β ≈ 0.990 because it is the collector-to-emitter fraction IC/IE."],"reason":"Use measured currents to distinguish β=IC/IB from inverse ratio and α=IC/IE; add a worked DC beta/alpha example to assigned Q-point lesson.","sourceModuleId":"M12","sourcePageId":"L08-0-05","id":"ALF52-A-048"},{"id":"ALF52-A-051","expectedPrompt":"An I2C device pulls SDA low even when the bus is idle. What symptom follows?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["The bus becomes a differential 100-ohm Ethernet pair","Treat an I2C SDA line that stays low as proof of a doubled clock rate, because the pull-up resistor automatically fixes a stuck device.","The shared line may remain stuck low and prevent normal bus transactions","Every device is necessarily healthy"],"newChoices":["The SDA pull-up restores a logic HIGH even while another connected device actively sinks current, so normal bus-idle is preserved.","With SCL still operating, addresses and data can be transferred normally because the shared SDA wire is used for acknowledgments only.","The shared line may remain stuck low and prevent normal bus transactions","A sustained SDA LOW on the idle bus signifies successful completion of a STOP condition, so normal bus transfers can resume."],"reason":"Require distinguishing open-drain held-LOW bus fault from incorrect pull-up, SDA-only-ACK and STOP assumptions; direct link to electrical pull-up teaching.","newPrompt":"An I²C analyzer shows SDA continuously LOW after the controller releases the bus; SCL and the pull-up supply remain normal. What immediate bus-level symptom should the technician expect?","sourceModuleId":"M19","sourcePageId":"L18-0-06"},{"id":"ALF52-A-052","expectedPrompt":"A freshly imaged computer must join a secure local network. Which practice is appropriate?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["Join the network immediately with the installed default administrator credentials, since an approved image makes later security review unnecessary.","Confirm approved OS/image, drivers, patch level, device identity and least-privilege network settings","Install drivers from anonymous sites only","Use the default administrator password indefinitely"],"newChoices":["Verify the OS build and patch level, but connect with the image’s shared default administrator account until first network sign-in succeeds.","Confirm approved OS/image, drivers, patch level, device identity and least-privilege network settings","Confirm the manufacturer’s driver package, then omit endpoint identity and authentication checks because its Ethernet connection is physical.","Replace default credentials and record the OS image, but allow unrestricted network access until application acceptance tests finish."],"reason":"Make distractors plausible incomplete provisioning sequences with credential/identity/least-privilege defects, not unrelated obviously unsafe acts.","newPrompt":"Before attaching a freshly imaged technician workstation to a managed network, which preparation best protects the network while preserving a reproducible installation?","sourceModuleId":"M21","sourcePageId":"L27-0-03"},{"id":"ALF52-A-054","expectedPrompt":"Which device primarily converts visible light into a current or voltage signal?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["Ordinary fixed resistor","A crystal oscillator","Magnetic reed relay","Photodiode"],"newChoices":["A light-dependent resistor whose resistance changes with illumination and requires a separate bias/readout circuit","A phototransistor whose collector current is enhanced by transistor gain when illuminated","A photovoltaic power cell operated to supply useful energy to a load rather than as a fast reverse-biased receiver","Photodiode"],"reason":"Contrast fast reverse-biased photodiode photocurrent with photoresistive sensing, phototransistor gain and photovoltaic power extraction; corrected lesson is mechanism-specific.","newPrompt":"A fast optical receiver uses a reverse-biased light-sensitive semiconductor junction feeding a transimpedance amplifier. Which component directly generates photocurrent without transistor gain?","sourceModuleId":"M25","sourcePageId":"L19-0-10"},{"id":"ALF52-A-055","expectedPrompt":"A LAN switch carries digital traffic to several devices. What wired-network feature matters?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["Speaker output impedance only","Antenna polarization only","Physical and link-layer standards, negotiated speeds and correct cabling","Select a LAN patch cable by its DC conductor resistance only; negotiated link speed and transmission characteristics do not influence interoperability."],"newChoices":["Check host DNS servers and IP subnet masks first; Ethernet physical link cannot come up until Layer 3 host addresses agree.","Change the switch VLAN and host firewall policy first; these control whether a copper PHY can detect cable and negotiate link.","Physical and link-layer standards, negotiated speeds and correct cabling","Check conductor DC continuity alone and accept the cable if no wires are open; pair geometry, terminations and link negotiation cannot affect link-up."],"reason":"Test PHY vs network-layer distinction, medium and standards rather than generic sound/radio distractors. Add directly aligned Ethernet negotiation teaching.","newPrompt":"Two switched Ethernet ports do not establish a physical link after cabling changes; the link indicators stay dark. Which checks most directly address the suspected link problem?","sourceModuleId":"M27","sourcePageId":"L19-0-03"},{"id":"ALF52-A-057","expectedPrompt":"A resistor fitted in series with an LED limits what?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Forward current at a known voltage difference","The LED's wavelength independently of current","Battery polarity regardless of wiring","Only the circuit's capacitance"],"newChoices":["About 10 mA of LED forward current, using the remaining 10 V across the 1.0 kΩ resistor","About 12 mA, using all 12 V across the resistor without subtracting the LED forward drop","About 2 mA, treating only the LED’s 2 V forward drop as the resistor voltage","About 100 mA, interpreting the specified 1.0 kΩ resistor as a 100 Ω resistor"],"reason":"Convert answer by terminology into discriminating Ohm-law arithmetic; preserve correct answer at index zero and align to existing LED example.","newPrompt":"A 12 V DC supply feeds an LED with an approximately 2 V forward drop through a 1.0 kΩ resistor. Ignoring other drops, what current does the resistor set approximately?","sourceModuleId":"M03","sourcePageId":"L02-0-07"},{"id":"ALF52-A-058","expectedPrompt":"A technician needs to locate a component's physical position on a crowded PCB. Which document usually helps most?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["Use the product-level block diagram to find the exact pad of a small surface-mount component without checking any assembly drawing or layout.","An unrelated wiring legend","An accounting invoice","Board assembly drawing or PCB layout with reference designators"],"newChoices":["A system block diagram that shows functional stages and interconnections but omits component positions","A wiring diagram that documents harness and external terminal routing but not populated-board component locations","A circuit schematic that identifies the electrical nets attached to U17 but does not establish its physical board position","Board assembly drawing or PCB layout with reference designators"],"reason":"Compare three authentic engineering drawing roles; physical placement and reference-designator orientation require assembly/layout information.","newPrompt":"A repair ticket identifies IC U17, but the technician must find its physical location and pin-1 orientation on a populated PCB. Which drawing type is the best starting point?","sourceModuleId":"M05","sourcePageId":"L13-0-00"},{"id":"ALF52-A-059","expectedPrompt":"A DC resistor and capacitor are connected in series to a step input. What occurs immediately after applying voltage to an initially uncharged ideal capacitor?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["A permanent sinusoid without any oscillator","Infinite steady-state DC current after charging","A transient charging current that decreases as the capacitor voltage rises","An unchanging capacitor voltage from the first instant"],"newChoices":["Current begins at 0 mA and grows toward 5 mA as the capacitor voltage approaches 10 V.","Current begins at 5 mA and remains steady after charging because the capacitor becomes a DC short.","Current initially reaches 5 mA and decays exponentially with τ = RC = 0.20 s as capacitor voltage rises.","Current begins at 10 mA and doubles over one time constant because capacitor voltage rises."],"reason":"Correct AC objective mapping to RLC series operation and place direct DC RC transient/τ calculation teaching at revised linked M08 page.","newPrompt":"An ideal 10 V step is applied at t=0 to a series 2.0 kΩ resistor and initially uncharged 100 µF capacitor. Which current response is predicted?","sourceModuleId":"M08","sourcePageId":"L04-0-05","expectedObjectiveIds":["4.2"],"expectedFocusObjectiveId":"4.2","newObjectiveIds":["4.3"],"newFocusObjectiveId":"4.3"},{"id":"ALF52-A-060","expectedPrompt":"A crystal-controlled oscillator is selected over a simple RC oscillator. What is a common reason?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["A crystal generates an unlimited current source","Improved frequency reference stability and accuracy","All crystals oscillate without supporting circuitry","A crystal eliminates temperature drift completely"],"newChoices":["Its oscillation frequency is set by supply voltage alone and can always be corrected by adjusting the output load current.","Improved frequency reference stability and accuracy","Its nominal accuracy does not depend on resonator cut, load capacitance, temperature or oscillator circuit design.","A passive crystal oscillates continuously with no sustaining gain circuit, so no startup or oscillator-power conditions matter."],"reason":"Present realistic timing tolerance application and frequency-determination limitations, linking the specific crystal vs RC comparison lesson.","newPrompt":"An embedded clock must hold its nominal frequency within a tight tolerance over normal operating conditions. Why might an engineered crystal-resonator clock be chosen rather than a simple uncalibrated RC clock?","sourceModuleId":"M10","sourcePageId":"L11-0-06"},{"id":"ALF52-A-061","expectedPrompt":"An IC includes CPU core, memory and peripherals on one package. What broad category best describes it?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Microcontroller","Discrete relay","Standalone fixed resistor","Passive isolation transformer"],"newChoices":["Microcontroller","A general-purpose microprocessor system depending on external memory and peripheral controller ICs for these functions","An FPGA configured primarily as programmable logic fabric rather than a built-in CPU-plus-ADC controller","A custom fixed-function analog signal-conditioning ASIC with no programmable processor core or firmware memory"],"reason":"Distinguish MCU integration from MPU plus external memory, programmable logic and nonprogrammable analog ASIC; correct focus to explicit microcontroller objective.","newPrompt":"A single chip has a CPU core, on-chip flash and SRAM, timers, GPIO and an ADC. Firmware samples a sensor and controls a PWM output without an external memory controller. Which device category is the most direct fit?","sourceModuleId":"M20","sourcePageId":"L16-0-03","expectedObjectiveIds":["3.8"],"expectedFocusObjectiveId":"3.8","newObjectiveIds":["13.12"],"newFocusObjectiveId":"13.12"},{"id":"ALF52-A-064","expectedPrompt":"Two boards exchange serial data with no shared ground reference or galvanic isolation. What is the risk?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["Matching the UART baud rate is enough to make two separately grounded boards communicate safely, even without a common voltage reference or isolation.","Signal voltages may not be interpreted reliably and ground differences can damage inputs","Any USB cable guarantees isolation","The RX pin becomes a power supply"],"newChoices":["Correct baud, frame format and idle polarity make signal interpretation independent of the boards' reference voltages.","Signal voltages may not be interpreted reliably and ground differences can damage inputs","Each receiver can use its local protective-earth bond in place of an assessed signal return, even if the supply domains differ.","Using a connector that also supports USB makes the UART path galvanically isolated without an isolation component."],"reason":"Remap to ETA 12.3 and assigned lesson on reference offsets; alternatives challenge timing-versus-electrical compatibility, return paths, and isolation assumptions.","newPrompt":"Two low-voltage boards exchange single-ended 3.3 V UART data, but their reference potentials are not confirmed compatible. Why is matching the baud rate not enough?","sourceModuleId":"M19","sourcePageId":"L18-0-03","expectedObjectiveIds":["12.5"],"expectedFocusObjectiveId":"12.5","newObjectiveIds":["12.3"],"newFocusObjectiveId":"12.3"},{"id":"ALF52-A-065","expectedPrompt":"When upgrading RAM, which property must be compatible with the computer?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Supported RAM generation/form factor, speed and capacity limits","Keyboard keycap color","Match the computer's storage filesystem before changing RAM, because file allocation format controls the physical memory generation and slot geometry.","Desktop wallpaper resolution"],"newChoices":["Supported RAM generation/form factor, speed and capacity limits","Choose DDR5 SO-DIMMs if the advertised speed is higher, since SO-DIMM size makes DDR generations mechanically and electrically interchangeable.","Match the DDR4 form factor and total capacity, but ignore the memory controller and firmware support for module organization and speed.","Install any DDR4 desktop DIMM with a matching capacity; the operating system can adapt a full-size DIMM to a laptop socket."],"reason":"Require actual generation/socket/controller/capacity selection versus superficially plausible memory-upgrade shortcuts.","newPrompt":"A laptop manufacturer lists DDR4 SO-DIMM support, a maximum total capacity and a validated speed range. Which checks should determine a proposed memory upgrade?","sourceModuleId":"M21","sourcePageId":"L14-0-00"},{"id":"ALF52-A-067","expectedPrompt":"A common-anode seven-segment LED display needs one segment lit. What generally drives that segment?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["An infinite current source on the cathode","An open circuit on every pin","A properly current-limited lower potential at the corresponding cathode","Forward-bias a common-anode LED segment by raising its cathode above the positive common terminal, relying on the package to limit current."],"newChoices":["Bring the selected cathode to the same voltage as the common anode; equal terminal potentials forward-bias the LED.","Drive the selected cathode above the common-anode supply; reversing the polarity increases forward segment current.","A properly current-limited lower potential at the corresponding cathode","Drive the common-anode pin LOW and the selected cathode HIGH; this is the same polarity convention as a common-cathode display."],"reason":"Remove physically impossible options, explicitly test polarity and preserve current-limit requirement.","newPrompt":"A seven-segment LED display has a common ANODE tied to a suitable positive supply. Which drive arrangement lights one selected segment while protecting it from excess current?","sourceModuleId":"M25","sourcePageId":"L19-0-09"},{"id":"ALF52-A-069","expectedPrompt":"A plot of diode current against voltage bends upward rapidly in forward bias. What does its nonlinear shape demonstrate?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Current is not proportional to voltage over the entire curve","The diode is an ideal fixed resistor everywhere","Forward voltage is independent of current exactly","The axes must always be reversed"],"newChoices":["Current is not proportional to voltage over the entire curve","The ratio V/I remains at 1.1 kΩ at both points, indicating a fixed ohmic resistance.","The added 0.10 V produces only another 0.5 mA because the diode behaves as a constant 200 Ω resistor.","The tenfold current increase identifies reverse breakdown, despite both marked points having positive forward voltage."],"reason":"Use two specified points to make graph interpretation and nonconstant V/I reasoned rather than guessable from absolutes; existing lesson gives nonlinear junction model.","newPrompt":"On a forward-biased diode I–V graph, current is 0.5 mA at 0.55 V and 5.0 mA at 0.65 V. What does the sharp current rise with only 0.10 V more forward bias show?","sourceModuleId":"M11","sourcePageId":"L08-0-03"},{"id":"ALF52-A-070","expectedPrompt":"A long copper run has smaller conductor diameter than specified. Which effect is likely at equal length and material?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["Reduce the wire diameter to lower its resistance, because electrons have less material to traverse and the voltage drop falls at a given current.","No change in resistance","The conductor becomes a capacitor only","Higher DC resistance and greater voltage drop under the same load current"],"newChoices":["Half the DC resistance and voltage drop, because a narrower conductor contains fewer charge carriers to impede motion.","The same DC resistance and voltage drop, since copper resistivity and length have not changed.","Twice the DC resistance but half the voltage drop, because less metal divides the current into a smaller path.","Higher DC resistance and greater voltage drop under the same load current"],"reason":"Remap from inductor-coil dimensions to conductor geometry and AWG objective; test R=ρL/A with equal length and fixed current.","newPrompt":"Two equal-length copper conductors at the same temperature carry the same load current. Conductor B has HALF the cross-sectional metal area of conductor A. Which change is expected in B?","sourceModuleId":"M22","sourcePageId":"L19-0-03","expectedObjectiveIds":["3.3.1"],"expectedFocusObjectiveId":"3.3.1","newObjectiveIds":["7.4"],"newFocusObjectiveId":"7.4"},{"id":"ALF52-A-071","expectedPrompt":"A transformer is required to provide two electrically separated secondary voltages. Which specification is relevant?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["Resistor tolerance printed on the case","An audio line-level gain setting","Appropriate isolated multi-secondary winding arrangement","The color of winding tape only"],"newChoices":["A center-tapped single secondary whose two half-windings share their center node, making the two outputs independently galvanically isolated.","A tapped autotransformer winding that shares a conductor with the primary while offering multiple voltages.","Appropriate isolated multi-secondary winding arrangement","One ordinary secondary followed by two nonisolated voltage regulators with a common 0 V return, because voltage differences imply galvanic isolation."],"reason":"Challenge center-tapped/shared-return versus independent isolated secondaries, autotransformer and regulator distinctions, retaining key index 2.","newPrompt":"An AC transformer must provide TWO output windings with independently floating returns and specified primary-to-secondary safety isolation. Which construction specification directly meets this requirement?","sourceModuleId":"M09","sourcePageId":"L07-0-11"},{"id":"ALF52-A-072","expectedPrompt":"A silicon diode forward biased near normal conduction is measured. Which observation is broadly plausible at modest current?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["A silicon diode's forward voltage is exactly 0.70 V for every specified current and temperature as long as the package carries a silicon marking.","A forward drop on the order of 0.6–0.8 V depending on current and temperature","A fixed -100 V reverse drop","Infinite forward resistance at all currents"],"newChoices":["Si: 10.0 mA and 7.0 mW; Ge: 9.15 mA and 2.75 mW (the currents are larger with a higher assumed forward drop).","Si: about 9.15 mA and 6.4 mW; Ge: about 10.0 mA and 3.0 mW.","Si: about 9.15 mA and 39 mW; Ge: about 10.0 mA and 47 mW (resistor dissipation used as diode power).","Si: about 0.915 mA and 0.64 mW; Ge: about 1.00 mA and 0.30 mW (using 4.7 kΩ instead of 470 Ω)."],"reason":"Replace one-point silicon recognition with real silicon/germanium current and diode-power analysis directly taught by CV3-002; retain 3.7.2 objective and answer index 1.","newPrompt":"A paper circuit has a 5.0 V DC source, a 470 Ω resistor and one forward-oriented diode. Approximate the silicon forward drop as 0.7 V or the germanium drop as 0.3 V. Which pair correctly gives each series current and DIODE power?","sourceModuleId":"M11","sourcePageId":"L08-0-03"},{"id":"ALF52-A-073","expectedPrompt":"A rechargeable cell pack approaches full charge. What must the charger control?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Voltage/current profile and termination appropriate to its chemistry and protection design","Use the same charging profile for all battery chemistries because a correct connector determines charge safety","Apply an unchanging current indefinitely so the voltage can rise without an end-of-charge or thermal condition","Reverse the pack terminals after each cycle to restore lost capacity without checking polarity or cell design"],"newChoices":["Hold the approved constant-voltage limit, monitor tapering current and terminate under the maker’s current/temperature/protection criteria.","Stop regulating voltage and continue the original constant charge current until the current spontaneously becomes zero.","Transition directly to a lead-acid-style indefinite float/trickle stage merely because the Li-ion voltage target was reached.","Increase charging voltage above the manufacturer’s upper limit to restore the original constant-current rate after the voltage stage begins."],"reason":"Make learners distinguish CC→CV and termination in Li-ion from inappropriate continued CC, indefinite float and elevated voltage; direct remediation to already existing detailed charging lesson.","newPrompt":"An approved Li-ion charger has finished its specified constant-current stage and the cell has reached the specified upper charging voltage. What is the appropriate next control principle?","sourceModuleId":"M13","sourcePageId":"L08-0-02"},{"id":"ALF52-A-074","expectedPrompt":"Before soldering near a flammable solvent, what should the worker do?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["Heat the solvent container to reduce viscosity","Use a small electric soldering iron beside a covered solvent container, assuming its tip cannot ignite vapors below the solvent boiling point.","Cover the bench in paper towels","Remove or control the ignition hazard, use approved ventilation and follow site safety procedures"],"newChoices":["Begin immediately if the lid is tightly closed, since any vapor already released stops being flammable when the container is capped.","Run only the ordinary solder-fume extractor and start work while residual solvent vapor remains; filtering flux fumes also eliminates vapor ignition risk.","Use additional ESD grounding and a lower tip temperature, then proceed without checking the solvent safety information or vapor hazard.","Remove or control the ignition hazard, use approved ventilation and follow site safety procedures"],"reason":"Teach and assess flammable-vapor ignition versus general ESD/fume controls; safe stop-work action remains keyed at index 3.","newPrompt":"A soldering task is scheduled beside a recently opened container of flammable cleaning solvent. The container is now closed, but vapor may remain near the work area. What is the safest decision before operating the hot iron?","sourceModuleId":"M15","sourcePageId":"L12-0-00"},{"id":"ALF52-A-076","expectedPrompt":"An RJ45 plug is crimped onto the wrong cable type and only some pairs test connected. What is the appropriate next step?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["Leave the existing mismatch between cable conductor type and connector contacts, then twist the failed conductors outside the plug as a repair.","Use the correct connector for conductor type, recrimp and verify wire map and performance","Remove insulation and leave bare wires","Force a second plug over the first"],"newChoices":["Keep the stranded-only plug, increase crimp force and accept the result once a simple continuity beep passes.","Use a solid-conductor-compatible plug and approved crimp tool, reseat conductors with required pair order/jacket support, then verify wire map and required link performance.","Replace the plug with the correct contact type but crimp it using ordinary pliers; a visible locking tab confirms electrical quality without further checks.","Use the matching plug and crimper, but skip pair identification and strain relief; a successful link-light indication alone certifies the cable."],"reason":"Ask for tool/contact/pair order/strain relief/test sequence instead of obvious rework choice; MCQ is conceptual evidence only, not physical demonstration of 7.13.","newPrompt":"A solid-conductor UTP cable was terminated using a modular plug intended only for stranded conductors, and its wire-map tester reports intermittent opens. Which retermination procedure addresses the cause and verifies the result?","sourceModuleId":"M22","sourcePageId":"L19-0-05"},{"id":"ALF52-A-077","expectedPrompt":"An audio amplifier clips a sine wave at its rails. What sound-quality result is likely?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Increased harmonic distortion caused by truncated waveform peaks","Perfectly linear reproduction at all amplitudes","Noise-free compression of all harmonics","Automatic correction of phase distortion"],"newChoices":["Increased harmonic distortion caused by truncated waveform peaks","A narrow notch confined to each zero crossing caused by underbiased push-pull output devices","A fixed 60 Hz component originating from supply ripple even when the amplifier is not driven","Broadband random noise added by an input stage that does not flatten waveform peaks"],"reason":"Direct clipping lesson already covers rail-limited peaks; replace noncompetitive absolutes with plausible alternative audio faults. Correct keyed cause remains index 0.","newPrompt":"A scope shows an amplifier output sine wave flattening at both positive and negative peaks only when input drive is increased. What most directly distinguishes this defect from hum, hiss or crossover distortion?","sourceModuleId":"M24","sourcePageId":"L10-0-06"},{"id":"ALF52-A-078","expectedPrompt":"A half-wave dipole is installed for a frequency band. How is approximate physical length initially selected?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["Select a half-wave antenna by transmitter output power, since frequency affects radio modulation but not the physical size required for resonance.","Always 1 meter regardless of frequency","By the audio tone used by the operator","By a fraction of wavelength with end-effect adjustment and measurement"],"newChoices":["Start near 2.0 m total, because the dipole occupies a full wavelength before adjusting end effects.","Start near 0.50 m total, because a half-wave dipole has a quarter-wavelength overall length.","Start near 1.0 m for each arm, because both arms must independently be a half-wavelength long.","Start near 1.0 m total, then adjust physical length for conductor geometry, surroundings and measured match."],"reason":"Test λ=c/f numerical transfer and dipole two-arm geometry; existing lesson explicitly teaches 150 MHz, approximate 1 m total and tuning qualifications.","newPrompt":"For a paper design at 150 MHz, use c≈3.0×10^8 m/s to estimate a half-wave dipole’s TOTAL starting conductive length before final tuning. Which approach is correct?","sourceModuleId":"M26","sourcePageId":"L20-0-00"},{"id":"ALF52-A-079","expectedPrompt":"A workshop produced 80 completed jobs using 160 labor hours. What productivity measure is correct?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["2 jobs per labor hour","80 jobs per minute","0.5 jobs per labor hour","160 jobs per job"],"newChoices":["0.500 jobs per labor-hour, found by averaging the two individual hourly rates without weighting.","1.944 jobs per labor-hour, found by dividing the department’s labor-hours by its jobs.","About 0.514 jobs per labor-hour, found by dividing 36 total jobs by 70 total labor-hours.","0.600 jobs per labor-hour, found by using the first technician’s jobs and hours only."],"reason":"Correct link from estimating page to productivity page, and test denominator-weighted department aggregation over a common period (CV3-029).","newPrompt":"Over the same week, technician A completed 24 accepted jobs in 40 labor-hours, while technician B completed 12 in 30 labor-hours. What is the department’s combined throughput in jobs per labor-hour?","sourceModuleId":"M28","sourcePageId":"L28-0-07"},{"id":"ALF52-A-081","expectedPrompt":"A drawing gives all dimensions in millimeters. Why must technicians observe this unit note?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Mistaking millimeters for inches causes enormous mechanical-placement errors","Metric values always mean AC voltage","The legend determines the resistor's power automatically","Dimension units never affect assembly"],"newChoices":["Reject: 1.010 in is 25.654 mm, above the allowed maximum of 25.600 mm.","Accept: 1.010 in is only 0.010 larger than 1.000, which is less than the stated 0.2 tolerance.","Accept: 1.010 in rounds to 1.0 in, so rounding eliminates the stated metric tolerance.","Reject: 1.010 in equals 25.654 cm, rather than millimeters, so it is greatly oversized."],"reason":"Replace generic unit-label recognition with measured dimensional tolerance comparison; page needs explicit conversion/tolerance reasoning under drawing principle 6.5.","newPrompt":"A mechanical detail calls for hole spacing 25.4 ±0.2 mm. An inspection measures 1.010 inches. Using 25.4 mm per inch, how should the technician interpret the drawing tolerance?","sourceModuleId":"M05","sourcePageId":"L13-0-00"},{"id":"ALF52-A-083","expectedPrompt":"A rectifier output has noticeable 120 Hz ripple on a 60 Hz full-wave system. Which component generally helps smooth it?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["A microphone mounted nearby","Place a resistor in series with the output but omit any energy-storage element; resistance alone removes periodic ripple at unchanged DC load current.","Properly selected filter capacitor and/or additional ripple filtering","A disconnected resistor across no nodes"],"newChoices":["Two series capacitors carry the DC load current, while a shunt inductor diverts ripple voltage to the return.","The inductor stores charge on its plates, while the capacitors stop all DC load current across the supply output.","Shunt capacitors store charge and bypass ripple; the series choke opposes ripple-current changes while carrying DC.","A C–L–C filter changes rectifier ripple from 120 Hz to 60 Hz without needing any filtering action."],"reason":"More representative ETA 10.4 filter-topology question, linked directly to existing capacitor-input C-L-C π filter lesson, rather than only generic smoothing.","newPrompt":"A 60 Hz full-wave rectifier has 120 Hz ripple. Its unregulated supply uses a C–L–C (π) filter after the rectifier. What do these elements do to ripple while passing DC load current?","sourceModuleId":"M13","sourcePageId":"L08-0-00"},{"id":"ALF52-A-084","expectedPrompt":"A soldering objective requires the student to demonstrate wick removal on actual hardware. Which evidence would be sufficient for a physical-demonstration claim?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["An automatically scored written quiz is sufficient proof of wick removal proficiency even if nobody has observed the student's actual soldering technique.","Qualified observation of the safe technique and inspected post-rework board","Claiming a video was watched","Typing the word 'complete' with no inspection"],"newChoices":["Press dry braid hard onto the cold pad, pull it off repeatedly, and use mechanical friction instead of heating to avoid dwell time.","Use compatible flux and controlled heat to wet the braid, lift iron and braid together after solder wicks, then cool and inspect the pad.","Heat a joint until the braid attaches, then pull the braid sideways while solder remains solid to strip excess solder quickly.","Raise the tip far above the approved process range to shorten heating, and omit subsequent visual inspection when solder disappears."],"reason":"Change meta-assessment about evidence into a real procedural judgment. Written MCQ remains conceptual ONLY and cannot establish hands-on ETA 5.12 demonstration proficiency.","newPrompt":"On a de-energized, cooled and properly supported training PCB, a technician plans to remove excess solder with fluxed copper braid. Which described procedure best preserves the copper pad?","sourceModuleId":"M15","sourcePageId":"L12-0-08"},{"id":"ALF52-A-085","expectedPrompt":"A programmable logic controller reads a limit switch and controls a conveyor. Which function is central?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Industrial input logic executing control outputs with deterministic scan or event behavior","Generating internet search results","Providing mains isolation with no power circuitry","Replace the physical limit switch with a fixed memory bit and treat the program's internal cycle as proof that actual conveyor position is known."],"newChoices":["Read the physical limit input, evaluate the stop condition, and de-energize the ordinary motor-enable output when the end limit is true.","Keep the motor output latched until the next manual start command; input changes only update indicators during the scan.","Replace the hardwired input with a permanently true software value to make the controller cycle consistently regardless of position.","Toggle the motor output on every PLC scan regardless of the limit input; faster scan timing ensures correct stop position."],"reason":"Tests actual input/scan/output control decision; never treats normal PLC logic as sufficient for safety-rated machine stopping.","newPrompt":"In a simplified instructional PLC scan, an end-of-travel limit input changes from FALSE to TRUE while a conveyor motor is enabled. Which control rule responds correctly to the physical input?","sourceModuleId":"M20","sourcePageId":"L14-0-04"},{"id":"ALF52-A-086","expectedPrompt":"An equipment repair uses a thin-gauge wire for a higher-current supply. What should be checked?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["Use wire with a matching insulation color to the original assembly; the supply's nominal voltage determines safe wire current without other checks.","Whether the spool is expensive","Assume AWG number is unrelated to conductor size","Conductor ampacity, voltage drop, insulation, installation and applicable standards"],"newChoices":["22 AWG: about 3.39 V drop; choose it because the largest AWG number uses the least copper.","18 AWG: about 1.34 V drop; choose it because its drop is close to 1.0 V.","14 AWG: about 0.53 V drop; choose it because a larger conductor is always necessary.","16 AWG: about 0.83 V drop; it is the thinnest listed choice within the 1.0 V limit."],"reason":"Turn one credible broad checklist into a bounded AWG size calculation; explicitly keep ampacity, thermal/insulation and code checks separate from voltage-drop constraint.","newPrompt":"For a paper 4 A DC load with a 16 m total conductor loop and maximum 1.0 V permissible drop, compare these stated copper resistances: 22 AWG 0.053 Ω/m, 18 AWG 0.021 Ω/m, 16 AWG 0.013 Ω/m, 14 AWG 0.0083 Ω/m. Which is the thinnest listed conductor that meets the drop limit, subject to all other ratings?","sourceModuleId":"M22","sourcePageId":"L19-0-03"},{"id":"ALF52-A-087","expectedPrompt":"A receiver produces unwanted squeals when two audio paths interfere. Which behavior could cause conflict?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["Treat any signal coupled between nearby audio paths as beneficial negative feedback, even if the amplifier starts oscillating or howling.","Turning off all power","Coupling or feedback between signals leading to oscillation or interference","Applying a stable shielded cable in every case"],"newChoices":["A 60 Hz supply-ground hum added equally whether the microphone channel is muted or active.","A power-stage clipping limit that truncates waveform peaks without requiring sound to reenter the microphone.","Speaker output coupling back into the microphone with sufficient in-phase loop gain to sustain acoustic feedback.","Random preamp hiss that is uncorrelated with speaker position and does not form a closed audio loop."],"reason":"Teach reinforcing closed-loop positive acoustic feedback; contrast with hum, clipping and random hiss without advising removal of safety grounding.","newPrompt":"A microphone-to-amplifier-to-speaker setup develops a rising, narrow squeal when the speaker faces the microphone; the squeal stops when the mic is muted. Which mechanism best explains the symptom?","sourceModuleId":"M24","sourcePageId":"L19-0-08"},{"id":"ALF52-A-088","expectedPrompt":"A coaxial feed line joins a transmitter to a 50-ohm antenna system. What is a suitable cable consideration?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["The print color of the outer jacket alone","Characteristic impedance and loss at the operating frequency","Whether the cable is made of solid steel only","AC mains frequency rating exclusively"],"newChoices":["Coaxial line has no outer conducting shield, while twin-lead is necessarily a hollow metallic tube operated as a waveguide.","Coax is generally an unbalanced center/shield line; twin-lead is a balanced parallel-conductor line with a different typical impedance.","A metallic waveguide is an ordinary twisted-pair audio cord, so it shares the same low-frequency and cutoff behavior as twin-lead.","A nominal 75 Ω coax line always matches a 50 Ω RF connector system because both use metal conductors with low DC resistance."],"reason":"Match ETA 19.2.1 list/compare feedline types instead of generic cable choice; existing lesson specifically covers coax, balanced parallel-conductor lines and waveguides.","newPrompt":"Which comparison correctly identifies common RF feedline TYPES and an important interface distinction between them?","sourceModuleId":"M26","sourcePageId":"L20-0-04"},{"id":"ALF52-A-091","expectedPrompt":"A parallel RLC circuit is observed around resonance. Which property distinguishes branches?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["Both branches of a parallel RLC circuit necessarily carry in-phase currents because they share the same instantaneous terminal voltage.","An inductor is a perfect DC open circuit","Inductor and capacitor currents can oppose in phase even while sharing the same voltage","Capacitor current disappears at every AC frequency"],"newChoices":["Capacitive, because the capacitor branch leads the voltage even though its reactive current is smaller.","Resistive, because the inductive and capacitive branch currents have equal magnitudes and exactly cancel.","Inductive, because the net reactive current is 2 A lagging after the leading and lagging currents combine as phasors.","Capacitive, because the resistive current and capacitor current must add to 3 A leading and override the inductive branch."],"reason":"Change the assessed construct to official 4.7 inductive/capacitive/resistive classification using actual parallel-branch phasor arithmetic; key index 2 retained. Add instructional application in same receiving lesson.","newPrompt":"An ideal parallel RLC network has 2 A resistive current in phase with the shared AC voltage, 3 A inductive branch current lagging by 90°, and 1 A capacitive branch current leading by 90°. How should the net circuit be classified?","sourceModuleId":"M08","sourcePageId":"L06-0-03"},{"id":"ALF52-A-092","expectedPrompt":"Which amplifier precedes a power amplifier and raises weak sensor-level audio?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["Passive attenuator only","Preamplifier","RF dummy load","Antenna duplexer"],"newChoices":["A line buffer intended mainly to drive a cable at roughly unity gain; the 10 mV level is already adequate for the next stage.","A low-noise microphone preamplifier with voltage gain near 100 V/V (40 dB) before the line driver and power amplifier.","A speaker power stage configured to deliver roughly 1 V RMS at high current directly into the microphone capsule.","A passive line attenuator with a 40 dB voltage reduction to protect the line-level input from overload."],"reason":"Require distinguishing microphone preamplifier, line amplifier and speaker power stage, with correct gain calculation. Existing M14 page directly teaches functional roles; no new teaching required.","newPrompt":"A microphone produces 10 mV RMS and the next line-level processing stage expects approximately 1 V RMS, while a separate power amplifier drives the loudspeaker. What first amplifier stage and approximate voltage gain are appropriate?","sourceModuleId":"M14","sourcePageId":"L10-0-00"},{"id":"ALF52-A-093","expectedPrompt":"A seven-segment display must show decimal 2. Which design element converts binary-coded input to segment drive pattern?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["BCD-to-seven-segment decoder or display driver","Passive antenna without electronics","Mains rectifier bridge alone","Ladder logic that never receives data"],"newChoices":["a, b, d, e, g; c and f off.","a, b, c, d, g; e and f off.","a, c, d, f, g; b and e off.","a, b, c, d, e, f, g; all segments on."],"reason":"Assess actual digit-code-to-segment pattern reasoning rather than name-only recognition; retain valid key at index 0. Add missing explicit segment lettering/table prerequisites.","newPrompt":"A BCD-to-seven-segment decoder receives 0010 (decimal 2). Using the standard a–g segment lettering, which illuminated pattern should its current-limited common-cathode display produce?","sourceModuleId":"M17","sourcePageId":"L11-0-05"},{"id":"ALF52-A-094","expectedPrompt":"A laptop loses all files after drive failure. Which policy most directly mitigates future loss?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["Saving a second desktop shortcut","Renaming folders every month","Assuming RAID alone replaces all backup","Versioned backups tested with an actual restore"],"newChoices":["A mirrored local SSD set that tolerates one hardware failure but retains the latest file deletions on the surviving drive.","Cloud folder synchronization alone, which propagates new versions and deletions to connected clients without a separate recovery plan.","A single full backup kept on the same laptop SSD, checked only for a successful backup-complete notification.","Independent versioned backup copies outside the original failure domain, with tested restoration of a known earlier version."],"reason":"Require comparison between redundancy, sync, single-failure-domain copies and independently tested versioned backup rather than trivial cosmetic distractors. Direct existing lesson is adequate.","newPrompt":"A service laptop must recover measurement logs after either SSD failure or an accidental deletion that synchronizes to the cloud. Which backup arrangement best supports recovery from BOTH events?","sourceModuleId":"M21","sourcePageId":"L17-0-05"},{"id":"ALF52-A-095","expectedPrompt":"A photovoltaic cell is illuminated while driving a suitable load. What produces its electrical output?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["The case expands mechanically as the only energy source","A perpetual magnet inside the cell","Photons create mobile electron-hole pairs separated by the cell's junction field","Attribute photovoltaic output to light reflected from the glass cover without semiconductor carrier generation or an internal electric field."],"newChoices":["Heating the cell creates a Seebeck voltage across two dissimilar-metal junctions; semiconductor electron–hole generation is incidental.","Light merely changes the resistance of the cell so that an external battery supplies all output current as in a photoresistor circuit.","Absorbed photons generate electron–hole pairs; junction fields and carrier transport collect them, building a voltage that drives current through the load.","Illumination directly ejects electrons from the protective glass into the air and then back to the load without carrier generation in the semiconductor."],"reason":"Replace clearly absurd options with neighboring photothermal, photoconductive and external-photoelectric misconceptions; explicitly teach junction carrier collection and load current.","newPrompt":"A silicon p–n-junction solar cell supplies current to an external load only when adequately illuminated. Which physical process explains its ability to deliver electrical power without an external bias supply?","sourceModuleId":"M25","sourcePageId":"L19-0-10"},{"id":"ALF52-A-096","expectedPrompt":"A field technician cannot access a shop bench. Which change in service planning is reasonable?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["Disable record keeping when away from the office","Carry approved portable test equipment, spares, documentation and site safety controls","Perform unqualified live-mains tests for speed","Skip site-specific authorization and grounding checks if the product's bench repair procedure was previously approved for that same hardware."],"newChoices":["Repeat the shop test verbatim with the same fixtures and disregard site access, environmental conditions and equipment authorization.","Arrange authorized access and isolation, select approved portable instruments and likely spares, document site conditions, and plan escalation if safe fault isolation is impossible.","Ship every installed unit to the shop immediately before reviewing fault logs, power conditions or access constraints, because field diagnosis cannot produce useful evidence.","Rely on remote logs alone and schedule replacement without confirming wiring, physical conditions or required acceptance testing after the work."],"reason":"Assess comparisons between controlled bench diagnosis and constrained field workflow; choose the existing direct field-vs-shop lesson rather than a solely reactive page. Keep safety and authorization requirements.","newPrompt":"A board passes bench diagnostics but fails intermittently at a customer installation with restricted equipment access. Which FIELD service plan best addresses constraints that were absent in the shop?","sourceModuleId":"M28","sourcePageId":"L28-0-05"},{"id":"ALF52-A-100","expectedPrompt":"A relay must switch a 24 V DC inductive coil. What should be reviewed at the contacts?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["Connect a shunt capacitor across the input and omit both series chokes, since that is electrically identical to an L-C-L T-network.","Contact DC ratings, coil load, arcing and suitable suppression","The glass color of the relay case","Whether the coil is physically round"],"newChoices":["Use an AC-only triac solid-state relay; zero-cross turn-off will reliably interrupt the DC load and eliminate the need to check its ratings.","Select an electromechanical NO contact rated for the DC inductive load and switching life, then evaluate approved suppression at the SOLENOID without confusing it with the relay actuator-coil clamp.","Select any dry-contact reed relay rated for low-level signals; a low contact resistance automatically guarantees safe DC inductive switching.","Choose a relay from its coil voltage alone; a 24 V actuator coil guarantees contacts rated to break every 24 V DC solenoid current."],"reason":"Evaluate relay type (electromechanical vs AC triac SSR/reed), NO contact behavior, DC inductive breaking ratings and load-side suppression distinctly from relay coil protection; supports 3.9 and subskills 3.9.1–3.9.3.","newPrompt":"A controller must interrupt a 24 V DC solenoid (inductive LOAD) and requires a specified normally-open contact with no leakage in the off state. Which relay choice and switching protection best match those requirements?","sourceModuleId":"M09","sourcePageId":"L07-0-02"},{"id":"ALF52-B-006","expectedPrompt":"Which biasing choice keeps a BJT operating approximately in its linear amplification region?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["A permanently shorted collector-emitter path","An appropriate DC quiescent point with headroom for the signal swing","A reversed supply on all terminals","No source of collector current under any condition"],"newPrompt":"A common-emitter NPN amplifier has a 12 V collector supply, with its emitter voltage small relative to 12 V. A roughly symmetrical 4 V-peak collector signal is required. Which measured collector DC bias is the best starting point, subject to checking the actual load line and device limits?","newChoices":["About 11.5 V, because placing the collector almost at the positive rail ensures equal positive and negative signal headroom.","About 6 V, allowing substantial swing toward both saturation and the positive supply rail while retaining bias stabilization.","About 0.5 V, because biasing just above saturation provides the largest unclipped negative and positive swing.","About 9.5 V, because maximizing the static collector voltage is more important than preserving headroom toward the upper rail."],"sourceModuleId":"M12","sourcePageId":"L08-0-05","reason":"Replace three impossible distractors with plausible Q-point/headroom misconceptions; preserve true key 1 and direct transistor-bias remediation. Supply and emitter approximations are explicitly specified."},{"id":"ALF52-B-007","expectedPrompt":"A transistor amplifier has an emitter resistor bypassed by a capacitor. Why might the capacitor be fitted?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["To reduce AC emitter degeneration while retaining DC bias stabilization","To short the power rails at all frequencies","To replace the collector load","To make the circuit independent of transistor temperature"],"newPrompt":"A common-emitter BJT amplifier has a DC emitter resistor R_E with a capacitor directly in parallel. The measured small-signal gain is higher at midband than at very low frequencies. Why?","newChoices":["At midband the capacitor has relatively low reactance and shunts part of the AC emitter current around R_E, reducing local negative feedback; at DC R_E still stabilizes bias.","At midband the capacitor increases emitter negative feedback, while at DC it carries the bias current that stabilizes the Q-point.","At very low frequency the capacitor becomes a near-short, removing R_E from the AC path; at midband it opens and reduces gain.","The capacitor changes the collector load resistance rather than the emitter AC impedance, so gain rises without changing emitter feedback."],"sourceModuleId":"M12","sourcePageId":"L08-0-05","reason":"Repair incorrect generic op-amp remediation by teaching emitter degeneration and bypass frequency response adjacent to BJT Q-point; wrong options now make credible frequency/feedback errors."},{"id":"ALF52-B-010","expectedPrompt":"A simple computer block diagram includes CPU, RAM, nonvolatile storage and I/O. Which part stores actively running program data temporarily?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["Power cord insulation","RAM","The monitor bezel","An audio jack only"],"newPrompt":"A computer loads an application from its solid-state drive, edits a document without saving, then loses power. The installed application remains stored, but the unsaved work held in the main working memory is lost. Which explanation matches the computer blocks?","newChoices":["The SSD serves as volatile working memory while CPU registers provide the persistent installation files.","RAM holds the main volatile working data; nonvolatile SSD storage retains files and installed applications through a power loss.","The CPU cache is the general-purpose persistent storage location for installed programs, while the SSD holds only the unsaved document.","Firmware ROM normally holds the editable unsaved document, while RAM retains the complete installed application without power."],"sourceModuleId":"M20","sourcePageId":"L14-0-00","reason":"Test interplay of RAM, nonvolatile SSD, CPU cache and firmware rather than identifying the only memory term; existing major-computer-sections page already gives the prerequisite."},{"id":"ALF52-B-011","expectedPrompt":"A fiber cable has two ends that must be joined with low loss. What technique is commonly used for a permanent precision splice?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Fusion splicing with appropriate preparation and measured verification","Join fiber ends by polishing them and clamping their jackets together, because mechanical jacket alignment guarantees core alignment and splice loss.","Wrapping the broken ends with copper wire","Soldering the glass cores together"],"newPrompt":"A fiber technician can use either a fusion splicer or a rated precision mechanical splice. Which comparison correctly describes the two methods and a needed quality check?","newChoices":["Fusion uses prepared, accurately aligned ends joined by controlled heat; a mechanical splice aligns prepared glass ends in a precision fixture, often with index-matching material. Verify splice loss/protection as specified.","Both methods require identical heating of glass ends; a mechanical splice differs mainly because its protective sleeve is removable after cooling.","Fusion avoids fiber cleaving and loss verification because the arc automatically corrects bad end faces; a mechanical splice instead requires a clean cleave.","Mechanical splicing relies on aligning outer cable jackets, so precise fiber-core alignment and loss assessment matter only in fusion splicing."],"sourceModuleId":"M22","sourcePageId":"L19-0-05","reason":"Cover genuine fusion versus mechanical splice principle, preparation and verification under 7.9.1; existing cable-preparation teaching provides the prerequisites."},{"id":"ALF52-B-012","expectedPrompt":"An audio recorder samples at 48 kHz. What is its ideal Nyquist frequency, the boundary above which input signals would alias without filtering?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["48 kHz","96 kHz","12 Hz","24 kHz"],"newPrompt":"An ideal audio ADC uniformly samples at 48,000 samples/s. If an unwanted 30 kHz sine reaches its input without effective anti-alias filtering, what is the Nyquist frequency and the lowest-frequency alias it can produce?","newChoices":["48 kHz Nyquist; 30 kHz appears unaliased because it is below the sample rate.","96 kHz Nyquist; 30 kHz is too slow to cause aliasing at this sampling rate.","24 kHz Nyquist; 30 kHz aliases to 6 kHz because alias frequency is the excess over Nyquist.","24 kHz Nyquist; 30 kHz aliases to 18 kHz because |30−48| kHz folds into the baseband."],"sourceModuleId":"M24","sourcePageId":"L19-0-07","reason":"Keep correct answer position 3 while strengthening sampling-rate, folding and anti-alias inference; insert missing Nyquist and alias derivation in assigned recording lesson."},{"id":"ALF52-B-013","expectedPrompt":"A receiver is intended to listen to a broadcast without transmitting. What distinguishes it from a two-way transceiver?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["A receiver necessarily has no antenna","A transceiver has no local oscillator","A receiver has no intentional RF transmit function","A receiver can only use analog audio"],"newPrompt":"A city dispatch system needs a worker to hear an assigned call AND reply over an authorized radio channel; a music station sends a program to thousands of listeners who do not reply through their radios. What equipment/use distinction is correct?","newChoices":["A standard broadcast receiver transmits acknowledgments back on its reception frequency, while a dispatch transceiver can receive only.","Both systems require every listener to transmit to the sender because radio reception by itself cannot recover speech or music.","The worker uses a communications transceiver with intentional transmit and receive functions; the audience uses broadcast receivers for one-to-many reception.","Dispatch can use only commercial broadcast receivers as long as they have a local oscillator; local-oscillator signals provide the return voice transmission."],"sourceModuleId":"M26","sourcePageId":"L20-0-05","reason":"Exercise ETA 19.5 radio usage comparison (dispatch response versus one-to-many broadcast) using genuine receiver/transceiver functional distinctions; key remains position 2."},{"id":"ALF52-B-015","expectedPrompt":"A technician is preparing to use compressed air on a dusty PCB. Which concern requires consulting the site procedure?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Eye protection, pressure limits, aerosol hazards and ESD-sensitive assemblies","Air pressure always makes circuit boards intrinsically safe","Only the nozzle color matters","Compressed air can always be used on energized HV supplies"],"newPrompt":"At a U.S. general-industry electronics bench, an authorized technician proposes compressed air to clean a DEENERGIZED ESD-sensitive board. Assuming air cleaning is permitted by the site and no stricter rule applies, which plan satisfies OSHA 29 CFR 1910.242(b) and board-specific controls?","newChoices":["Use an approved method with cleaning-air nozzle pressure reduced below 30 psi, effective chip/particle guarding and PPE; also follow ESD, contamination and equipment procedures.","Use 30 psi nozzle pressure exactly with safety glasses but omit chip guarding because the board produces dust rather than metal chips.","Use 20 psi with an ESD-controlled workstation but omit particle guarding and PPE because a low pressure alone satisfies the federal cleaning rule.","Use full shop line pressure with guarding and PPE because 1910.242(b) sets only a recommended pressure, not a required restriction."],"sourceModuleId":"M01","sourcePageId":"L01-0-06","reason":"Require actual OSHA compressed-air cleaning limit and combined guarding/PPE controls instead of eliminating absurd hazard claims. Add targeted instruction and procedural boundaries to existing pneumatic-energy safety page."},{"id":"ALF52-B-017","expectedPrompt":"A triangle in a schematic has a small bubble on its output. What does the bubble normally denote?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["An external mains transformer","Physical speaker placement","Logical inversion at that signal terminal","The test-point ground symbol"],"newChoices":["An enabled high-impedance output, because the bubble indicates an output-enable input.","A logic HIGH output, because the triangle passes logic unchanged despite the output bubble.","A logic LOW output, because the bubble means inversion of the input logic state.","A toggling output on each rising input edge, because the bubble marks a clock trigger."],"sourceModuleId":"M17","sourcePageId":"L11-0-01","reason":"Preserve the correct inversion key but replace unrelated schematic distractors with genuine logic-symbol misconceptions; link to the existing gate-symbol passage that explicitly explains bubble notation. Primary objective 6.1 remains schematic symbol recognition.","newPrompt":"A digital buffer symbol is a triangle with a small inversion bubble on its output. With the input held at a valid logic HIGH, which output behavior does the symbol indicate?"},{"id":"ALF52-B-018","expectedPrompt":"A capacitor is used across a DC supply near an IC. What function is it likely serving?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["A guaranteed voltage booster without circuitry","Local decoupling that supplies transient current and reduces noise","A permanent DC current source","A motor bearing replacement"],"newChoices":["Its main job is to sit in series with the supply, blocking DC but passing the IC current pulses.","It supplies local transient charge and bypasses some high-frequency rail noise; layout, ESR and ESL still affect performance.","It regulates the 3.3 V rail independently of the power source, so supply droop cannot occur during large pulses.","It stores charge only at power-off; moving it farther from the IC reduces loop inductance and improves high-frequency decoupling."],"sourceModuleId":"M07","sourcePageId":"L07-0-04","reason":"Add true local IC-rail decoupling physics, time-domain droop estimate and parasitic limits to capacitor-type lesson; options compete on series/shunt connection, ideal-regulation myth and physical layout.","newPrompt":"An IC draws brief extra current pulses on a 3.3 V rail. A ceramic capacitor is mounted close between its supply and ground pins. Which explanation best predicts its benefit and limitation?"},{"id":"ALF52-B-019","expectedPrompt":"A compass placed near an energized straight wire deflects. Which field is responsible?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Magnetic field circulating around the current-carrying wire","Only the wire's printed insulation color","An acoustic pressure field","A perfect static electric field with no magnetic component"],"newChoices":["Wire: concentric circles; bar magnet: external flux from north toward south; solenoid: approximately axial interior field.","Wire: straight axial field; bar magnet: concentric circles; solenoid: purely radial interior field.","Wire: purely radial field; bar magnet: uniform zero exterior field; solenoid: concentric circles only.","Wire: concentric circles; bar magnet: no exterior field; solenoid: purely radial interior field."],"sourceModuleId":"M09","sourcePageId":"L07-0-10","reason":"Test comparison of three magnetic-field configurations under ETA 2.2.1 instead of recognition of one mechanism; already explicitly taught in target lesson, with geometry-based distractors.","newPrompt":"Which comparison correctly describes the magnetic field geometry around a long straight current-carrying wire, a bar magnet and a long energized solenoid?"},{"id":"ALF52-B-023","expectedPrompt":"A clock input runs at 2 MHz and a digital divider-by-four counter is used. What nominal output frequency results?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["500 kHz","8 MHz","2 kHz","1 MHz"],"newChoices":["500 kHz","8 MHz","2 kHz","1 MHz"],"sourceModuleId":"M10","sourcePageId":"L11-0-04","reason":"Key, numeric distractors and stem are already sound. Only change required is redirecting answer remediation from unrelated bus-timing lesson to the existing counter/divider lesson that teaches f_out=f_in/N; no gratuitous item rewriting."},{"id":"ALF52-B-026","expectedPrompt":"A capacitor reads its nominal capacitance but has unexpectedly large ESR. What trouble is plausible?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["The dielectric permittivity becomes infinite","Excess heating and ripple under AC ripple-current service","All capacitance measurements must report zero","Every capacitor's ESR is meant to be exactly 0 ohms"],"newChoices":["0.25 W heating; nominal capacitance proves acceptable ripple performance at every frequency.","0.50 W heating; compare ESR test frequency and temperature with operating specifications.","1.00 W heating; an ESR meter directly measures DC insulation leakage at operating voltage.","0.125 W heating; the ESR reading establishes the same loss at all frequencies and temperatures."],"sourceModuleId":"M23","sourcePageId":"L03-0-08","reason":"Replace impossible options with P=Irms²×ESR calculation and instrumentation caveat demanded by 8.9, with realistic numeric near misses. Teach meter methods and limits directly.","newPrompt":"A 100 µF electrolytic tests near its nominal capacitance, but an ESR meter reports 2.0 Ω at a stated test frequency. If 0.50 A RMS ripple current flows and ESR remains 2.0 Ω at that operating condition, which estimate and instrument caution are correct?"},{"id":"ALF52-B-027","expectedPrompt":"An instrument has a graphical panel of pixels illuminated from behind. Which display type is a plausible example?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["LCD with backlight","A passive relay contact","An analog fuse","A toroidal inductor"],"newChoices":["Backlit LCD: liquid-crystal pixels modulate the separate light source.","OLED: each addressed pixel emits light and does not need a backlight.","Seven-segment LED: discrete diode segments emit fixed digit shapes.","CRT: a scanned electron beam excites phosphor on the screen."],"sourceModuleId":"M25","sourcePageId":"L19-0-09","reason":"Compare four real display technologies instead of one display against three unrelated components; same assigned lesson already teaches all four types, supplemented by CV3 teaching.","newPrompt":"An instrument panel forms an image with pixels that modulate light from a separate LED illumination layer instead of emitting their own light. Which display mechanism matches?"},{"id":"ALF52-B-028","expectedPrompt":"Two wireless nodes connect only intermittently in a noisy plant. Which factor deserves attention?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["Only the color of the network icons","The mechanical size of the keyboard","Ignore adjacent RF channels and plant interference because a digital network's error detection prevents all connection loss from collisions.","Protocol, RF band, channel congestion, antenna placement and signal margin"],"newChoices":["Check only the web application; ANSI/TIA-568 defines Wi-Fi radio channels and RF retransmission behavior.","Replace the patch-panel cable without RF tests; IEEE 802.11 defines the building copper-cabling link certification limits.","Increase AP transmit power without a survey; ANSI/TIA standards guarantee digital links cannot suffer interference.","Survey RF interference, channels and antenna placement; IEEE 802.11 governs WLAN radio, while the applicable ANSI/TIA-568 series addresses structured cabling."],"sourceModuleId":"M27","sourcePageId":"L20-0-08","reason":"Preserve 20.2 objective but concretely distinguish WLAN RF versus structured uplink, IEEE 802.11 versus ANSI/TIA-568 series. Exact standards/scope checked with IEEE SA and TIA official material; add worked layer-by-layer troubleshooting.","newPrompt":"Wireless LAN terminals in an industrial plant drop their Wi-Fi link near machinery; the access point has a wired building-network uplink. Which troubleshooting and standards pairing separates the two interfaces correctly?"},{"id":"ALF52-B-029","expectedPrompt":"Why might a power tool be rated in watts rather than only volts?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["Power depends only on enclosure volume","Watts express voltage alone while current describes a separate electrical quantity; the load's power rating is independent of both together.","Watts express rate of energy use or delivery as load current also matters","A watt is always a resistance unit"],"newChoices":["36 W, subtracting the two load currents before multiplying by 12 V.","24 W, since the smaller load determines a shared supply power budget.","84 W, adding 5.0 A and 2.0 A then multiplying by the 12 V source.","60 W, since only the higher-current tool determines total parallel power."],"sourceModuleId":"M03","sourcePageId":"L02-0-07","reason":"The original 2.4 basic-use objective mismatches actual P=VI power requirement. Remap to 2.14 in M03 and its parallel-load power-budget lesson; require arithmetic rather than definition alone.","newPrompt":"Two DC tools share a regulated 12 V source. One draws 5.0 A and the other 2.0 A while running together. Ignoring losses, what is their combined electrical input power?","expectedObjectiveIds":["2.4"],"expectedFocusObjectiveId":"2.4","newObjectiveIds":["2.14"],"newFocusObjectiveId":"2.14"},{"id":"ALF52-B-032","expectedPrompt":"A noisy DC power rail supplies sensitive analog circuitry. Which filter combination may help?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["A speaker diaphragm mounted on the PCB","Changing the font on the voltage label","Increase the unshielded power-lead length to attenuate noise, since added wire always acts as a predictable low-pass filter at any load.","Proper series impedance and shunt capacitance chosen for the frequency and load"],"newChoices":["The 10 Ω resistor drops about 0.2 V at 200 mA; it is safe to ignore DC voltage loss.","At 100 kHz the shunt capacitor impedance always increases, so a larger series resistor improves bypassing.","The resistor drops 2 V, but a second 10 Ω shunt resistor is a stronger high-frequency bypass than a capacitor.","The resistor drops 2 V; preserve shunt filtering but choose acceptable series DC loss and verify load transients."],"sourceModuleId":"M08","sourcePageId":"L06-0-02","reason":"Replace nonsense distractors with RC loss/bypass tradeoffs and a numeric DC-drop check grounded in M08 filter theory; add explicit supply-filter implementation example.","newPrompt":"A 5 V analog rail supplies a steady 200 mA load and carries 100 kHz switching noise. A proposed low-pass filter uses a 10 Ω series resistor followed by a 10 µF shunt capacitor. What design assessment is most defensible?"},{"id":"ALF52-B-033","expectedPrompt":"A clipped pulse has slow edges that must be sharpened. Which circuit is an appropriate candidate?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["Use a single reverse-biased diode with no threshold reference to restore both rising and falling edge timing independent of input amplitude.","Passive fuse only","Comparator with hysteresis or logic buffer within valid thresholds","A diode used at infinite reverse voltage"],"newPrompt":"A sensor waveform rises slowly through a logic threshold and has 80 mV of noise. The receiver detects repeated transitions near that threshold. Which conditioning change best produces one clean transition while preserving a defined switching boundary?","newChoices":["Increase only the series resistance at the receiver input, assuming slower edges always remove noise-induced toggling.","Add a simple clamp diode without a reference and assume it will define separate rising and falling thresholds.","Use a comparator or Schmitt-trigger input with appropriately chosen upper and lower switching thresholds.","Use a plain high-gain comparator without hysteresis and set its threshold exactly at the noisy input level."],"sourceModuleId":"M10","sourcePageId":"L04-0-08","reason":"Remove fuse/infinite-voltage distractors and test why two switching thresholds suppress chatter; existing directly taught Schmitt-trigger lesson is sufficient."},{"id":"ALF52-B-035","expectedPrompt":"An audio preamplifier raises a 5 mV microphone signal before the power stage. What class of device is it?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Small-signal preamplifier","Standalone RF load","Mains fuse only","Optical lens mount"],"newPrompt":"A microphone produces 5 mV, and an active stage boosts it to 150 mV for subsequent audio processing but cannot supply the current required to drive an 8 Ω speaker. Which amplifier role best describes this stage?","newChoices":["Small-signal audio preamplifier; it raises signal level without being designed as the final speaker power driver.","Audio power-output amplifier; its role is proved by the increased voltage even though output current is insufficient for a speaker.","RF power amplifier; a microphone requires a carrier-frequency stage whenever its voltage is below 1 V.","Broadband video line driver; the output signal amplitude alone determines that the stage is a video interface."],"sourceModuleId":"M14","sourcePageId":"L10-0-00","reason":"Remove answer word leaked in stem; require distinguishing voltage gain in small-signal chain from power-driving load. Existing ETA 11.1 amplifier-types passage directly teaches distinctions."},{"id":"ALF52-B-037","expectedPrompt":"Two instruments share signal ground through multiple distant paths and measure hum. Which issue is plausible?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["The cable being too short to contain electrons","A device's serial number changing automatically","Ground-loop currents introducing unwanted voltage differences","The instruments requiring opposite Earth rotations"],"newPrompt":"Two mains-powered audio test instruments are linked by a shielded signal cable and also share protective-earth connections. A 60 Hz hum appears when the signal cable is connected. What mechanism should be assessed first without defeating protective earth?","newChoices":["Insufficient analog bandwidth in the signal cable, which primarily attenuates high audio frequencies.","A mismatch of instrument input impedance, which can change gain but does not itself establish a mains-frequency return loop.","Parallel ground-return paths carrying unwanted current and developing a 60 Hz difference between signal references.","Microphone acoustic feedback between loudspeakers and microphones, even though no acoustic transducer is present."],"sourceModuleId":"M19","sourcePageId":"L18-0-03","reason":"Replace impossible distractions with real signal-integrity causes and explicitly teach ground-loop path currents and safe isolation. Keep ETA grounding objective 12.3."},{"id":"ALF52-B-039","expectedPrompt":"A spectrum analyzer displays a large peak at 100 MHz and harmonics. What is it measuring?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Spectral power or amplitude versus frequency within its specified setup","A spectrum analyzer plots signal amplitude only as a function of time; the horizontal axis cannot represent frequency or spectral harmonics.","PCB board dimensions","A password strength score"],"newPrompt":"A technician must determine whether a 100 MHz oscillator also emits a spur near 200 MHz. Which instrument setup and interpretation correctly support that check?","newChoices":["Set a spectrum analyzer span including 100 and 200 MHz, appropriate input attenuation/reference level and resolution bandwidth; examine peaks versus frequency.","Set an oscilloscope to display amplitude versus time at 1 ms/div; its horizontal axis directly identifies the 200 MHz spectral spur without further analysis.","Set a frequency counter to 100 MHz; its single dominant frequency readout independently quantifies all harmonic amplitudes.","Set a spectrum analyzer center frequency to 100 MHz with a 10 kHz span; this guarantees that a 200 MHz component lies on screen."],"sourceModuleId":"M23","sourcePageId":"L03-0-06","reason":"Go beyond recognizing plot axes by testing span, reference-level/input attenuation and resolution bandwidth. Point remediation to instrument controls lesson, not RF overview."},{"id":"ALF52-B-040","expectedPrompt":"An OLED panel shows light-emitting pixels without an LCD backlight. What is a key trade-off?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["An OLED must illuminate a shared backlight behind the display pixels, so its darkest blacks are limited by the same light leakage as an LCD.","OLED has no semiconductor materials","OLED can only display one fixed letter","Self-emission can improve black level, while lifetime/burn-in behavior may matter"],"newPrompt":"A control panel displays the same bright status icons for long periods. It is being changed from a backlit LCD to an OLED. Which comparison best predicts a likely benefit and a maintenance trade-off?","newChoices":["OLED pixels must share the same backlight as LCD pixels, so both have identical black-level limitations.","An OLED cannot reduce light emission for dark pixels; only an LCD can switch local pixels off.","OLED image persistence can be prevented completely by leaving each bright status icon continuously energized.","Self-emissive OLED pixels can give deep blacks, but unequal cumulative pixel aging can cause persistent image retention."],"sourceModuleId":"M25","sourcePageId":"L19-0-09","reason":"Replace unrelated distractors with display-physics misconceptions and teach cumulative OLED emitter wear explicitly under 18.1.1."},{"id":"ALF52-B-041","expectedPrompt":"A remote satellite uplink must point toward the target satellite. What is a central link concern?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["Reversing a battery label","Eliminating all propagation delay","Antenna pointing, path loss, polarization and operating frequency","Aim the uplink antenna toward the satellite but ignore polarization and fade margin because free-space path loss is independent of frequency."],"newPrompt":"A satellite terminal has adequate transmitter power but receives a weak downlink after its dish is moved. Which check best distinguishes an RF path problem from merely increasing transmitter output?","newChoices":["Assume a satellite beam remains aligned after dish movement as long as frequency is unchanged; change only modulation rate.","Check cable connector resistance only; dish orientation and polarization have no role once equipment is powered.","Verify pointing/line of sight, polarization, operating band, path loss and weather/fade margin as part of the receive-link budget.","Increase RF output power until alignment is irrelevant; a transmitter can overcome every polarization mismatch or blockage."],"sourceModuleId":"M27","sourcePageId":"L20-0-08","reason":"Assess real satellite link budget and alignment instead of nontechnical distractors; reinforce 20.3 with frequency-dependent path loss, polarization, fade and geometry."},{"id":"ALF52-B-043","expectedPrompt":"A carbon-film resistor is overheated by excessive load. Which basic property should be checked?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Resistance, tolerance and safe power dissipation","Only the ink color on packaging","A device's Wi-Fi MAC address","The capacitive reactance of its leads as the only rating"],"newPrompt":"A 220 Ω resistor in a fast-pulse sensing circuit needs stable resistance, limited parasitic inductance and a verified pulse-power rating. Which substitution decision best compares common resistor constructions?","newChoices":["Evaluate a suitable metal-film or qualified low-inductance type against tolerance, pulse and thermal ratings; do not assume a standard wirewound part is equivalent at fast edges.","Prefer any standard wirewound part because a wound resistance element has no parasitic inductance and always preserves fast pulse shape.","Choose a carbon-composition part solely because it is carbon based, without reviewing its tolerance, temperature behavior or pulse specification.","Replace it with an NTC thermistor having the same nominal room-temperature value because its resistance cannot change during the circuit pulse."],"sourceModuleId":"M03","sourcePageId":"L07-0-03","reason":"Shift from generic overheating to physical resistor-material selection under competency 2.7; existing construction/inductance/ratings discussion directly supports it."},{"id":"ALF52-B-044","expectedPrompt":"A block diagram depicts input, filter, mixer, IF amplifier and output. What can it support?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["Identifying every individual pin number","A block diagram specifies every resistor value and every internal net connection, so a detailed schematic is unnecessary for component-level fault tracing.","Proving exact PCB copper topology","Locating which functional stage may contain a fault before inspecting detail schematics"],"newPrompt":"A receiver block diagram shows antenna → RF filter → mixer → IF filter → demodulator. A valid RF signal reaches the mixer and the local oscillator is present, but no IF signal appears at the mixer output. What is the most defensible next step?","newChoices":["Begin by replacing the demodulator because it follows the IF filter, even though the expected IF has not been observed at the mixer output.","Use the block diagram to infer the exact failed transistor lead and solder pad; detailed schematics are unnecessary.","Conclude the antenna is faulty despite having measured a valid input to the mixer.","Localize the problem to the mixer or its immediate connections/conditions, then consult a schematic for component-level tests."],"sourceModuleId":"M05","sourcePageId":"L13-0-00","reason":"Replace direct replay of the lesson example with a different signal-trace decision and realistic stage-isolation errors; keep block-diagram objective 6.2."},{"id":"ALF52-B-045","expectedPrompt":"A replacement electrolytic capacitor is installed across a DC rail. Which part attribute is essential?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["Decorative sleeve color only","Making physical diameter identical regardless of voltage rating","Correct polarity, capacitance, voltage, ripple rating and temperature suitability","Ignoring leakage and ESR"],"newPrompt":"A DC filter needs a polarized 1000 µF electrolytic. The existing part is rated 35 V, 105 °C and at least 0.8 A ripple RMS; the supply can reach 28 V. Which proposed replacement best meets those minimum electrical requirements, subject to mechanical fit and ESR verification?","newChoices":["1000 µF, 25 V, 105 °C, 1.2 A ripple RMS; extra ripple capability compensates for voltage rating.","1000 µF, 35 V, 85 °C, 1.2 A ripple RMS; temperature rating can be reduced whenever voltage rating is unchanged.","1000 µF, 50 V, 105 °C, 1.0 A ripple RMS, correct polarity and otherwise compatible electrical specifications.","1000 µF, 50 V, 105 °C, 0.4 A ripple RMS; voltage headroom compensates for insufficient ripple capacity."],"sourceModuleId":"M07","sourcePageId":"L07-0-04","reason":"Evaluate competing real capacitor specs instead of cosmetic errors; add ripple self-heating, rating and qualification model to the assigned capacitor-type page."},{"id":"ALF52-B-046","expectedPrompt":"A motor has a permanent magnet rotor and current-carrying stator coils. What interaction produces torque?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["Only the motor's paint exerts torque","Magnetic fields of currents and magnets interact to exert force","Static capacitance always drives rotation alone","Resistance always becomes negative"],"newPrompt":"A permanent-magnet motor has a stationary magnetic field and a current-carrying conductor in its rotor. Why does it develop torque, and what happens if the current direction is reversed while the field remains fixed?","newChoices":["The force depends only on conductor resistance; changing current direction leaves torque direction unchanged.","Magnetic force on a current-carrying conductor generates torque; reversing current with field fixed reverses the instantaneous force direction.","Back EMF alone drives the rotor even at zero current, so current reversal cannot change electromagnetic torque.","Capacitive attraction produces rotation regardless of winding current or magnetic flux orientation."],"sourceModuleId":"M09","sourcePageId":"L07-0-01","reason":"Correct indirect magnetism lesson link to direct motor/generator page and test physical torque mechanism and sign under fixed field."},{"id":"ALF52-B-047","expectedPrompt":"A replacement diode must block 40 V reverse bias. Which rating is particularly relevant?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Reverse-voltage rating with appropriate margin","Only its forward case color","Its alphabetical part prefix alone","The capacitance of nearby connectors"],"newPrompt":"A silicon rectifier conducts 0.30 A with approximately 0.80 V forward drop and may have to block a 40 V reverse peak. Which statement correctly estimates instantaneous forward conduction loss and the reverse-voltage check?","newChoices":["About 0.24 W when conducting; select a diode with a sufficiently higher repetitive reverse-voltage rating and verify current/thermal limits.","About 0.24 W when conducting; select a repetitive reverse-voltage rating below 40 V because reverse current is normally small.","About 0.80 W when conducting; a 40 V reverse-voltage rating always provides adequate surge margin for a 40 V peak.","About 12 W of reverse loss from 40 V × 0.30 A; forward current necessarily flows unchanged during reverse blocking."],"sourceModuleId":"M11","sourcePageId":"L08-1-01","reason":"Original type-identification objective 3.7 cannot be established by a reverse-rating lookup. Remap to verified M11 objective 3.7.2 and require forward VI loss plus reverse-voltage rating decision.","expectedObjectiveIds":["3.7"],"expectedFocusObjectiveId":"3.7","newObjectiveIds":["3.7.2"],"newFocusObjectiveId":"3.7.2"},{"id":"ALF52-B-049","expectedPrompt":"A repair uses solder on a high-reliability PCB with controlled process requirements. What should guide alloy choice?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["Whichever wire melts at the highest possible temperature","Lead content guessed from color","Assembly specification, component/board compatibility and process temperature","Select solder strictly by the lowest material price, without verifying the assembly's alloy requirement or acceptable process temperature."],"newPrompt":"An approved high-reliability PCB repair specifies a lead-free SAC305 solder alloy and a qualified thermal profile. The store has Sn63/Pb37, low-melting Sn-Bi, and SAC305 wire. What is the defensible material choice?","newChoices":["Use Sn63/Pb37 because its eutectic 183 °C melting point makes it automatically interchangeable with any lead-free joint.","Use Sn-Bi because its lower melting range always protects every package without needing solder-joint reliability qualification.","Use specified SAC305 and the approved flux/process window; evaluate any alloy substitution through the assembly authority.","Choose any solder labeled lead-free because all lead-free alloys wet and solidify identically under one thermal profile."],"sourceModuleId":"M15","sourcePageId":"L29-0-02","reason":"Replace general checklist and absurd wrong choices with alloy-family and thermal/process compatibility discrimination for competency 5.5."},{"id":"ALF52-B-051","expectedPrompt":"A computer's diagram shows CPU, memory, and storage blocks; a program flowchart shows decisions and loops. What distinguishes them?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["A block diagram shows hardware functional relationships; a flowchart shows process/control sequence","A computer block diagram details the exact instruction order and decision outcomes, while a program flowchart shows how the physical chips are wired.","A flowchart only gives board dimensions","A block diagram is always software source code"],"newPrompt":"A troubleshooting team needs one drawing to locate the connection between a controller, RAM and a sensor interface, and another to explain why firmware retries a failed sensor read three times. Which pair of drawings is most appropriate, in that order?","newChoices":["Computer functional block diagram, then program flowchart.","Program flowchart, then PCB component-placement drawing.","Electrical schematic, then I/O terminal wiring diagram; both directly represent the loop decision structure.","Program flowchart for the connections, then a functional block diagram for the software branch decisions."],"sourceModuleId":"M20","sourcePageId":"L14-0-06","reason":"Test drawing selection for actual fault scenarios without stating the hardware-versus-sequence distinction in the stem; existing teaching is sufficient."},{"id":"ALF52-B-052","expectedPrompt":"A coax connector produces intermittent contact after repeated bending. What should be inspected?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["The invoice date only","LCD brightness","Adjust transmitter microphone gain until the connector stops intermittently opening, assuming the failure is caused by weak audio modulation.","Strain relief, center conductor engagement, shield termination and mating wear"],"newPrompt":"A de-energized coax patch cable intermittently loses its RF return path when flexed near its connector. A static check of the center conductor is normal. Which inspection and test plan best targets the remaining fault?","newChoices":["Replace the instrument source because center continuity proves both center and shield paths are intact.","Measure only DC insulation between the center and braid once, then declare the connector mechanically sound regardless of flexing.","Replace the coax with a same-thread connector without checking its characteristic impedance, braid termination or cable support.","Inspect shield-to-connector termination, strain relief and mating wear; perform approved shield-continuity and flex-sensitive checks."],"sourceModuleId":"M22","sourcePageId":"L19-0-13","reason":"Use shield-path evidence and plausible but incomplete test approaches; preserve a safe de-energized diagnosis and distinguish center from braid continuity."},{"id":"ALF52-B-055","expectedPrompt":"A replacement assembly part number differs by a revision suffix. What is the best action?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Cross-reference manufacturer documentation for approved interchangeability","Treat a replacement assembly's matching initial part digits as proof of interchangeability, even when the revision suffix changes protection circuitry.","Discard all service history","Assume revision suffixes never matter"],"newPrompt":"A service manual lists control board P-240-B for equipment revision R3. Inventory offers P-240-C with the same connector shape, but the revision matrix lists P-240-C only for equipment R4. What is the proper next decision?","newChoices":["Hold the substitution until controlled OEM documentation confirms compatibility or an approved retrofit for the exact R3 unit.","Install P-240-C because connector fit and a matching root part number override the revision-matrix limit.","Install P-240-C after checking only the power-rail voltage; firmware and safety-interlock changes do not affect board compatibility.","Use the newer part if its manufacture date is later, even if the service matrix excludes the installed unit revision."],"sourceModuleId":"M28","sourcePageId":"L28-0-04","reason":"Turn generic cross-reference recognition into controlled revision matrix interpretation while retaining originally correct answer and learning objective."},{"id":"ALF52-B-058","expectedPrompt":"A choke in an RF power circuit has a ferrite core. What is a common reason for selecting its core material?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["To force inductance to exactly zero","Magnetic permeability and loss characteristics appropriate for frequency/current","Choose a ferrite core strictly for its near-zero resistivity, because a conducting core eliminates magnetic losses at any RF frequency.","Only for aesthetic weight"],"newPrompt":"A switching-supply choke carries 0.8 A DC plus 500 kHz ripple. Two cores give similar low-current inductance, but one has much greater core loss at 500 kHz and saturates below 0.8 A. Which selection rule is sound?","newChoices":["Select whichever core has the larger initial permeability regardless of DC-bias saturation or AC core heating.","Evaluate the core material’s permeability, frequency-dependent loss and saturation at actual ripple frequency/current and temperature.","Select the core with the lowest measured DC winding resistance alone; copper resistance determines all magnetic-core losses.","Use the low-current inductance number as the only specification because effective inductance and losses remain fixed under bias."],"sourceModuleId":"M07","sourcePageId":"L07-0-05","reason":"Force comparison under frequency, bias and thermal conditions rather than generic ferrite keyword recognition."},{"id":"ALF52-B-059","expectedPrompt":"A DC motor reverses rotation when the polarity of its applied armature supply is reversed in a suitable configuration. Why?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Reversing current reverses torque direction relative to the field","Capacitance becomes infinite","The rotor's mass disappears","Electrical frequency doubles regardless of supply"],"newPrompt":"In a separately excited DC motor, the field winding current is held constant while armature current is reversed under an approved test setup. What happens to electromagnetic torque direction, and why does this not generalize to reversing both windings?","newChoices":["Torque reverses because armature current changes sign relative to fixed magnetic flux; reversing both field and armature can retain the original torque direction.","Torque always reverses whenever both field and armature supply leads are interchanged together, since both signs multiply to a negative.","Torque direction depends on the armature terminal voltage magnitude alone and not on flux or current direction.","Reversing current changes back EMF but cannot affect force direction in a fixed magnetic field."],"sourceModuleId":"M09","sourcePageId":"L07-0-01","reason":"Test conditional current/flux torque sign and the important separately excited versus series motor distinction; existing B-046 motor teaching supports prerequisite."},{"id":"ALF52-B-060","expectedPrompt":"A LED emits visible light when forward biased. What physical event produces photons?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["Permanent magnet field rotation only","Electron flow exclusively through a gas tube","Heating of a tungsten filament","Carrier recombination across semiconductor energy states"],"newPrompt":"In a forward-biased direct-bandgap LED, injected electrons and holes meet in the active region. Which process accounts for the emitted light, rather than ordinary resistor heating?","newChoices":["Electrons crossing a passive metal lead radiate a photon of energy equal to the supply voltage regardless of material.","The applied voltage mechanically vibrates a filament inside the semiconductor package until it glows.","Photon absorption across the junction generates charge carriers; this is the light-emitting direction of normal LED operation.","Radiative electron–hole recombination releases energy as photons, with emission spectrum influenced by semiconductor bandgap."],"sourceModuleId":"M11","sourcePageId":"L08-0-01","reason":"Replace impossible distractors with meaningful distinctions between radiative recombination, resistive heating and photodiode absorption; existing LED teaching suffices."},{"id":"ALF52-B-062","expectedPrompt":"A BGA circuit must be handled for rework. Which action supports damage prevention?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["Prying the cold package from pads","Approved ESD handling, moisture-sensitivity controls, profile and specialized inspection","Inspect BGA connections only from the package's upper surface and use an ordinary hot iron, since the hidden joints do not need reflow profiling.","Assuming all hidden joints are visible"],"newPrompt":"A moisture-sensitive BGA package has exceeded its allowed out-of-bag exposure, and its solder balls are hidden beneath the body. Which controlled preparation best supports qualified rework?","newChoices":["Apply the generic board reflow peak temperature immediately; room humidity and package exposure history matter only after soldering.","Check package MSL/exposure records and approved drying instructions, apply a qualified thermal profile with ESD controls, and use suitable hidden-joint inspection.","Use a thermal profile intended for a different package size if it melts the outer balls; hidden joints can be accepted by top-side appearance.","Bake every BGA at the highest available oven setting, then pry it free while the PCB is cold to shorten dwell time."],"sourceModuleId":"M15","sourcePageId":"L12-0-04","reason":"Add missing moisture-sensitivity and hidden-ball rework instruction, then require correct process sequence and qualified rather than universal baking."},{"id":"ALF52-B-064","expectedPrompt":"In a microprocessor, what part executes arithmetic and logical instructions?","expectedAnswerIndex":3,"expectedChoices":["The case fan motor","A passive isolation transformer","An analog headphone jack only","Arithmetic logic unit within the CPU core"],"newPrompt":"A CPU executes an instruction that adds two values held in registers and updates the zero/status flag. Which internal block primarily performs the arithmetic operation, as distinct from sequencing or data storage?","newChoices":["The program counter, which performs the addition while pointing to the next instruction.","The instruction decoder, which stores the operands and calculates the arithmetic result independently of execution hardware.","The data cache, which performs integer arithmetic while temporarily holding lines fetched from memory.","The arithmetic logic unit, which computes the result and associated condition flags under control of the processor."],"sourceModuleId":"M20","sourcePageId":"L14-0-06","reason":"Make wrong answers plausible CPU components (PC, decoder, cache), testing functions rather than word recognition. Existing CPU-block teaching sufficient."},{"id":"ALF52-B-065","expectedPrompt":"A technician crimping an RJ45 connector checks the tool and termination. Which sign indicates a properly completed crimp?","expectedAnswerIndex":2,"expectedChoices":["A firm latch and flat-looking contacts alone establish crimp quality even if the wire map shows a transposed pair and the jacket is not gripped.","The connector tabs are cracked but still latch","Contacts are fully seated with the correct conductor order, jacket retention and an electrically verified wire map","Pairs are untwisted for several inches and left exposed"],"newPrompt":"After terminating an 8-conductor copper network cable in a compatible modular plug, a technician sees fully seated contacts, but a wire-map tester reports pins 3 and 6 crossed at one end. What qualifies the termination for acceptance?","newChoices":["Accept the crimp if all eight contacts look flush because a visual check supersedes a failed wire map.","Accept the crimp after verifying the plug latches, because a crossed pair cannot affect an Ethernet electrical link.","Correct the conductor arrangement with an approved compatible termination, verify jacket strain retention and a passing wire map, then perform required performance testing.","Accept the cable after restoring shield continuity alone; shield measurements prove all eight conductors follow the intended pinout."],"sourceModuleId":"M22","sourcePageId":"L19-0-05","reason":"Turn cosmetically obvious quality question into tester-result interpretation, RJ45 pin order, wire map and strain retention decision."},{"id":"ALF52-B-066","expectedPrompt":"A media system turns on a powered speaker with a 12 V trigger signal. What must be verified?","expectedAnswerIndex":1,"expectedChoices":["Every RCA jack accepts 12 V triggers","Trigger voltage, current, connector polarity and device specifications","Any headphone output is an interchangeable trigger","The speaker impedance alone defines the trigger interface"],"newPrompt":"An alarm panel offers a dry-contact output, while a home-theater controller expects a powered 12 V trigger input to activate an alert scene. What must be checked before interfacing these systems?","newChoices":["Connect the dry contact directly and assume its closure generates a 12 V source without an external supply.","Verify both manufacturers’ alarm/control interfaces, trigger voltage/current and polarity, contact ratings and any required rated supply or isolated interface.","Feed the alarm panel audio output into the trigger input, because the same RCA-style connector guarantees a compatible control signal.","Use the speaker load-impedance value to determine the control voltage; no alarm output or trigger specification is needed."],"sourceModuleId":"M24","sourcePageId":"L19-0-01","reason":"Correct definite 17.5 competency misalignment by introducing alarm-to-entertainment interface; change realistic interface choices, retain verified 17.5 tag and original answer index."},{"id":"ALF52-B-071","expectedPrompt":"A schematic shows TP7 tied to ground near a regulator. Why include a test point?","expectedChoices":["Provide a known reference node for repeatable safe measurements","Guarantee fault-free power","Use a ground test point to set the maximum safe iron temperature, treating it as a calibration connection for the soldering station.","Set the maximum solder temperature"],"expectedAnswerIndex":0,"newPrompt":"On a low-voltage regulator schematic, TP7 is marked SIGNAL_RETURN and TP8 is marked 3V3_OUT. You must check its output using an approved instrument. Which plan best uses the test-point locations?","newChoices":["Verify TP7 is the specified return for this measurement, then measure TP8 relative to TP7 using an appropriate instrument and authorized safe procedure.","Measure TP8 relative to an arbitrary metal enclosure screw because every chassis is necessarily the same net as signal return.","Connect the scope ground clip to TP8 and its tip to TP7, because the reading is independent of probe reference and grounding arrangement.","Short TP7 to TP8 temporarily to make the regulator output easier to locate on the schematic."],"sourceModuleId":"M05","sourcePageId":"L13-0-04","reason":"Test diagram-labeled reference and output placement, not irrelevant solder controls; keep explicit instrument safety boundaries."},{"id":"ALF52-B-072","expectedPrompt":"An inductor and capacitor are in series with an AC source. At their ideal resonant frequency, what occurs?","expectedChoices":["Both elements become permanent open circuits at any frequency","The source frequency doubles automatically","At series resonance, both L and C become open circuits and eliminate current regardless of source impedance or the remaining series resistance.","Their reactive impedances cancel, leaving the resistive component to limit current"],"expectedAnswerIndex":3,"newPrompt":"A 10 V RMS sinusoidal source drives a SERIES RLC circuit with R = 20 ohms, L = 10 mH and C = 1 microfarad. Ignoring other losses, what is the source current magnitude near its resonant frequency?","newChoices":["Approximately 0.05 A, because the inductive and capacitive reactance magnitudes are added at resonance.","Approximately 0 A, because the inductor and capacitor are both open circuits at resonance.","Approximately 5 A, because the reactances cancel and resistance also disappears.","Approximately 0.50 A, because the net reactance cancels and 20 ohms of resistance remains."],"sourceModuleId":"M08","sourcePageId":"L06-0-03","reason":"Replace duplicate open-circuit distractors with a quantitative series RLC resonance calculation and an explicit R assumption."},{"id":"ALF52-B-073","expectedPrompt":"A free-running astable multivibrator differs from a triggered monostable circuit how?","expectedChoices":["Both are always identical unchanging DC sources","A monostable is the free-running oscillator that alternates indefinitely, while an astable waits for one trigger and then returns to one stable state.","Astable continuously alternates states; monostable produces a timed pulse after a trigger","Astable can never use capacitors"],"expectedAnswerIndex":2,"newPrompt":"A lab uses a crystal-controlled clock generator that runs continuously and a one-shot that emits one 5 ms pulse per valid trigger. Which statement best describes their relationship to oscillators and multivibrators?","newChoices":["Every multivibrator is necessarily a sinusoidal oscillator; the one-shot must free-run without a trigger.","The clock generator and triggered one-shot are both bistable multivibrators with two persistent output states.","The clock generator is an oscillator; a monostable multivibrator emits a timed pulse on triggering, while an astable multivibrator can itself be a free-running oscillator.","The triggered one-shot is the continuous timebase, while any astable circuit produces only one pulse and stops."],"sourceModuleId":"M10","sourcePageId":"L11-0-09","reason":"Match objective 4.6: oscillator class versus astable/monostable multivibrator relationships and waveform timing."},{"id":"ALF52-B-074","expectedPrompt":"A source follower FET stage has gain less than 1 but low output impedance. What practical use fits?","expectedChoices":["Providing infinite power without a supply","Buffering a high-impedance source into a lower-impedance load","Converting resistance into frequency by itself","Acting as a passive high-voltage fuse"],"expectedAnswerIndex":1,"newPrompt":"A biased small-signal MOSFET source follower has transconductance gm = 4 mS and an effective small-signal source load of 1 kilo-ohm. Neglecting channel-length modulation and body effect, what approximate voltage gain follows from Av = gmR/(1 + gmR)?","newChoices":["Av is about 4.0; source followers normally multiply voltage above their supplied gate swing.","Av is about 0.80; a 100 mV RMS input produces about 80 mV RMS output before headroom and clipping limits.","Av is about 0.20; the gmR term must be divided into unity without adding one to the denominator.","Av is exactly 1.00 at any gm and load because source followers have no finite loading."],"sourceModuleId":"M14","sourcePageId":"L10-0-04","reason":"Make official FET-gain analysis actual rather than buffer naming; use a stated approximate common-drain model and constraints."},{"id":"ALF52-B-076","expectedPrompt":"An existing disk volume reports damaged filesystem metadata. Which type of utility is designed to check and, where supported, repair its filesystem structures?","expectedChoices":["A disk-imaging utility that copies sectors into a backup image but does not itself check logical filesystem integrity","A file-archiving utility that compresses selected folders without validating disk allocation structures","A malware-scanning utility that inspects files for threats but does not check volume allocation metadata","Disk checking or repair utility used under an approved backup/recovery procedure"],"expectedAnswerIndex":3,"newPrompt":"A maintenance PC logs directory-allocation inconsistencies on a data volume, but SMART health reports no immediate drive fault. After preserving recoverable data, which utility is appropriate for checking logical filesystem metadata?","newChoices":["A disk-image backup utility, because copying the raw volume automatically proves every logical directory and allocation entry is valid.","A device-manager utility, because reinstalling a USB driver reconstructs damaged on-disk file allocation structures.","A malware-signature scanner, because a clean malware report guarantees the filesystem directory index is consistent.","An OS-compatible filesystem consistency checker (such as an approved fsck/chkdsk procedure), with recovery safeguards and documented results."],"sourceModuleId":"M21","sourcePageId":"L17-0-05","reason":"Realign filesystem consistency utility to official competency 16.5, not install/storage objective 16.4, and teach backup-first limits.","expectedObjectiveIds":["16.4"],"expectedFocusObjectiveId":"16.4","newObjectiveIds":["16.5"],"newFocusObjectiveId":"16.5"},{"id":"ALF52-B-077","expectedPrompt":"A frequency counter reports unstable digits on a low-level noisy input. What is a reasonable first check?","expectedChoices":["Assume the counter is reading resistance","Connect directly to mains to amplify the signal","Input amplitude, triggering sensitivity, coupling and gate time","Declare the oscillator defective without checking setup"],"expectedAnswerIndex":2,"newPrompt":"A 1.000 MHz signal generator is known-good on an oscilloscope, but a counter alternates between about 1.0 and 2.0 MHz when noise appears on one edge. What is the strongest first counter-specific check?","newChoices":["Lengthen the counter gate time only; this always removes false triggering caused by noisy edges.","Replace the generator timing crystal because the counter reading is sufficient proof of a 2 MHz generator output.","Check input amplitude, coupling and trigger threshold/hysteresis for multiple counted crossings, then select a suitable gate time and verify against the scope.","Switch to a shorter gate interval without investigating the counter input sensitivity or signal-to-noise ratio."],"sourceModuleId":"M23","sourcePageId":"L03-0-04","reason":"Test counter false edge triggering against gate-time precision with controlled noisy-signal evidence."},{"id":"ALF52-B-078","expectedPrompt":"An OLED display has image retention after many hours of showing a static logo. What is the likely concern?","expectedChoices":["A broken quartz oscillator in every case","Uneven pixel aging or burn-in","Magnetic tape demagnetization","Insufficient copper thickness in the cable"],"expectedAnswerIndex":1,"newPrompt":"An OLED kiosk has displayed the same bright white logo for months. Even after a power cycle, a faint outline remains when a uniform mid-gray test pattern is displayed. Which failure mechanism is most consistent?","newChoices":["Only a temporary interface framebuffer artifact; OLED emission materials do not age differently with usage.","Uneven aging of repeatedly illuminated emissive subpixels (persistent burn-in), distinguished from a reversible short-term image retention effect.","Failure of an LCD-style common backlight diffuser, which every OLED must use to light its pixels.","A video cable impedance mismatch that can produce a stable logo outline only on pixels previously lit for months, irrespective of new content."],"sourceModuleId":"M25","sourcePageId":"L19-0-09","reason":"Differentiate persistent OLED differential subpixel wear from short-term retention and input/backlight faults."},{"id":"ALF52-B-081","expectedPrompt":"A load of 4 ohms and 12 ohms is connected in parallel. What is the equivalent ideal resistance?","expectedChoices":["16 ohms","8 ohms","3 ohms","48 ohms"],"expectedAnswerIndex":2,"newPrompt":"A 10 V RMS AC source drives three PARALLEL branches: R = 100 ohms, an ideal inductor with XL = 100 ohms, and an ideal capacitor with |XC| = 50 ohms. What is the total source current magnitude and phase?","newChoices":["0.40 A in phase with voltage, because the magnitudes of all three branch currents add directly.","0.14 A lagging by about 45 degrees, because inductive current is necessarily larger than capacitive current.","0.14 A leading by about 45 degrees, because 0.10 A resistive and net 0.10 A capacitive branch currents combine as phasors.","0.10 A in phase with voltage, because inductive and capacitive branch currents always cancel regardless of their reactances."],"sourceModuleId":"M08","sourcePageId":"L04-0-02","reason":"Replace resistor-only parallel arithmetic under objective 4.4 with RLC branch-current and phasor reasoning; fix missing sourcePageId."},{"id":"ALF52-B-083","expectedPrompt":"An amplifier has gain 20 dB at 1 kHz but significantly lower gain at 100 kHz. What does this reveal?","expectedChoices":["Its frequency response is not flat over those frequencies","Its output power is always zero","It is a perfect all-pass network","Input impedance must be exactly one ohm"],"expectedAnswerIndex":0,"newPrompt":"With a 0.10 V RMS input at both test frequencies, an amplifier outputs 1.0 V RMS at 1 kHz and 0.20 V RMS at 100 kHz without clipping. Which interpretation best fits the measured small-signal gain response?","newChoices":["Its voltage gain falls from 10 (20 dB) to 2 (about 6 dB), showing frequency-dependent high-frequency roll-off under the stated load.","Its gain is 20 dB at both frequencies because the test input is unchanged, so any output difference must come from measurement noise.","Its gain rises from 2 to 10 with frequency; increasing frequency always increases gain when the output becomes smaller.","Its 100 kHz result proves slew-rate limiting without checking large-signal amplitude or the amplifier bandwidth and test setup."],"sourceModuleId":"M14","sourcePageId":"L10-0-01","reason":"Require voltage gain, decibel interpretation and separation of bandwidth roll-off from slew-rate and measurement error."},{"id":"ALF52-B-084","expectedPrompt":"Two ICs share an 8-bit parallel data bus. What must prevent simultaneous conflicting drive?","expectedChoices":["Enable both parallel output drivers at all times and rely on the combined data bits to resolve bus contention without tri-state control.","Omit ground reference on all chips","Use a speaker cable as a clock","Bus arbitration or tri-state/output-enable control so only the intended device drives lines"],"expectedAnswerIndex":3,"newPrompt":"Two memory devices share eight push-pull data lines to a controller. A timing trace shows that device A still drives bit D3 HIGH when device B begins driving D3 LOW. What should the controller change?","newChoices":["Assert both chip-select and output-enable signals through the transition to keep the data bus actively driven at all times.","Keep both outputs enabled but sample the bus a clock cycle later; waiting removes electrical driver contention.","Reverse D3 at one receiver so the processor sees matching logic, without changing the simultaneous output-drive conflict.","Enforce mutually exclusive output enables and a valid bus-turnaround interval so one push-pull driver releases D3 before the other drives it."],"sourceModuleId":"M17","sourcePageId":"L11-0-04","reason":"Assess ownership/turnaround timing rather than obvious bus versus speaker incompatibility; existing ETA 13.5 teaching already covers contention."},{"id":"ALF52-B-085","expectedPrompt":"A software system uses an anti-malware utility. What is a sound operational practice?","expectedChoices":["Run a one-time malware scan after installation, then leave definitions unchanged so that later alerts remain comparable to the first baseline.","Treat every unsigned file as confirmed malware automatically","Keep definitions and software current, follow organization policy, and verify detections before deleting critical files","Assume the tool replaces backups"],"expectedAnswerIndex":2,"newPrompt":"An updated endpoint-protection utility flags a signed instrument-control executable after a new detection-rule update. The computer also stores calibration results. What response best protects both the system and recoverability?","newChoices":["Disable future security updates permanently so this trusted executable cannot trigger another false alarm.","Delete the application and all calibration results at once, because a detection is conclusive proof that every associated file is malicious.","Follow the organization's response process, preserve recoverable data, verify the alert and file provenance with authorized security staff, then quarantine or remediate as directed.","Whitelist the whole installation folder without investigation, because a code signature proves that no signed executable can be malicious."],"sourceModuleId":"M21","sourcePageId":"L17-0-04","reason":"Align malware/virus protection to dedicated ETA 16.7, and evaluate signed-file false-positive triage and recovery safeguards.","expectedObjectiveIds":["16.5"],"expectedFocusObjectiveId":"16.5","newObjectiveIds":["16.7"],"newFocusObjectiveId":"16.7"},{"id":"ALF52-B-087","expectedPrompt":"A phototransistor receives increased illumination. What commonly changes?","expectedChoices":["Collector conduction for appropriate bias conditions","Copper trace width physically expands","It becomes a silicon-controlled rectifier","Supply frequency is forced to 50 Hz"],"expectedAnswerIndex":0,"newPrompt":"An NPN phototransistor has its emitter at signal return and its collector connected to +5 V through a 10 kilo-ohm pull-up. As illumination increases, its collector voltage falls from 4.8 V to 1.2 V. Which explanation fits this circuit?","newChoices":["Illumination increases collector conduction, raising the voltage drop across the pull-up and pulling the collector node lower.","Illumination lowers collector current; the greater pull-up voltage drop proves a higher collector-to-emitter resistance.","The phototransistor produces a higher collector voltage because every light sensor has voltage output proportional to illumination.","The pull-up resistor switches itself into a negative resistance mode; photocurrent cannot alter the output voltage."],"sourceModuleId":"M25","sourcePageId":"L19-0-10","reason":"Apply phototransistor light-current transduction to an actual biased load and inverted output voltage rather than unrelated distractors."},{"id":"ALF52-B-089","expectedPrompt":"What is the proper way to write a microampere quantity in SI notation?","expectedChoices":["Use mA for microampere by definition","Use µmA as standard double prefix","Use µA for microampere and avoid stacking prefixes","Use MA for microampere"],"expectedAnswerIndex":2,"newPrompt":"NIST SI guidance gives special spellings when certain prefixes attach to the unit name ohm. Which pair correctly writes the FULL unit names for 1 kΩ and 1 MΩ?","newChoices":["kiloohm and megaohm, keeping all prefix vowels even when followed by ohm.","kilOhm and megOhm, capitalizing the base unit name because its symbol is Ω.","kilohm and megohm, using the accepted contractions in the full unit names.","kiloom and megaom, removing the silent letter h from the electrical resistance unit."],"sourceModuleId":"M02","sourcePageId":"L04-0-03","reason":"Change SI symbol-recall item to directly sample the officially tagged NIST SP811 9.3 full-unit-name spelling exceptions, without remapping."},{"id":"ALF52-B-090","expectedPrompt":"A signal disappears between TP2 and TP3 in a functional chain. What is the next efficient step?","expectedChoices":["Replace the upstream board and downstream amplifier together without probing the missing signal or checking the intervening stage's power rails.","Probe the intervening stage's input/output and supplies using an approved tracing method","Short the test points together","Assume the missing signal proves no power"],"expectedAnswerIndex":1,"newPrompt":"A low-voltage audio stage receives a verified 1 kHz sine wave at its input. Its output test point is flat, and the amplifier ENABLE input measures LOW while the supply is valid. Which next diagnostic step best uses these observations?","newChoices":["Replace the upstream signal generator, because a flat downstream signal always proves its source is defective.","Trace the enable/control path against the documented truth table before condemning the amplifier or replacing the interconnect.","Replace the downstream loudspeaker immediately, because a disabled amplifier has already established a speaker fault.","Increase the signal generator voltage until the amplifier transmits despite its measured disabled state."],"sourceModuleId":"M05","sourcePageId":"L13-0-04","reason":"Assess a novel last-good/first-bad control-path diagnosis, avoiding the exact TP2/TP3 wording of the lesson example."},{"id":"ALF52-B-094","expectedPrompt":"A program must perform an operation only when a sensor exceeds a limit. What construct is appropriate?","expectedChoices":["An unconditional comment","Conditional branch such as if/else","Changing the PCB solder mask","A fixed resistor alone"],"expectedAnswerIndex":1,"newPrompt":"A simulation samples temperature T=102 °C and must assert ALARM only if T is STRICTLY GREATER than 100 °C, then clear ALARM otherwise. Which pseudocode meets the stated rule?","newChoices":["if T >= 100 then ALARM=1 else ALARM=0, which also triggers at exactly 100 °C.","if T > 100 then ALARM=1 else ALARM=0, which implements the strict threshold and clears the alarm below it.","if T < 100 then ALARM=1 else ALARM=0, which reverses the required comparison.","while T > 100: ALARM=1, with no else/reset path to clear the output once the condition becomes false."],"sourceModuleId":"M20","sourcePageId":"L14-0-03","reason":"Select and trace exact threshold semantics, not just a label if/else among unrelated physical components."},{"id":"ALF52-B-096","expectedPrompt":"A component in an AV system is powered but sends no signal to the next unit. Which troubleshooting order is useful?","expectedChoices":["Substitute the destination device before checking input selection or the signal cable, because an illuminated power light already proves source operation.","Cut protective earth to remove hum","Increase amplifier output until a fault disappears","Verify source output, cable/connector, destination input selection and settings systematically"],"expectedAnswerIndex":3,"newPrompt":"An audio source produces normal sound when connected directly to a known-good powered speaker. The same source reaches a mixer input meter, but there is no line-level signal at the mixer output and a downstream amplifier stays silent. What should be checked next?","newChoices":["Replace the audio source because the mixer power light indicates its internal audio paths must be operational.","Replace the downstream amplifier because a normal mixer input meter rules out output routing or muting problems.","Replace every connecting cable before checking the mixer output, because cable faults are always the most likely cause.","Check mixer bus routing, mute and output-level settings, then verify output at the mixer connector before moving downstream."],"sourceModuleId":"M24","sourcePageId":"L19-0-01","reason":"Use explicit source and receiver evidence to isolate an inter-equipment signal fault instead of selecting a generic checklist."},{"id":"ALF52-B-097","expectedPrompt":"An antenna is oriented vertically while a receive antenna is horizontal. What problem may occur?","expectedChoices":["Frequency is always doubled","The cable becomes an ideal transformer","Polarization mismatch can reduce received signal","No effect at any frequency or path"],"expectedAnswerIndex":2,"newPrompt":"In a controlled line-of-sight RF test using ideal linearly polarized antennas, the receiver is rotated 45 degrees from the transmitter's polarization with the distance and cable unchanged. About how much power reduction results from polarization mismatch alone?","newChoices":["About 6 dB, because rotating the antenna 45 degrees is equivalent to doubling link distance under every propagation condition.","Exactly zero loss, because antenna polarization affects only analog modulation and not received digital power.","About 3 dB, because received power scales approximately with cos²(45 degrees) = 0.5 in the idealized polarization-only model.","An unpredictable doubling of carrier frequency, because receive antenna rotation changes electromagnetic wave wavelength."],"sourceModuleId":"M26","sourcePageId":"L20-0-03","reason":"Replace verbatim lesson scenario with controlled 45-degree polarization-power reasoning and real competing link effects."},{"id":"ALF52-B-100","expectedPrompt":"A supply schematic adds a second series choke after a shunt filter capacitor. Which arrangement is characteristic of an L-C-L T-section filter?","expectedChoices":["A capacitor alone connected across the input","Two capacitors tied directly in series with no return path","Two inductors shorted across the output","Series choke, shunt capacitor to return, then another series choke"],"expectedAnswerIndex":3,"newPrompt":"A technician must place a two-port L-C-L low-pass T filter between a rectifier and a load. Which topology and nominal load connection identify the T section rather than an L section or C-L-C pi section?","newChoices":["Input -> series L1 -> series C -> load, with no capacitor connected to the common return node.","Input -> shunt C1 -> series L -> shunt C2 -> load, the C-L-C pi section.","Input -> series L -> shunt C -> load, with only one series choke, the L section.","Input -> series L1 -> midpoint shunt C to return -> series L2 -> load connected after L2."],"sourceModuleId":"M13","sourcePageId":"L08-0-00","reason":"Require two-port output-port placement and T vs L/pi differentiation; document ambiguous printed objective output wording."}];
  const ITEM_TEACHING = [{"id":"ALF52-A-030","module":"M03","page":"L01-0-02","reason":"Answer and distractors already sound; linked page lacks explicit Wh energy integration.","paragraphs":[["Electrical energy is accumulated power over time","Power is a rate (watts = joules per second), while electrical energy is the amount transferred during an interval. Under constant voltage and current, first find P = V × I. Then multiply by elapsed time: E = P × t. Using watts and hours gives watt-hours (Wh), not watts; using seconds gives joules, with 1 Wh = 3600 J. A higher instantaneous wattage is not the same as the total energy used over hours."],["Worked energy example with unit tracking","An ideal 24 V load draws steady 0.50 A: P = 24 V × 0.50 A = 12 W. During 3 h it consumes E = 12 W × 3 h = 36 Wh (0.036 kWh), equivalent to 129,600 J. Distinguish energy from component thermal rating: a 12 W load is not necessarily a 12 W resistor. Real energy accounting must include changing draw, efficiency and standby consumption when relevant."],["Independent energy check","Without copying the worked numbers: a 12 V load draws 0.25 A steadily for 4 h. Find power, Wh and joules before opening the answer. Result: P = 3 W; E = 3 W × 4 h = 12 Wh = 43,200 J. If you obtain 12 W, you have confused power and energy."]]},{"id":"ALF52-A-032","module":"M07","page":"L07-0-05","reason":"Existing page teaches inductance vs turns but not explicit magnetizing ampere-turns at fixed current.","paragraphs":[["Coil ampere-turns versus inductance","Magnetizing force of a simple winding depends on ampere-turns N × I, where N is turn count and I is current. For an ideal long solenoid, the magnetizing field strength is approximately H = NI/ℓ in A/m when length ℓ is in meters. This is different from inductance L, which describes flux linkage per current and can vary roughly with N² under fixed geometry. Increasing turns with a fixed-voltage supply also changes wire length/resistance and therefore current; do not silently assume constant current."],["Worked constant-current solenoid comparison","Two ideal air-core solenoids each have length 0.10 m and current 0.50 A. One has 100 turns: H ≈ 100 × 0.50 / 0.10 = 500 A/m. The other has 200 turns: H ≈ 1000 A/m. Field strength doubles in this ideal constant-current model. A ferromagnetic core would also introduce material response and saturation limits; winding heat remains a separate design constraint."],["Independent solenoid check","A 150-turn air-core winding is 0.30 m long and carries 0.20 A. Calculate ideal H. Then double current and state the new H. Answer: 150 × 0.20 / 0.30 = 100 A/m; doubling I gives 200 A/m, absent saturation and geometry change. Do not equate this doubling with an automatic doubling of inductance."]]},{"id":"ALF52-A-038","module":"M20","page":"L14-0-03","reason":"Explicit off-by-one and loop-trace prerequisites for competing code-form distractors.","paragraphs":[["Counted-loop execution is a traceable contract","A C-style for-loop has initialization; continuation condition; counter update. With for (int i=0; i<10; i++), the body executes at i=0,1,…,9: ten executions. The counter then becomes 10, fails i<10, and exits. An expression such as blink_once() must represent a complete on/off blink here; GPIO initialization, timing and current limiting are separate hardware requirements."],["Off-by-one worked comparison","For i=0; i<=10; i++, both 0 and 10 are included: 11 blinks. For i=1; i<10; i++, values 1 through 9 give 9. For i=0; i<10; i+=2, values 0,2,4,6,8 give 5. Check the start, stop comparison and step, not whether the code merely contains FOR."],["Try a new loop without looking","Predict the iterations of for (int k=2; k<12; k+=3) { blink_once(); }. Write every k value: 2,5,8,11, so there are four complete blinks. If your count is five, you probably treated the excluded upper bound as included."]]},{"id":"ALF52-A-047","module":"M10","page":"L11-0-06","reason":"Direct/converse piezoelectric effect and sensor reasoning missing in existing crystal oscillator explanation.","paragraphs":[["Direct and converse piezoelectric effects","In a piezoelectric material, mechanical stress changes electric polarization and produces charge at electrodes: this is the direct effect used by many vibration/force sensors. Conversely, an applied electric field can produce mechanical strain: the converse effect used by actuators and crystal excitation. A resistive strain gauge needs applied bias to read a stress-dependent resistance; a coil-and-magnet pickup uses electromagnetic induction. These are different physical transduction principles."],["Worked piezoelectric charge estimate","Treat a specified force-sensitive element as an ideal small-signal model with charge sensitivity 10 pC/N. If dynamic force changes by 20 N, generated charge magnitude is 200 pC. With an ideal effective capacitance of 100 pF, Q=CV predicts an illustrative open-circuit change of 200 pC/100 pF=2 V. In a real sensor, charge amplifier input, stray capacitance, material orientation, frequency response and leakage alter observable voltage; the coefficient is illustrative, not a universal quartz specification."],["Predict and distinguish direction","A sensor is tapped and produces a brief charge pulse with no external drive to actuate it. Is this direct or converse action? Direct: stress generates charge. If an alternating voltage is applied to a crystal to make it vibrate, that drive uses the converse effect; resonant electrical signals may then involve both directions. A steady force may not give a stable indefinitely-held voltage because charge leaks away."]]},{"id":"ALF52-A-048","module":"M12","page":"L08-0-05","reason":"Explicit β_DC ratio, α relationship and Q-point current example absent in assigned bias lesson.","paragraphs":[["Beta and alpha in a forward-active BJT","A BJT obeys IE=IC+IB for conventional current magnitudes at the terminals. At a chosen DC operating point, common-emitter current gain is β_DC=IC/IB; common-base gain α_DC=IC/IE. Thus α=β/(β+1) and β=α/(1−α), in the normal active-region idealization. β is not a fixed chip-wide constant: it varies with current, temperature, unit and operating region, and it differs from a small-signal incremental gain. Enhancement/depletion terminology belongs to field-effect devices, not BJT beta."],["Worked current-gain and conservation check","At the Q-point, measure IB=20 μA and IC=2.00 mA=2000 μA. β_DC=2000/20=100. IE=2020 μA=2.02 mA, so α_DC=2000/2020≈0.9901. Reverse division gives 0.01, which is not beta. For a loaded common-emitter stage, do not predict collector current as βIB without checking the supply, collector resistor and saturation limits."],["Independent BJT practice","At an active-region DC bias point, IB=40 μA and IC=4.0 mA. Calculate β and IE before looking: β=100 and IE=4.04 mA. Then α≈4.0/4.04≈0.990. If the load restricts collector current below the βIB prediction, the active-region assumption may fail and the transistor may be saturated."]]},{"id":"ALF52-A-055","module":"M27","page":"L19-0-03","reason":"Original page teaches channel media but not Ethernet PHY link negotiation; question must be teachable before assessment.","paragraphs":[["Ethernet link establishment and media compatibility","An Ethernet switch port and the attached network interface exchange electrical or optical link information through their physical layers (PHYs). A functioning cable/channel must match the relevant Ethernet medium, connector, pin/pair arrangement and rated distance/signal characteristics; DC continuity alone cannot establish transmission quality. Compatible port speeds, duplex behavior where applicable, transceivers and autonegotiation settings determine whether a valid link can form. Do not assume all forced speed/duplex choices are supported; gigabit copper uses its standard-specific negotiation requirements. A physical link indicator does not depend on host DNS or IP address configuration."],["Worked no-link investigation","A laptop and a switch have no link indicator after a patch lead is replaced. Step 1 verify both ports powered/enabled and read the model and supported speed/media. Step 2 reseat approved connectors and inspect the cable, pin map and category/application suitability. Step 3 compare advertised/negotiated port modes and faults in managed port status, then test with a known-good standards-compliant cable. Step 4 once link is established, only then troubleshoot VLAN, addressing or DNS as higher-layer causes of traffic failure. A wire-map pass rules out some opens/swaps but not all high-frequency loss or crosstalk problems."],["Independent decision","A switch port shows no physical link but a colleague proposes changing the workstation DNS resolver. Predict whether DNS can cause the link indicator to illuminate: not by itself, because name resolution runs above the PHY. Identify two tests that separate cabling from a disabled/incompatible port: substitute an approved known-good patch cable and inspect both endpoint port enable/media/speed status. Once link exists but packets still fail, Layer 2 VLAN and Layer 3 addressing become relevant."]]},{"id":"ALF52-A-059","module":"M08","page":"L04-0-05","reason":"Existing linked page emphasizes sinusoidal/pulsed inputs but lacks explicit first-order DC RC step charging model and numeric calculation.","paragraphs":[["DC step into series RC: first principles","For an initially uncharged ideal capacitor, capacitor voltage cannot change instantaneously without unbounded impulse current. In a practical series resistor step V, vC(0+)=0 and the full source voltage initially appears across R, so i(0+)=V/R. Charge accumulation raises vC, reducing resistor voltage and hence current. In the ideal linear first-order model i(t)=(V/R)exp(−t/RC), vC(t)=V(1−exp(−t/RC)); τ=RC sets the 1/e current decay and ~63.2% voltage rise at one time constant. At DC steady state, an ideal capacitor stops conducting current through its dielectric."],["Worked charging calculation","For 10.0 V applied to 2.00 kΩ and 100 µF in series, RC=(2000 Ω)(100×10^-6 F)=0.200 s. Initially i=10/2000=0.005 A=5.00 mA and vC=0. At t=τ=0.200 s, i≈5.00/e=1.84 mA and vC≈10(1−1/e)=6.32 V. Much later, i approaches zero and vC approaches 10 V. These values presume ideal parts, zero initial voltage and a step source; leakage, tolerances and ESR matter in real equipment."],["Independent transient check","Try a fresh 5 V step with R=1.0 kΩ and C=47 µF from zero charge. Calculate i(0+)=5.0 mA, τ=47 ms, and i(τ)≈1.84 mA. Explain why a test option claiming infinite steady current is wrong: the capacitor voltage reaches the supply, leaving almost no ideal resistor drop. Do not confuse the immediate DC step transient with sustained AC sinusoidal current or LC oscillation."]]},{"id":"ALF52-A-065","module":"M21","page":"L14-0-00","reason":"Assigned teaching contrasts RAM with SSD, but lacks the hardware compatibility workflow.","paragraphs":[["A-065 prerequisite model","Physical RAM compatibility is determined by the motherboard socket, memory generation, module form factor, memory-controller capabilities, firmware/BIOS compatibility, number of slots, supported density/capacity and any ECC/registered-module requirements. DDR4 and DDR5 are not plug-interchangeable despite both being described as RAM. DIMM and SO-DIMM form factors are not mechanically interchangeable. A higher speed rating is not a promise of full rated operating speed; a compatible memory system may negotiate a lower supported rate."],["A-065 worked example","Paper upgrade: a laptop service manual specifies two DDR4 SO-DIMM slots, 32 GB maximum total and a supported 2666 MT/s data rate. Two compatible 16 GB DDR4 SO-DIMMs fit the stated nominal capacity and form factor; a 32 GB DDR5 SO-DIMM does not, regardless of its speed. Verify exact part support, maximum per-slot capacity, firmware requirements and any model-specific exclusions before purchase. Capacity is 2 ×16=32 GB, not 64 GB."],["A-065 independent check","Try a motherboard specified for two non-ECC DDR4 desktop DIMMs and a 64 GB total limit. A 32 GB DDR4 SO-DIMM is rejected on form factor even though capacity fits; a pair of compatible 32 GB desktop DIMMs meets the arithmetic limit but still requires controller/firmware validation. Explain why storage filesystem format does not control physical RAM compatibility."]]},{"id":"ALF52-A-067","module":"M25","page":"L19-0-09","reason":"Assigned display lesson lacks explicit common-anode versus common-cathode polarity and segment current paths.","paragraphs":[["A-067 prerequisite model","Each seven-segment display segment is an LED with an anode and cathode; forward bias requires the anode at a higher potential than the cathode by enough to conduct current. In a common-ANODE package, all segment anodes are joined at a suitable positive rail; a selected segment lights when its cathode is pulled LOW through a specified current-limiting path. In a common-CATHODE package, segment cathodes share a low reference and a selected anode is driven HIGH through current limiting. An LED does not regulate its own current. Read the package pinout and current/peak-duty ratings; do not assume an MCU GPIO can safely sink the sum of all lit segments."],["A-067 worked example","Paper-only 5 V common-anode example: an LED segment has an assumed forward drop of 2.0 V and desired 10 mA. With its cathode driven close to 0 V through a series resistor, R ≈ (5−2)/0.010=300 Ω; a 330 Ω standard value yields about 9.1 mA, ignoring driver voltage drop. Resistor dissipation is approximately (3 V)²/330 Ω=27 mW. Choose actual limits from the datasheet and check driver sink-current rating."],["A-067 independent check","Predict two states for a common-anode segment held at +5 V: cathode at +5 V gives approximately zero forward voltage and OFF; a current-limited cathode near 0 V yields forward conduction and ON. Explain why simply reversing segment polarity is not a substitute for current limiting. In multiplexing, account for peak segment current and duty cycle."]]},{"id":"ALF52-A-069","module":"M11","page":"L08-0-03","reason":"A concrete comparison of graph slopes and V/I is useful for objective 9.7.","paragraphs":[["A-069 prerequisite model","Reading an I–V graph requires identified axes, units, polarity and operating region. Ohmic behavior means I is proportional to V across the tested range, producing an approximately constant ratio V/I and a straight line through the origin. A diode forward I–V curve is nonlinear: incremental resistance r_d≈ΔV/ΔI over a short segment can differ from the DC ratio V/I. Neither ratio is an unconditional fixed device resistance; temperature changes the curve."],["A-069 worked example","Suppose two graph points read (V=0.55 V,I=0.5 mA) and (V=0.65 V,I=5.0 mA). DC V/I falls from 0.55/0.0005=1100 Ω to 0.65/0.005=130 Ω. The incremental secant resistance is ΔV/ΔI=0.10/0.0045≈22.2 Ω. Distinguish the three quantities; the 22.2 Ω number characterizes this interval, not all forward voltages."],["A-069 independent check","Try two further forward points: 0.60 V at 2 mA and 0.70 V at 12 mA. The interval secant r≈0.10/0.010=10 Ω, while V/I changes from 300 Ω to about 58.3 Ω. Say what a straight-line resistor plot would have shown instead; explain why a data point without units cannot support the calculation."]]},{"id":"ALF52-A-070","module":"M22","page":"L19-0-03","reason":"Add geometry/voltage-drop prerequisite to copper-gauge lesson so a learner can reason beyond memorizing gauge names.","paragraphs":[["A-070 prerequisite model","DC conductor resistance follows R=ρL/A for uniform material at a fixed temperature: ρ is resistivity, L is one-way conductor length in the chosen path, and A is conducting cross-sectional area. Halving A at the same L and ρ doubles the conductor resistance. At equal current I, ΔV=IR and power dissipation I²R also double. For an outgoing-and-return circuit count both conductor lengths, connection/contact resistance and actual thermal limits; AWG identifies nominal diameter, not a guaranteed permissible ampacity in every installation."],["A-070 worked example","Paper comparison: copper run A has measured 0.40 Ω end-to-end conductor resistance. Identical run B at the same temperature and length has half the cross-sectional copper area, so idealized R_B=0.80 Ω. At 2.0 A, A drops 0.80 V and dissipates 1.6 W; B drops 1.6 V and dissipates 3.2 W. This is one conductor, not a two-conductor round trip; do not infer a safe field wire choice from this example."],["A-070 independent check","For a fixed conductor length and copper resistivity, suppose area becomes three times greater. Predict R becoming one-third, and at unchanged current both ΔV and I²R becoming one-third. Explain why changing the diameter is not identical to reducing length, and why electrical-code tables, insulation, terminals and temperature rating remain necessary for installation."]]},{"id":"ALF52-A-071","module":"M09","page":"L07-0-11","reason":"Existing transformer lesson emphasizes one primary/one secondary but does not explicitly contrast independent multi-secondaries with center taps and autotransformers.","paragraphs":[["A-071 prerequisite model","Separate, suitably insulated secondary windings can provide distinct secondary voltages and, where the construction and ratings support it, separate floating output returns. A single center-tapped secondary has a shared electrical midpoint: the two halves are not isolated from one another. A tapped autotransformer uses a common winding and cannot be treated as primary-to-secondary galvanically isolating. Two regulators sharing a source return may make different voltage rails but not independently isolated output circuits. Isolation classification depends on insulation system, creepage, clearance and manufacturer ratings, not a sketch of separate coils alone."],["A-071 worked example","Paper case: a rated transformer has primary P and two individually insulated secondary windings S1 (nominal 12 V AC) and S2 (nominal 6 V AC). Each winding voltage is specified across its own terminals. Under the stated independent insulation construction neither secondary requires a shared return. If the design instead uses a 12 V center-tapped single winding, both 6 V halves share the center tap, so they do NOT meet a requirement for galvanic isolation from each other."],["A-071 independent check","Choose between (a) a two-secondary isolation-rated transformer, (b) a center-tapped secondary, and (c) a tapped autotransformer for two independently floating outputs. Only (a) can meet the requirement if appropriately rated; state why a center tap shares a node and why an autotransformer is not isolating. Never energize an unidentified transformer to test this exercise."]]},{"id":"ALF52-A-074","module":"M15","page":"L12-0-00","reason":"Assigned ESD safety page does not explicitly explain flammable vapor ignition/fire controls before soldering.","paragraphs":[["A-074 prerequisite model","Soldering irons and nearby electrical equipment can be ignition sources around flammable solvent vapors. Flash point is the temperature at which a liquid emits sufficient vapor for ignition under specified test conditions; this is NOT the solvent boiling point. A closed bottle does not guarantee that previously released vapor has dispersed; a solder fume extractor is not automatically suitable for flammable solvents. Determine the actual chemical and hazards from the current SDS/site controls, remove solvents and vapor hazards from the hot-work zone, use required rated ventilation and authorization, and pause the job if safe conditions cannot be established."],["A-074 worked example","Paper scenario: a worker cleaned a board with a flammable product and immediately proposes soldering while the bench still smells of solvent. The correct sequence is STOP the hot task; check the SDS and site hot-work/chemical procedures; remove or isolate chemicals and contaminated wipes; establish required safe ventilation and verified conditions under responsible personnel direction; then proceed only if the controls authorize the operation. The odor alone is not a reliable quantitative vapor-concentration test."],["A-074 independent check","Compare three proposed decisions: (A) put on an ESD strap, (B) turn on ordinary bench solder fume extraction, (C) clear the work area, obtain chemical/safety clearance and use approved controls. Only (C) addresses the ignition hazard. Explain why no classroom solder or solvent demonstration is appropriate for this question."]]},{"id":"ALF52-A-081","module":"M05","page":"L13-0-00","reason":"Drawing types are explained but dimensional-unit notes, tolerance limits and conversions are not worked directly.","paragraphs":[["A-081 prerequisite model","An engineering drawing’s title block and general notes establish length units unless a dimension states otherwise. Convert the MEASUREMENT into the drawing unit before judging limits: 1 inch is exactly 25.4 mm. A dimension 25.4±0.2 mm specifies an acceptance interval 25.2 to 25.6 mm; it is not ±0.2 inches. Do not round a borderline reading before checking drawing precision or instrument uncertainty. For formal acceptance, use the specified measurement procedure and calibrated equipment."],["A-081 worked example","Paper case: nominal spacing 25.4±0.2 mm, measured 1.010 inches. Conversion 1.010×25.4=25.654 mm; the upper limit is 25.600 mm, so the nominal measured reading is outside tolerance by 0.054 mm. By contrast, 1.005 inches is 25.527 mm, nominally within tolerance. Instrument uncertainty matters if a result lies close to the boundary."],["A-081 independent check","Practice: a bracket drawing states 50.0±0.5 mm. A measurement is 2.000 inches. Convert to 50.8 mm and decide: above 50.5 mm, reject on the nominal reading. Explain why comparing 2.000 directly with 50.0 or treating ±0.5 as inches is wrong."]]},{"id":"ALF52-A-086","module":"M22","page":"L19-0-03","reason":"Existing AWG lesson distinguishes sizes but does not demonstrate resistance-per-length and two-conductor voltage-drop selection from a stated limit.","paragraphs":[["A-086 prerequisite model","For a low-voltage TWO-conductor DC feed, total loop conductor length normally includes outgoing and returning paths. If R_per_meter is provided, calculate R_loop = R_per_meter×L_loop; voltage drop ΔV=I×R_loop. For identical copper material/length, increasing conductor cross-sectional area reduces resistance. AWG numbering runs inversely: 16 AWG is thicker than 18 or 22 AWG. A voltage-drop paper calculation is only one gate: then verify acceptable ampacity, conductor/insulation temperature, terminals, environment and applicable code using reliable product data."],["A-086 worked example","Paper case: 4 A, 16 m round-trip loop, no more than 1.0 V drop. For 18 AWG at 0.021 Ω/m, R=0.336 Ω and ΔV=1.344 V (fails). For 16 AWG at 0.013 Ω/m, R=0.208 Ω and ΔV=0.832 V (passes). 14 AWG at 0.0083 Ω/m yields 0.531 V, but uses more copper; among the stated choices, 16 AWG is the thinnest that meets this SINGLE constraint. This is not sufficient for specifying real installation wiring."],["A-086 independent check","Practice: at 2 A and 12 m round-trip, compare 0.034 Ω/m and 0.021 Ω/m, with a 0.6 V limit. Drops are 2×12×0.034=0.816 V (fail) and 2×12×0.021=0.504 V (pass). State that this alone does not verify thermal ampacity, insulation and connectors."]]},{"id":"ALF52-A-087","module":"M24","page":"L19-0-08","reason":"Assigned audio-conflict lesson emphasizes ground loops and signal interfaces but lacks a first-principles model for reinforcing acoustic feedback.","paragraphs":[["A-087 prerequisite model","Acoustic feedback closes a loop: microphone → amplifier → loudspeaker → air → microphone. If the returning sound is reinforcing at some frequency and the loop has sufficient gain, the tone grows into a sustained howl/squeal, constrained by nonlinearities. It is frequency- and geometry-dependent, not the same as hiss (random broadband noise), clipping (rail-limited peaks) or power-line hum (often 50/60 Hz and harmonics). A phase-inverting electronic stage alone does not guarantee stability; phase around the FULL loop is what matters."],["A-087 worked example","Paper diagnosis: a PA whistles only with an open mic pointed toward a nearby speaker; muting that microphone stops the tone and changing the mic/speaker orientation changes the threshold. These clues favor acoustic feedback. Safer corrective actions in an authorized audio setup include adjusting placement, reducing excessive gain and using an appropriate directional mic; do not defeat protective earth to cure audio faults."],["A-087 independent check","Practice: compare (A) 60 Hz hum that persists with microphone muted, (B) broad hiss that tracks preamp gain, (C) a loud narrow tone that changes when mic faces speaker. Identify (C) as likely feedback and explain how signal loop geometry separates it from A and B."]]},{"id":"ALF52-A-091","module":"M08","page":"L06-0-03","reason":"Series phasor examples are present; ETA 4.7 also requires classification from the combined current in parallel RLC circuits.","paragraphs":[["A-091 prerequisite model","In ideal parallel R, L and C branches, the applied RMS voltage is shared. Resistor current is in phase with the voltage; ideal inductor current lags it 90 degrees and capacitor current leads 90 degrees. Treat leading and lagging reactive currents as opposite signed phasors: I_total = I_R + j(I_C-I_L) when the source voltage is the 0-degree reference. If I_L exceeds I_C, total current lags and the network is inductive; if I_C exceeds I_L, it leads and the network is capacitive; equality leaves an ideal net resistive current. Real losses shift exact phase."],["A-091 worked example","At the same frequency, I_R=2 A in phase, I_L=3 A lagging and I_C=1 A leading. The reactive sum is j(1-3)=-j2 A; I_total=2-j2 A. Magnitude is sqrt(2²+2²)≈2.83 A, phase about -45 degrees. Because current lags voltage, the overall network is inductive—not capacitive just because a capacitor is present."],["A-091 independent check","Practice: same circuit but I_C=4 A instead of 1 A. I_total=2+j(4-3)=2+j1 A, magnitude sqrt(5)≈2.24 A, current leads voltage by about 26.6 degrees: classify capacitive. With I_L=I_C, classify ideally resistive. Explain why 2+3+4 A is not a valid total-current addition."]]},{"id":"ALF52-A-093","module":"M17","page":"L11-0-05","reason":"The existing display-driver page teaches decoding and current limiting but lacks an explicit a–g glyph example for BCD 2.","paragraphs":[["A-093 prerequisite model","A seven-segment numeral has a horizontal top segment a, upper-right b, lower-right c, bottom d, lower-left e, upper-left f and middle g. In a conventional pattern, 0 lights a,b,c,d,e,f; 1 lights b,c; 2 lights a,b,d,e,g; 3 lights a,b,c,d,g. A BCD decoder maps a four-bit valid decimal code to the corresponding segment controls. On a common-cathode LED, a segment normally lights when current is properly sourced through it to the common cathode; a common-anode display uses opposite output drive polarity. Actual driver IC pinout and active levels must be checked."],["A-093 worked example","BCD 0010 equals decimal 2, so illuminate a,b,d,e,g while c and f remain dark. If the display shows 3 instead, c may be on while e is off; this is a plausible decoding/driver fault. Current-limiting resistors or a specified constant-current driver are still required, and multiplex timing must be valid if there are multiple digits."],["A-093 independent check","Independent check: for BCD 0001, illuminate b and c for digit 1. For BCD 0011, illuminate a,b,c,d,g for digit 3. Explain why a binary code alone is not a safe direct connection to the segments without proper driver polarity and current limitation."]]},{"id":"ALF52-A-095","module":"M25","page":"L19-0-10","reason":"Existing lesson mentions light-generated charge and bandgap but not the complete carrier-separation-to-load-current causal chain for a photovoltaic p–n junction.","paragraphs":[["A-095 prerequisite model","For an ordinary photovoltaic p–n junction, photons with sufficient energy create electron–hole pairs in or near the absorber. The built-in junction field helps separate carriers near the depletion region; minority-carrier diffusion can also bring generated charge to the junction. Collection at contacts establishes a photovoltage; with an external load, electrons travel through the external circuit and deliver electrical power. The illumination supplies the energy. This differs from a photoresistor, which generally needs an external source to measure light-dependent resistance, or a thermocouple, whose Seebeck voltage depends on temperature differences."],["A-095 worked example","Illustrative paper case: at a chosen operating point a cell delivers 0.50 V and 0.20 A into an appropriate load; its electrical output is P=VI=0.10 W. If reduced illumination lowers available photocurrent, the same load generally gets less power, with the exact voltage/current determined by the cell I–V curve. Open-circuit voltage alone does not imply output power: with I=0, delivered P is zero. Solar cell conversion is not a battery silently replenished by light."],["A-095 independent check","Practice: explain why an illuminated cell at open circuit can have voltage but deliver zero power to an external load; then compute output for 0.45 V and 0.10 A at a stated load (0.045 W). Contrast photon generation and carrier collection with simple heat-induced resistance change."]]},{"id":"ALF52-A-100","module":"M09","page":"L07-0-02","reason":"Existing relay page identifies types and coil-driver flyback but does not explicitly separate coil-side driver protection from suppression across a different switched DC inductive load.","paragraphs":[["A-100 prerequisite model","A relay has at least two functional circuits: the actuator coil that moves contacts, and a load circuit through the contacts. When contacts open on an inductive DC load (for example a solenoid), the load inductance resists sudden current decay and can drive a contact-voltage transient and arc. Check the relay datasheet for DC voltage, current and inductive-load breaking/life rating: a high AC resistive rating is not automatically a safe DC inductive rating. Approved load-side suppression (diode, TVS or RC device as appropriate) can reduce voltage transients but can change load de-energization time; a coil-driver flyback diode is a DIFFERENT component across the relay actuator coil. Review safety and release-time specifications rather than adding devices ad hoc."],["A-100 worked example","Paper example: a controller operates a relay with a 12 V actuator coil, but the contacts open a separate 24 V DC solenoid. A diode placed across the 12 V actuator coil protects its transistor; it does not necessarily suppress the 24 V SOLENOID when the contacts open. The correct design separately verifies the normally-open contact DC inductive ratings and the solenoid-load suppression path. An AC triac SSR may fail to turn off a DC load after triggering and must not be substituted without explicit DC switching ratings."],["A-100 independent check","Practice: a contact is rated 5 A at 250 VAC resistive but only 0.5 A at 24 VDC inductive. Would you approve a 1 A 24 VDC solenoid based on the 5 A figure? No; it exceeds the stated inductive DC rating. Name where actuator-coil and load-side suppression devices would each connect, and explain why a slower release may be undesirable for some controls."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-007","module":"M12","page":"L08-0-05","reason":"B-007 has a specific common-emitter AC-versus-DC bypass component mechanism absent from the original assigned generic op-amp feedback page.","paragraphs":[["B-007 prerequisite model","In a typical NPN common-emitter stage, the emitter resistor R_E creates DC negative feedback: as emitter current rises, emitter voltage rises and reduces forward base-emitter drive, helping stabilize the Q-point against transistor variation and temperature. Unbypassed R_E also creates AC emitter degeneration, reducing small-signal voltage gain but often improving linearity. A bypass capacitor C_E placed in parallel has reactance magnitude X_C=1/(2πfC). At DC (f=0) the ideal capacitor is open, so R_E remains for bias; as frequency rises, its impedance decreases and it diverts part of the AC emitter current, reducing degeneration. Actual gain/frequency response also depends on source impedance, transistor small-signal emitter resistance, load and other capacitors."],["B-007 worked example","Worked frequency comparison: R_E=1.0 kΩ, C_E=100 µF. At 10 Hz, X_C≈159 Ω; at 1 kHz, X_C≈1.59 Ω. The bypass impedance is much smaller at 1 kHz, so emitter degeneration is reduced much more strongly there, assuming surrounding impedances and component ratings support the approximation. Neither calculation says C_E shorts the resistor for DC: an ideal capacitor passes no steady DC current. Bypassing may increase distortion as feedback falls; it is not automatically a free gain improvement."],["B-007 independent check","Independent check: for R_E=1 kΩ and C_E=10 µF, estimate X_C at 10 Hz and 1 kHz: about 1592 Ω and 15.9 Ω, respectively. Which frequency provides stronger bypass action, and is DC stabilization eliminated? Answer: 1 kHz provides much stronger bypass; steady-DC stabilization remains through R_E. Explain why physically removing R_E would not be equivalent to adding C_E."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-012","module":"M24","page":"L19-0-07","reason":"B-012 directly requires the Nyquist frequency and aliasing mechanism absent from the assigned original recording lesson.","paragraphs":[["B-012 prerequisite model","Digital audio sampling measures a suitably conditioned analog waveform at discrete times. For uniform sample rate f_s, the Nyquist frequency is f_N=f_s/2. A real-valued band-limited waveform must be restricted to below f_N to avoid overlapping spectral replicas; an analog anti-alias filter attenuates unwanted higher-frequency content before the ADC. A frequency above f_N does not simply get recorded correctly: periodic sampling cannot distinguish it from certain lower-frequency aliases. In a simple single-tone case, locate the observed baseband frequency by subtracting integer multiples of f_s and reflecting to the interval 0 to f_s/2. Anti-alias filters require a usable transition band and do not provide infinite rejection exactly at the boundary."],["B-012 worked example","Worked audio case: f_s=48 ksample/s, so f_N=48/2=24 kHz. An unwanted 30 kHz tone is 6 kHz above Nyquist, but its primary alias is |48−30|=18 kHz, not 6 kHz. A digital recorder would therefore contain misleading 18 kHz energy if the analog input filter had not adequately rejected 30 kHz. The per-sample sequence at 30 kHz produces the same frequency content as an 18 kHz tone with an appropriate phase, even though the original analog input was ultrasonic."],["B-012 independent check","Independent transfer: at f_s=44.1 ksample/s, what is f_N and where would an inadequately filtered 30 kHz tone alias? Results: f_N=22.05 kHz and alias |44.1−30|=14.1 kHz. Then check a 10 kHz input at the same rate: it is below Nyquist and does not fold in the ideal band-limited model. State why selecting a high digital sample rate does not remove the need for analog input filtering."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-015","module":"M01","page":"L01-0-06","reason":"B-015 originally referenced a pneumatic lockout page that did not teach 29 CFR 1910.242(b) cleaning rules or PCB-specific cleaning safeguards.","paragraphs":[["B-015 prerequisite model","Under U.S. general-industry 29 CFR 1910.242(b), compressed air used for cleaning must be reduced to LESS THAN 30 psi and used with effective chip guarding and personal protective equipment. OSHA guidance explains pressure at the cleaning nozzle/opening under applicable static, including dead-ended, conditions; check approved device and applicable site procedures rather than assuming a regulator dial alone proves compliance. Effective barriers must protect other exposed workers as well as the operator. A site may prohibit compressed air or require lower limits, local exhaust, approved ESD-safe vacuum or cleaning agents. For electronics, also evaluate ESD charge, moisture, contaminants and aerosol/propellant compatibility. Deenergizing/isolation and stored-energy controls are separate requirements; reduced pressure is not permission to clean exposed live hazardous-voltage equipment."],["B-015 worked example","Worked choice: a dusty deenergized assembly has an approved blow-off procedure. Setup A uses a certified reduced-pressure nozzle limited to 25 psi even when blocked, effective shielding from flying debris, required PPE, and an approved ESD-safe method. Setup B uses an 80 psi shop line and goggles but no debris shield. Only A can satisfy the stated compressed-air constraints, subject to actual approved equipment and contamination controls. A 30 psi exact limit also fails the literal below-30 requirement. Source: OSHA 29 CFR 1910.242(b) and OSHA directive STD 01-13-001; site interpretation takes precedence where stricter."],["B-015 independent check","Independent practice: at the same bench, a nozzle stays below 30 psi but the operator omits chip guarding and PPE. Is the OSHA compressed-air cleaning condition satisfied? No: all three conditions are required. Next, a protected nozzle is used on a board containing ESD-sensitive devices with no approved ESD method. Is federal pressure compliance sufficient for board protection? No: ESD/equipment controls must be evaluated separately. In both cases choose the site-approved safer alternative rather than improvising."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-018","module":"M07","page":"L07-0-04","reason":"Original capacitor-family page does not explicitly teach a local IC bypass capacitor and its transient charge and high-frequency impedance mechanism.","paragraphs":[["B-018 prerequisite model","A capacitor connected in parallel between an IC supply pin and local return behaves as a nearby charge reservoir and offers frequency-dependent bypass of noise. From Q=C V, a short transient with charge change ΔQ≈I Δt gives a first estimate ΔV≈IΔt/C before regulator response. In an ideal capacitor, |X_C|=1/(2πfC), decreasing with frequency; real capacitors have equivalent series resistance (ESR) and lead/trace inductance (ESL). Thus a large nominal capacitance far away may fail at fast edges: the supply-return loop and local placement matter. A bypass capacitor is not a voltage regulator, not a steady DC source and not wired in series with the IC supply."],["B-018 worked example","Worked: a 3.3 V IC draws an added 0.10 A for 1.0 µs before upstream current changes. An ideal local 10 µF capacitor would droop approximately ΔV=0.10×1e−6/(10×1e−6)=0.010 V, or 10 mV. This estimate ignores effective ceramic capacitance under DC bias, trace inductance, ESR and other loads; fast edge spikes can be larger. A 10 nF device has 1000 times smaller capacitance and would droop about 10 V under the same unrealistic isolated-load assumption, showing why charge storage must be evaluated rather than relying on a tiny HF capacitor alone."],["B-018 independent check","Independent check: using a 4.7 µF effective capacitor and an added 47 mA for 2 µs, compute approximate droop: ΔV=(0.047×2e−6)/(4.7e−6)=0.020 V or 20 mV. Explain why a capacitor on a distant power connector can have poorer fast-transient performance despite matching the capacitance value. Answer: current-loop trace/via inductance and resistance hinder fast response; place suitable capacitors according to IC manufacturer layout guidance."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-026","module":"M23","page":"L03-0-08","reason":"ESR tester competency 8.9 requires treating meter readings as frequency-dependent series losses, not just generic probe loading or nominal capacitance.","paragraphs":[["B-026 prerequisite model","A real capacitor can be represented as ideal capacitance C with series resistance ESR and parasitic inductance ESL. In AC ripple service, series-resistive heating is approximated by P_ESR=I_ripple,rms²×ESR at the *relevant* frequency and temperature. A capacitance meter estimates reactive charge storage; it can read nominal C even for a degraded part with excessive ESR. An ESR meter commonly injects a small AC signal and infers the resistive component of impedance at a selected frequency. Its value is not directly a DC leakage, dielectric withstand or ripple-current rating test. Follow instrument instructions, discharge/isolate appropriately and compare to the part datasheet under specified frequency, temperature and measurement conditions."],["B-026 worked example","Worked: a capacitor reads 100 µF and ESR=2.0 Ω at a test frequency. At 0.50 A RMS ripple, if the 2.0 Ω ESR approximation holds at the operating frequency, heat=0.50²×2.0=0.50 W. A healthy replacement measuring 0.20 Ω under the same test conditions would dissipate about 0.05 W at that ripple; component construction, cooling and ratings still matter. The 10:1 ESR reduction produces a 10:1 heat reduction at fixed current, not a claim of proportional lifetime or universal replacement suitability."],["B-026 independent check","Independent check: a filtered DC rail has 0.30 A RMS ripple current and the candidate capacitor ESR is 1.5 Ω under relevant conditions. Estimate I²R=0.30²×1.5=0.135 W. Would a normal capacitance reading rule out ESR overheating? No. Would an ESR tester alone prove low DC leakage? No. Explain what operating frequency, temperature, component type and meter model must be checked before interpreting the figure."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-028","module":"M27","page":"L20-0-08","reason":"Assessment 20.2 explicitly mentions an applicable ANSI/TIA standard, while initial WLAN interference item never names a standards family or distinguishes radio from cabling.","paragraphs":[["B-028 prerequisite model","A Wi-Fi terminal link is a radio interface governed by the applicable IEEE 802.11 family of wireless LAN MAC/PHY specifications (IEEE 802.11-2024 is a published revision). The fixed communications-cabling infrastructure feeding an access point is a different interface: the ANSI/TIA-568 family describes structured telecommunications cabling and related performance/installation requirements, including wireless-access-point cabling provisions in premises standards. These are complementary, not interchangeable: TIA-568 does not prescribe the Wi-Fi channel-access protocol, and IEEE 802.11 does not certify structured twisted-pair cabling. A site might have additional ANSI/TIA requirements for pathways, grounding, administration or infrastructure depending on the actual task. Verify the currently adopted and applicable standard version rather than attributing every wireless failure to a generic TIA document. Sources: https://standards.ieee.org/ieee/802.11/10548/ ; https://tiaonline.org/standardannouncement/tia-issues-new-telecommunications-cabling-guidelines-for-wireless-access-points-standard/ ."],["B-028 worked example","Worked layer test: handheld clients lose Wi-Fi near a large motor but remain connected to the plant network elsewhere. First check signal quality, noise/channel occupancy, antenna orientation and link retries close to the motor with approved diagnostic tools. Next check whether the access point Ethernet uplink also logs errors; if so inspect connectors, patching and cable compliance against the appropriate ANSI/TIA-568 cabling requirements. A Wi-Fi RF fault invokes IEEE 802.11 behavior and radio engineering; an uplink copper-cabling fault invokes physical Ethernet and structured-cabling requirements. A successful application ping by itself does not settle which layer intermittently failed."],["B-028 independent check","Independent transfer: a new access point has good RF signal and association but poor throughput from a network closet after a cable replacement. Which interface should be measured next and which standards family concerns its structured physical cabling? Test the wired uplink, including cable certification and terminations, using site-adopted ANSI/TIA-568 references. If an adjacent microwave source instead causes retransmissions on the air link, identify the IEEE 802.11 RF/WLAN interface and conduct a channel/interference survey. Do not interchange the two standards families."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-032","module":"M08","page":"L06-0-02","reason":"The existing first-order filter section explains transfer/cutoff under ideal loading but does not work a noisy rail filter with competing attenuation and DC delivery constraints.","paragraphs":[["B-032 prerequisite model","For a rail RC low-pass, a series impedance and a shunt capacitor can reduce high-frequency voltage noise at the load node, assuming the source, load, impedance and frequencies permit. For an ideal series R/shunt C (light load), f_c≈1/(2πRC), and capacitive reactance decreases as frequency increases. But a real DC load draws current through the series resistance, creating V_drop=I_loadR and resistive heat P=I_load²R. A large resistor may filter ripple yet starve the load. Ferrite beads or suitable inductors may have less DC resistance but have frequency-dependent impedance, saturation/current and resonance considerations; they must not be inserted blindly. Source impedance, bypass ESR/ESL, capacitor DC bias and load-transient stability require actual verification."],["B-032 worked example","Worked: 10 Ω followed by 10 µF yields unloaded RC cutoff f_c≈1/(2π×10×10e−6)=1592 Hz, below 100 kHz ripple. But at a steady 0.20 A load, V_drop=0.20×10=2 V, leaving only about 3 V from an ideal 5 V input; series resistor heating is I²R=0.20²×10=0.40 W. The nominal frequency attenuation does not justify the excessive DC drop. Select filtering with acceptable supply regulation, ripple attenuation, thermal limits and dynamic response; verify on a safe, low-voltage design."],["B-032 independent check","Independent check: a 3.3 V rail drives 100 mA through an optional 2 Ω series filter resistor and 4.7 µF capacitor. Compute DC drop=0.10×2=0.20 V and R heat=0.10²×2=0.020 W, then determine whether a 3.1 V delivered rail meets the IC minimum. That cannot be answered without the IC datasheet. Why is simply increasing R to 20 Ω not automatically better? It drops 2.0 V at 100 mA and may cause undervoltage during transients."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-037","module":"M19","page":"L18-0-03","reason":"The grounding lesson distinguishes references but does not directly explain line-frequency audio hum caused by multiple earth/signal returns.","paragraphs":[["B-037 prerequisite model","Protective earth is a shock-protection conductor; chassis/signal reference is chosen for circuit function. In an interconnected pair of instruments, earth conductors and an interconnecting cable shield can form parallel conductive return paths. A small mains-frequency leakage or magnetic-field-induced circulating current through finite wire/shield impedance produces ΔV=I_loop×Z_return, which can be added to sensitive single-ended audio. This is a ground loop, not proof that a protective-earth conductor is defective. Never remove a protective earth pin or defeat safety bonding to silence noise; investigate approved balanced/differential links, manufacturer-rated signal isolation and proper bonding under qualified procedures."],["B-037 worked example","Worked example: a 60 Hz return-loop current of 20 mA flows through a shared shield/return resistance of 0.5 Ω. Its estimated unwanted drop is 0.020×0.5=0.010 V (10 mV). Across a 5 mV microphone-level signal, 10 mV is substantial. By contrast, reducing a cable’s high-frequency bandwidth can attenuate treble but does not alone identify a 60 Hz loop. Diagnosis: observe whether hum changes with an approved differential input or correctly rated isolation interface while preserving safety earth."],["B-037 independent check","Independent check: two safety-grounded low-voltage devices share a cable and show 50 Hz hum in a 50 Hz supply region. If return-loop impedance doubles while circulating current stays constant, what happens to error voltage? Answer: ΔV=I_loop×Z doubles. Name one safe mitigation—balanced reception or approved galvanic signal isolation—and explain why lifting the mains earth conductor is not a permissible troubleshooting shortcut."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-039","module":"M23","page":"L03-0-06","reason":"ETA 8.13 asks operation: the existing page briefly names analyzer controls but does not give a full measurement sequence and display-interpretation check.","paragraphs":[["B-039 prerequisite model","A spectrum analyzer displays measured amplitude or power on a vertical scale against frequency on a horizontal axis, for a selected span. Set center/start/stop frequencies to include desired fundamental and suspected spur; choose reference level and attenuation so the instrument input is not overloaded; choose resolution bandwidth (RBW) sufficient to distinguish nearby peaks. A too-narrow span can hide a harmonic, while too-wide RBW can combine nearby signals. Instruments may create internal harmonics when overloaded; compare with a safe attenuation change and suitable calibration before blaming the circuit under test. This is a frequency-domain measurement, unlike the oscilloscope’s ordinary voltage-versus-time trace."],["B-039 worked example","Worked: an oscillator should have a fundamental at 100 MHz and a second harmonic at 200 MHz. A span from 80 to 220 MHz displays both, while a 99.995–100.005 MHz window cannot show the second harmonic. If the 100 MHz peak is −10 dBm and the 200 MHz peak is −40 dBm on a properly configured analyzer, the harmonic is 30 dB below the fundamental. The ratio is a level comparison, not an assertion about the analyzer input maximum or the oscillator’s peak-to-peak time waveform."],["B-039 independent check","Independent check: an analyzer center is 100 MHz with 20 MHz total span. Can it show a 150 MHz spur? No; a 90–110 MHz sweep excludes it. Name two controls to change before claiming no spur exists: expand/recenter the frequency range and set safe attenuation/reference level; adjust RBW to separate peaks. If a peak unexpectedly increases relative to the DUT when input attenuation is changed, consider instrument-generated distortion and verify using instrument procedure."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-040","module":"M25","page":"L19-0-09","reason":"The existing OLED-vs-LCD description omits aging and burn-in, a concept used explicitly in the keyed answer.","paragraphs":[["B-040 prerequisite model","A transmissive LCD controls how much of an external light source/backlight reaches the viewer, whereas an OLED pixel generates its own visible light by electroluminescence. Switching off an OLED pixel generally enables very low luminance at black; LCD blacks can be limited by light leakage in the panel/backlight arrangement. OLED emission materials age with use, and regions showing bright static graphics for prolonged periods can lose luminance at different rates. Persistent nonuniformity or burn-in is not guaranteed for every use pattern but is a real lifetime consideration. Image retention from some transient causes differs from irreversible differential aging; use the manufacturer’s specified mitigation features."],["B-040 worked example","Worked: a machine HMI shows a bright white alarm icon continuously at the upper right while the rest of the display varies. Under otherwise similar operation, the continuously illuminated pixel region has greater cumulative emissive duty than the variable background. An OLED may produce better dark-scene contrast than a conventional backlit LCD, but the fixed icon region may age differently. A technician should evaluate content duty cycle, ambient brightness, operating temperature, vendor lifetime ratings and any approved pixel-shifting or dimming features before selecting a panel."],["B-040 independent check","Independent check: a display uses mostly black UI screens with a small high-brightness status bar always on. Which portion of the OLED has the highest risk of differential wear? The static bright bar, other factors equal. Would the same explanation apply to standard LCD backlight brightness variation? Not in the same pixel-emitter mechanism, because LCD pixels modulate a separate light source. Explain one operational mitigation compatible with the vendor instructions, such as reducing persistent static brightness."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-041","module":"M27","page":"L20-0-08","reason":"The satellite lesson mentions distance and line-of-sight without developing pointing, polarization, frequency-dependent loss, rain fade or margin.","paragraphs":[["B-041 prerequisite model","A satellite RF link is a budget of transmitted power, antenna gains, propagation losses, receiver noise/sensitivity and additional implementation or weather margins. A directional dish must be pointed at the applicable satellite track and maintain a suitable unobstructed view. Polarization mismatch can reduce coupled signal even with strong transmit power. For otherwise equal antenna gains and geometry, the free-space loss term grows by about 6 dB when frequency doubles, and by about 6 dB when path length doubles: FSPL(dB)=32.44+20log10(d_km)+20log10(f_MHz). Atmospheric attenuation, rain, frequency plan and antenna pattern can add significant losses. This ideal FSPL relationship does not by itself predict total performance when real antenna gain or atmospheric effects change."],["B-041 worked example","Worked: compare hypothetical line-of-sight links with identical antenna gains, path length 1000 km and frequencies 2 GHz versus 4 GHz. The frequency term changes by 20log10(4000/2000)=20log10(2)≈6.02 dB, so the higher-frequency link has about 6 dB more ideal free-space loss under these fixed-gain assumptions. The actual link also depends on antenna gain, noise, EIRP, polarization and weather. If a dish is mispointed, raising output power does not reliably restore a correctly aligned receive path."],["B-041 independent check","Independent check: a link budget has 8 dB of clear-weather margin, and heavy rain adds 10 dB attenuation, with other conditions fixed. The margin becomes −2 dB and the link may fail. Distinguish this from a 90-degree linear-polarization mismatch, which is a separate coupling problem. Before retuning power, what should the technician verify? Pointing, obstructions, polarization, band, connectors, receiver status and authorized link-budget procedure."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-045","module":"M07","page":"L07-0-04","reason":"The assigned capacitor-types page covers voltage and temperature but lacks a worked ripple-current selection check used by the question.","paragraphs":[["B-045 prerequisite model","When replacing a polarized electrolytic capacitor, first read actual circuit requirements and the manufacturer datasheets: capacitance/tolerance, maximum working voltage plus justified margin, polarity, temperature rating, ripple-current RMS rating at specified frequency/temperature, ESR, lifetime and surge/inrush requirements. Ripple current causes series-resistive heating approximately I_rms²×ESR, so an acceptable µF reading alone is insufficient. Higher voltage rating does not excuse inadequate ripple capability, and a lower temperature class may be unsuitable even when capacitance matches. Physical dimensions, terminal layout, vent clearance and any special low-ESR stability requirements remain separate compatibility checks."],["B-045 worked example","Worked: a supply reaches 28 V and calls for 1000 µF/35 V minimum, 105 °C and at least 0.8 A RMS ripple. Candidate A has 25 V and 1.2 A ripple and fails voltage rating; candidate B 35 V, 85 °C and 1.2 A fails specified temperature; candidate C 50 V, 105 °C and 1.0 A meets these minimums if ESR, fit and other limits also match. A 0.4 A ripple candidate cannot be rescued merely by higher voltage rating. As an idealized comparison, 0.8²×0.2 Ω=0.128 W internal ripple heating at an ESR of 0.2 Ω under stated operating conditions."],["B-045 independent check","Independent check: a required 470 µF rail capacitor calls for ≥25 V rating, ≥0.3 A ripple RMS and 105 °C. Why is 470 µF/50 V/85 °C/0.6 A not automatically acceptable? It fails the specified temperature class. Why is 470 µF/35 V/105 °C/0.2 A not automatically acceptable? It fails ripple current. State two additional specs to compare before approving a replacement: ESR/frequency dependence, lifetime, size, lead spacing, surge current and vendor requirements are valid."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-046","module":"M09","page":"L07-0-01","reason":"The motor/generator lesson mentions magnetic force but lacks a worked conductor-direction example supporting the specific torque mechanism.","paragraphs":[["B-046 prerequisite model","A conductor carrying conventional current I in magnetic flux density B experiences a magnetic force approximately F=B I L sinθ for a straight effective active length L at angle θ to the field. Opposite forces on appropriate sides of a rotor make a couple and cause torque about the shaft. The magnetic field can come from permanent magnets or energized windings. Reverse current direction while keeping field direction fixed and the force/torque sign reverses; reverse both current and field and the original torque direction may be retained. Actual motors use commutators or electronically sequenced phases to keep producing appropriate rotating torque. Mechanical load, winding resistance, back EMF and controller current limits affect operating speed and torque."],["B-046 worked example","Worked: one active 0.05 m straight conductor carries 2 A perpendicular to a 0.4 T field. Force magnitude is F=0.4×2×0.05=0.040 N. If a simplified rotor arm is 0.03 m perpendicular to that force, its torque contribution is about 0.040×0.03=0.0012 N·m. Real machines have many conductors, geometry factors and time-varying commutation. If the direction of this conductor current flips while the magnetic field stays fixed, the 0.040 N force reverses direction."],["B-046 independent check","Independent check: a simplified conductor is 0.10 m long in a uniform 0.20 T field, with 3 A perpendicular current. Predict force 0.20×3×0.10=0.060 N. What happens when current becomes 1.5 A with field unchanged? Force halves to 0.030 N. What happens if current direction instead reverses at the same magnitude? Force direction reverses. Explain why the motor also needs geometry and correct current timing to maintain usable shaft rotation."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-047","module":"M11","page":"L08-1-01","reason":"The datasheet-selection lesson needs an explicit worked forward-bias power and reverse-blocking margin example for competency 3.7.2.","paragraphs":[["B-047 prerequisite model","For a silicon diode conducting forward current I_F with forward drop V_F at a specified operating temperature, approximate instantaneous forward dissipation as P_F=I_F×V_F. In a rectifier, average thermal dissipation must also account for current waveform, conduction duty cycle and device thermal impedance; a snapshot product is not automatically whole-cycle average power. When reverse-biased, a diode should block until breakdown/rating limits, with nonzero leakage. Compare the circuit’s worst repetitive reverse peak (including justified transients) against the datasheet repetitive peak reverse voltage VRRM with an appropriate margin. Check forward average/surge current, package dissipation, junction temperature, reverse leakage and actual conditions. Absolute-maximum ratings are not target operating points."],["B-047 worked example","Worked: with a diode forward drop of 0.80 V at 0.30 A, instantaneous forward conduction power is 0.80×0.30=0.24 W. If the circuit expects 40 V repetitive reverse peaks, a VRRM less than 40 V is not acceptable; choosing 40 V exactly offers no tolerance/transient allowance. A 100 V-rated candidate may offer voltage headroom, but still requires current, switching, thermal and waveform checks. It is incorrect to multiply 40 V reverse blocking by 0.30 A forward conduction current to infer 12 W reverse dissipation; those are different operating intervals."],["B-047 independent check","Independent check: a diode has V_F=0.65 V at I_F=0.50 A and may see 60 V repetitive reverse peaks. Estimate forward loss during conduction: 0.325 W. Would a 50 V VRRM-rated diode be suitable? No, its repetitive rating is below expected 60 V. A 100 V part might meet reverse requirement but must still meet current, surge, temperature and application-specific margin. Explain why an average thermal power calculation requires the conduction waveform instead of using 0.325 W as a guaranteed full-cycle average."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-049","module":"M15","page":"L29-0-02","reason":"Existing solder lesson names families and eutectic/pasty behavior but needs an applied material-qualification example.","paragraphs":[["B-049 prerequisite model","An alloy is selected against the controlled assembly specification before comparing melting temperatures. Sn63/Pb37 is a lead-bearing near-eutectic soft solder that melts around 183 °C; SAC305 (nominal Sn96.5/Ag3.0/Cu0.5) is a common lead-free electronics alloy with a roughly 217–220 °C melting interval; Sn-Bi alloys may melt substantially lower but can have different brittleness and metallurgical reliability. Alloy identity, flux chemistry, plated finishes, component temperature limits, rework history and local restricted-substance rules determine eligibility. Never treat the lowest processing temperature as a universal substitute for qualified reliability."],["B-049 worked example","Worked: an OEM repair procedure specifies SAC305, a compatible flux and a controlled lead-free heat cycle. A stocked Sn63/Pb37 spool could reflow sooner, and a Sn-Bi wire could reduce peak temperature, but both are material substitutions requiring OEM approval. The compliant choice is the specified SAC305 with the specified process, while checking component/PCB temperature limits. A technician documents the alloy lot and rework profile rather than turning up the iron until a joint looks shiny."],["B-049 independent check","Independent check: a second board is explicitly approved for Sn63/Pb37 but not for SAC305. Can you automatically switch to SAC305 because lead-free sounds newer? No; verify documentation, product applicability and compatible thermal limits. Explain how melting range affects dwell/process temperature and why wetting alone does not prove long-term fatigue or mixed-alloy reliability."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-052","module":"M22","page":"L19-0-13","reason":"Teach center-versus-shield fault localization and why a single continuity reading is insufficient.","paragraphs":[["B-052 prerequisite model","Coax has two conductive paths: a center conductor for signal and an outer shield for its reference/return and field containment, separated by dielectric. A connector must preserve contact pressure, shield bond, characteristic-impedance geometry and mechanical strain support. Center-pin continuity alone cannot prove outer-conductor continuity. A bend-dependent fault can be a cracked braid-to-shell bond, damaged center crimp, inadequate jacket grip or mating-interface wear. Isolate equipment and follow approved de-energized and RF testing procedures before flexing or disconnecting a cable; matching connector threads is not proof of RF interchangeability."],["B-052 worked example","Worked: a 50 Ω coax interconnect passes a 0.4 Ω center-pin end-to-end DC check, but the shield-to-shield resistance intermittently becomes open as the connector boot is flexed. This points to a shield termination/strain defect, not necessarily a transmitter gain setting. Inspect the shield braid bond, ferrule, jacket support, center seating, correct connector series and wear; replace or reterminate with approved hardware, then check the assembly under the required RF service test as well as DC continuity."],["B-052 independent check","Independent check: a different cable has good shield continuity but an intermittent center-pin open when flexed. Which path is suspect? The center conductor/contact and its strain support. Why does a connector with identical mating threads but 75 Ω nominal impedance not automatically replace a 50 Ω RF assembly? Because impedance, frequency and mechanical/return geometry affect reflections and performance."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-055","module":"M28","page":"L28-0-04","reason":"Provide explicit cross-reference matrix interpretation and what to document after an unapproved proposed substitution.","paragraphs":[["B-055 prerequisite model","A part number alone is not a full compatibility claim. Service parts may have family IDs, suffixes denoting hardware revision, firmware boundaries, alternate suppliers and serialized or equipment-revision applicability. Use the latest applicable controlled OEM compatibility/substitution matrix, exact equipment model/revision, regulatory/safety changes, electrical pin assignments, calibration/software and service bulletins before installing a substitute. A matching connector shell or higher apparent revision number cannot supersede an explicit not-approved note. If documentation conflicts, hold the replacement and request authorized engineering/service clarification."],["B-055 worked example","Worked: an R3 device originally uses board P-240-B. The OEM table lists P-240-C as an approved direct substitute only for R4 units; no R3 retrofit is listed. Physically matching connectors do not overturn the documented R4-only boundary. Record the R3 equipment revision and proposed part suffix, request an R3-approved replacement or controlled retrofit instruction, and do not install C just because it is newer."],["B-055 independent check","Independent check: a vendor catalog calls P-240-D a cross-reference to P-240-B, but the OEM service bulletin says D requires a new harness and calibration for R2 units. Can D be treated as plug-and-play in R2? No. Which artifacts should be retained in the repair record? Exact unit model/revision, candidate and installed part IDs/revisions, bulletin or compatibility-matrix version, approvals and postrepair verification steps."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-058","module":"M07","page":"L07-0-05","reason":"Add quantitative bias-qualification logic for ferrite core selection under ripple.","paragraphs":[["B-058 prerequisite model","Core permeability governs flux linkage and coil inductance in a given geometry, while loss depends on frequency, AC flux swing, material composition and temperature. A DC bias may drive a magnetic core toward saturation, reducing incremental inductance; the circuit can then draw a larger ripple current than intended. Winding copper resistance causes separate I²R heating and does not establish core loss. Different ferrite compositions and powder/laminated cores suit different frequency, flux and bias ranges. Specify inductance at the relevant current, allowable temperature rise, saturation/current curves, and loss characteristics before approving a substitution."],["B-058 worked example","Worked: a choke must carry 0.8 A DC plus ripple near 500 kHz. Core A has a nominal 100 µH at negligible bias but its inductance collapses near 0.6 A; core B provides the specified inductance through at least the needed current range with acceptable 500 kHz loss. Core A is not rescued by its lower unloaded loss or higher no-load permeability. At 0.8 A through a winding of 0.4 Ω, copper loss alone is I²R=0.64×0.4=0.256 W; core losses and ripple-current contributions still need checking."],["B-058 independent check","Independent check: a replacement ferrite core has the correct nominal L at 1 kHz but its loss chart ends at 100 kHz; the circuit runs at 800 kHz. Can the available figure alone establish equivalence? No. Identify at least two additional checks: impedance/permeability versus frequency, core loss at actual flux and frequency, saturation under DC bias, thermal ratings, geometry and winding resistance."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-062","module":"M15","page":"L12-0-04","reason":"Ledger confirms direct BGA lesson lacks explicit MSL, exposure history, qualified profile and hidden-joint inspection.","paragraphs":[["B-062 prerequisite model","Ball-grid arrays contain solder joints beneath the package body that cannot be fully inspected by a top-side glance. Mechanical prying while solid can tear lands and damage laminate. Before rework, identify package part and moisture-sensitivity level (MSL), packaging/dry-storage and floor-life history, and manufacturer or approved workmanship requirements; baking is a controlled remedy only when specified for the particular package and conditions. Protect ESD-sensitive assemblies. Use appropriate preheat, top/bottom temperature profile, board-support fixtures and controlled reflow/removal; inspect hidden joints with an approved method such as X-ray when required, in addition to electrical tests. Do not infer a universal bake temperature or visual acceptance standard for every BGA."],["B-062 worked example","Worked: a moisture-sensitive BGA is removed from dry-pack and its permitted exposure time expires before repair begins. The operator first verifies its exact MSL and the authorized dry handling/reconditioning instructions, documents the status, and does not simply add a few degrees to the reflow peak. An approved profile is measured at defined locations to limit package/PCB thermal gradients and warpage. Hidden balls cannot be judged by the package top; qualification or acceptance may require X-ray and electrical functional tests under the applicable procedure."],["B-062 independent check","Independent check: two BGA packages have similar dimensions but different floor-life limits and manufacturer rework profiles. Can the profile for one automatically be used on the other? No. Why could a clean-looking top surface conceal a bridged or open ball? The joint is beneath the package. List the information needed before proceeding: part and MSL, exposure history, drying guidance, profile, support/ESD controls, and specified inspection criteria."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-065","module":"M22","page":"L19-0-05","reason":"Give direct modular-connector procedure and wire-map interpretation absent from assigned generic crimp example.","paragraphs":[["B-065 prerequisite model","Modular copper connectors must match cable category, conductor type and diameter, plug design and the specified wiring convention. The jacket should be retained by the plug strain relief; excessive pair untwist degrades balance and can worsen crosstalk. Follow the specified conductor ordering (commonly T568A or T568B pin assignments for RJ45-style 8P8C terminations), use the manufacturer-approved crimp tool and inspect contact seating without damaged insulation. A cable tester’s continuity/wire-map result detects open, short, reversed or transposed conductors; a correct visual latch or shield continuity cannot substitute for an electrically correct pair map. Where service standards require it, perform additional link-certification/performance tests."],["B-065 worked example","Worked: a newly made cable has all eight contacts at equal height, the jacket is firmly crimped and the tester indicates pin 3 at end A maps to pin 6 at end B, and pin 6 maps to pin 3. If a straight-through wiring assignment was specified, this is a miswire despite attractive cosmetics. Verify the specified wiring convention at both ends, replace/repair the unsuitable termination with an approved plug and re-test wire map and required high-frequency performance."],["B-065 independent check","Independent check: a tester reports all pins correct but a connector has no jacket retention and its pairs are untwisted several centimeters. Is the termination automatically acceptable? No; mechanically inappropriate support and excessive untwist can impair reliability and performance. Name both a tool and a check used for acceptance: compatible crimp tool and a cable wire-map/continuity tester, plus a suitable certification tester where required."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-066","module":"M24","page":"L19-0-01","reason":"Explicitly link alarm-security interface to audiovisual control logic and explain dry-contact versus powered triggers.","paragraphs":[["B-066 prerequisite model","An alarm panel may expose isolated dry relay contacts (normally open/common or normally closed), a powered logic output or a communications/event interface. A dry contact switches an external circuit but does not generate 12 V by itself. An audiovisual controller may instead require a powered 12 V trigger with specified polarity, voltage tolerance and permitted current. Interfacing an alarm/security system to entertainment equipment (ETA objective 17.5) means identifying both sides, checking whether security monitoring/isolation requirements permit interconnection, selecting an approved interface/power source and maintaining safety and security functions. Connector shape alone does not establish a compatible voltage or reference."],["B-066 worked example","Worked: the alarm panel has a dry relay contact rated to switch a specified low-voltage current, while an AV controller needs a positive 12 V trigger input that draws 25 mA. The relay contact alone cannot source that voltage. Consult both manuals, verify whether a listed 12 V isolated supply and the rated contact can switch the trigger (including inrush and common-return restrictions), or use the OEM-approved interface module. Ensure no alteration of alarm supervision/certification, and perform approved low-voltage tests with the appropriate authority."],["B-066 independent check","Independent check: another alarm panel supplies 5 V logic at a maximum 10 mA, and the AV input specifies 12 V at 30 mA. May the 5 V logic output be wired straight in? No; voltage and current requirements are incompatible. Propose the safe decision process: check panel and AV specifications, choose a properly rated isolated or approved interposing interface, and verify alarm functionality and service requirements remain intact."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-071","module":"M05","page":"L13-0-04","reason":"Localize approved test points and safe reference selection before interpreting a regulator reading.","paragraphs":[["B-071 prerequisite model","A test point is a documented, accessible circuit node chosen for repeatable measurement of a signal, supply, or reference. A schematic label such as GND, SIGNAL_RETURN or TP7 describes a particular net; it does not certify a protective-earth connection or imply that a grounded bench oscilloscope may clip to it. First establish equipment isolation/reference and permitted test method, then choose the measuring tip and return according to documentation. A TP on the regulator output serves a different diagnostic role from a TP on the return rail."],["B-071 worked example","Worked: a de-energized, safely prepared 3.3 V training board identifies TP8 as 3V3_OUT and TP7 as SIGNAL_RETURN. A verified and appropriately rated DC voltmeter set to voltage measures 3.29 V between TP8 and TP7. That supports a normal output at the test condition, but not a conclusion that output ripple, transient response or load regulation are correct. A chassis screw is not a substitute for the schematic return without confirming the applicable bonding and reference topology."],["B-071 independent check","Independent check: another diagram labels TP9 at regulator input and TP10 at its enable pin. If TP8 reads 0 V, explain why checking the approved input-to-return and enable-to-return locations distinguishes missing supply, disabled regulation and a faulty output stage. State why a labeled GND pad cannot independently authorize unsafe connections to line-derived or floating supplies."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-072","module":"M08","page":"L06-0-03","reason":"Distinguish series-resonant residual resistance, reactive voltage magnification and parallel resonance.","paragraphs":[["B-072 prerequisite model","A series RLC impedance is Z = R + j(omega L - 1/(omega C)). The resonant angular frequency is omega0 = 1/sqrt(LC) and f0 = 1/(2 pi sqrt(LC)); at resonance the two reactances are opposite and net X is zero. The resistor and physical losses remain, so a finite practical source current is V_RMS/R_total. The ideal lossless series LC fed by an ideal AC voltage source at exact resonance has no finite steady-state solution; do not silently treat it as a zero-current open circuit. Capacitor and inductor individual RMS voltages may greatly exceed supply voltage."],["B-072 worked example","Worked: for L = 0.010 H and C = 0.000001 F, f0 is about 1592 Hz. Each ideal reactance is roughly 100 ohms in magnitude; in series they cancel, leaving R = 20 ohms. At 10 V RMS, I = 10/20 = 0.50 A RMS. The inductor or capacitor may each have about 50 V RMS even though the applied supply is 10 V, because those reactive voltages oppose one another in phase."],["B-072 independent check","Independent check: replace the 20-ohm series resistance with 40 ohms, keeping L and C unchanged. The ideal resonant frequency is unchanged (ignoring parasitics), while a 10 V RMS source now delivers 0.25 A RMS at resonance. Explain why a parallel RLC can instead present a high source input impedance at parallel resonance, even though a series RLC reaches its resistive minimum."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-073","module":"M10","page":"L11-0-09","reason":"Connect multivibrator subtypes to broader oscillator behavior instead of a binary false distinction.","paragraphs":[["B-073 prerequisite model","An oscillator is a circuit that produces a repeating waveform without requiring a new external trigger for each cycle. LC, crystal and RC oscillators may use different frequency-setting mechanisms and produce sinusoidal or approximately clock-like outputs. A multivibrator is a switching circuit with distinct states: astable repeatedly alternates and therefore qualifies as an oscillator; monostable has one stable resting state and generates a timed response to each accepted trigger; bistable has two stable states and stores a bit. Not every multivibrator runs continuously, and not every oscillator is an astable multivibrator topology."],["B-073 worked example","Worked: a 32.768 kHz crystal-based timer clock produces periodic edges for a counter, and a 555 one-shot produces a 5 ms pulse when a limit-switch transition is accepted. The crystal-based clock is a free-running oscillator while the one-shot is monostable. A separately configured 555 astable could also produce a continuous clock, though its drift and waveform properties may differ from the crystal generator."],["B-073 independent check","Independent check: a flip-flop keeps a logic HIGH indefinitely after SET and holds LOW after RESET. Name its multivibrator category and explain why it is not a clock oscillator. Then compare an RC astable that cycles without external triggers to a monostable that holds its output until triggered. Sketch the rest-versus-trigger/clock timing in words before selecting a circuit."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-074","module":"M14","page":"L10-0-04","reason":"Bridge device transconductance to FET common-drain voltage gain and load power.","paragraphs":[["B-074 prerequisite model","A small-signal MOSFET source follower is a common-drain buffer: its output follows the input gate voltage, with DC offset set by bias, finite headroom, and incremental voltage gain ordinarily below one. For a simple model ignoring body effect and output conductance, Av approximately equals gm*R/(1+gm*R), where R is the effective AC resistance at the source, including any attached load in parallel with the source-bias path. gm is in siemens (A/V), so gmR is dimensionless. This is not a universal formula for every FET topology or high-signal regime."],["B-074 worked example","Worked: gm = 0.004 S and R = 1000 ohms gives gmR = 4, so Av is 4/5 = 0.80. A 0.10 V RMS small-signal gate input produces approximately 0.080 V RMS at the source if the stage remains in its linear region. If that voltage appears across a separate 1000-ohm load, small-signal load power is V_RMS^2/R = 0.0064/1000 = 6.4 microwatts. Check that the effective AC R used for gain includes that load; this illustration cannot be applied twice to ignore loading."],["B-074 independent check","Independent check: if the effective source resistance falls to 500 ohms at the same gm, compute gmR=2 and Av approximately 2/3, about 0.667. For a 0.15 V RMS gate input this gives 0.10 V RMS at the source before clipping. Explain why lower source output impedance can be valuable even when voltage gain is below unity, and why bias voltage, drain supply, current capability and thermal limits still require independent verification."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-076","module":"M21","page":"L17-0-05","reason":"Distinguish filesystem checking from imaging, backup, physical media tests and antivirus utilities.","paragraphs":[["B-076 prerequisite model","A filesystem manages metadata such as file names, directory relationships, block allocation and free-space maps. A filesystem consistency checker such as fsck (on appropriate Unix-family filesystems) or chkdsk (on supported Windows filesystems) examines logical metadata and can sometimes repair inconsistencies. Disk imaging copies blocks for recovery or migration; SMART and diagnostics report storage-device health; antivirus tools scan for threats. These are different purposes. Filesystem repair itself can change records or orphan files, so protect recoverable data and follow platform-appropriate mounting/recovery instructions before invoking a repair mode."],["B-076 worked example","Worked: an instrument control computer boots but a data partition mounts read-only following an unexpected shutdown. Logs indicate directory-allocation errors; a manufacturer-approved and recoverable backup copy is taken. The technician selects an offline or appropriately scheduled filesystem consistency checker for that filesystem and reviews its report, rather than replacing drivers or assuming disk imaging verifies logical integrity. If physical-media errors also appear, the recovery strategy changes to preserve failing-media data first."],["B-076 independent check","Independent check: a disk has slow reads, increasing uncorrectable sectors and corrupted directories. Which tool diagnoses device condition and which checks logical structure? SMART/vendor diagnostics assess hardware condition; a compatible filesystem checker examines metadata, but running aggressive repair on failing media may worsen data loss. Explain why an image/backup before authorized repair is a safety net rather than a filesystem validation guarantee."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-077","module":"M23","page":"L03-0-04","reason":"Distinguish counter triggering errors from limited resolution and oscillator instability.","paragraphs":[["B-077 prerequisite model","A frequency counter counts recognized threshold crossings during a defined gate time. A short gate interval can increase quantization uncertainty in hertz, while input amplitude, threshold, coupling, noise and hysteresis govern whether one physical cycle produces one valid count. Noise can create repeated threshold crossings (extra counts), and too little amplitude can produce missed counts. Increasing gate time improves count resolution but cannot repair an unsuitable trigger level or remove multiple counted transitions. A scope is helpful for waveform shape and noise, but its own triggering must also be configured correctly."],["B-077 worked example","Worked: a reference source is 1.000 MHz and a counter sometimes reads 2.000 MHz, while the scope shows a noisy rising edge. A second spurious threshold crossing per physical cycle can cause roughly double counts; examine input termination, trigger level, noise filtering and allowed sensitivity, then select a gate time sufficient to achieve resolution. Do not replace an oscillator until independent source measurement supports a genuine source-frequency fault."],["B-077 independent check","Independent check: a clean 10 Hz signal is measured with a gate time of 0.1 s. Only about one cycle fits the gate interval, so counting resolution is poor. Would extending the gate time help? Yes for count resolution. If a different 10 Hz waveform rings and crosses the threshold three times per rising edge, would longer gate time by itself cure the systematic count error? No; correct the trigger settings or signal conditioning first."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-078","module":"M25","page":"L19-0-09","reason":"Explain OLED self-emission, uneven subpixel aging and distinctions from reversible image retention.","paragraphs":[["B-078 prerequisite model","Each OLED pixel emits light; it does not require an LCD-style common transmissive backlight. Organic emissive material changes with cumulative emission, drive, brightness, color and temperature, so a high-duty-cycle static logo can age some subpixels more than neighboring pixels. Persistent nonuniformity after input source changes and a uniform pattern may indicate differential burn-in; transient retention can also occur and should not be instantly labeled permanent. Pixel-refresh and compensation behavior vary by manufacturer and should be used only as documented."],["B-078 worked example","Worked: a display shows a white static menu for months. After switching to a clean mid-gray test field from a known-good source, a fixed faint menu silhouette remains at exactly the previously bright region. Because the pattern tracks historical on-panel use rather than incoming image content, differential emissive aging is more plausible than a bad HDMI cable. Check the product diagnostic/compensation procedures before classifying the panel, and do not promise software repair of irreversible material wear."],["B-078 independent check","Independent check: a similar display has a ghost image for 30 seconds after a bright scene but the pattern disappears after a manufacturer-approved retention-recovery interval. Why is that weaker evidence for permanent burn-in? Because short-term retention can clear. A permanently dark LCD panel with a faint flashlight-revealed image instead suggests an LCD backlight issue, which is a different technology and failure mechanism."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-081","module":"M08","page":"L04-0-02","reason":"Extend reciprocals for parallel impedances to the phase relationship among R, L and C branch currents.","paragraphs":[["B-081 prerequisite model","In a parallel network, each branch sees the same applied AC voltage. A resistor draws in-phase current I_R=V/R; an ideal inductor draws current lagging voltage by 90 degrees with magnitude V/XL; an ideal capacitor draws current leading voltage by 90 degrees with magnitude V/|XC|. Total current adds as complex phasors: I_total=I_R+j(I_C-I_L). Therefore |I_total|=sqrt(I_R^2+(I_C-I_L)^2), and its phase relative to voltage depends on whether the net reactive current leads or lags. Naively adding all magnitudes ignores phase and can misstate load, breaker sizing and power factor."],["B-081 worked example","Worked: a 10 V RMS source feeds R=100 ohms, XL=100 ohms and |XC|=50 ohms in parallel. I_R=0.10 A, I_L=0.10 A lagging and I_C=0.20 A leading. Net I=0.10+j0.10 A, magnitude sqrt(0.01+0.01)=0.141 A, phase +45 degrees: current leads voltage. Summing 0.10+0.10+0.20=0.40 A would be wrong. This assumes ideal elements at the same specified frequency and negligible source impedance."],["B-081 independent check","Independent check: if XC is instead 100 ohms, capacitive and inductive currents each have magnitude 0.10 A and cancel reactively. Total current becomes 0.10 A in phase, determined by the resistor branch. If XC falls to 25 ohms, I_C rises to 0.40 A, so net reactive current becomes +0.30 A; find sqrt(0.10^2+0.30^2)=0.316 A and explain why the source current now leads more strongly."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-083","module":"M14","page":"L10-0-01","reason":"Compute small-signal gain versus frequency and distinguish bandwidth limits from slew-rate or output clipping.","paragraphs":[["B-083 prerequisite model","For a defined small-signal voltage transfer gain A_v=Vout_RMS/Vin_RMS measured at the same load/reference, G_dB=20 log10(|A_v|). A falling output amplitude at constant input as frequency increases can indicate frequency-response roll-off, but the instrument bandwidth, probe factor, source impedance, load and distortion must also be checked. Small-signal bandwidth and slew rate are different specifications: a stage can satisfy gain-bandwidth but distort a sufficiently large or fast output."],["B-083 worked example","Worked: an amplifier accepts 0.10 V RMS at 1 kHz and 100 kHz; outputs are 1.0 V and 0.20 V RMS respectively. Voltage gain decreases from 10 to 2. In decibels these are 20 log10(10)=20 dB and 20 log10(2)≈6 dB. The drop is approximately 14 dB between the measured frequencies. Without waveform or input/output slew-rate evidence, the data alone do not prove that slew limiting rather than ordinary frequency roll-off is the cause."],["B-083 independent check","Independent check: another stage has 0.05 V RMS input at 10 kHz and 0.50 V RMS output, but at 500 kHz it has only 0.10 V RMS output with the same input. Calculate voltage gains 10 and 2; express each in dB and identify the direction of the response change. State an instrument/probe check that could distinguish true circuit roll-off from a measurement setup artifact."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-085","module":"M21","page":"L17-0-04","reason":"Provide the missing virus and malware defense/incident-response teaching for official 16.7.","paragraphs":[["B-085 prerequisite model","Malware includes viruses that replicate by infecting host files and other threats such as worms, trojans and ransomware, each with different propagation and harm mechanisms. Defensive layers include reputable updated endpoint protection, least-privilege accounts, verified software distribution, secure updates, segmented access and separate restorable backups. Code signing establishes a claim about publisher identity and file integrity under a trust chain; it does not prove the signed program is safe. Detections can be false positives or genuine compromise. Only authorized incident-response personnel should decide whether to quarantine, allowlist, isolate or restore critical software."],["B-085 worked example","Worked: a service computer runs a signed equipment calibration utility. After a detection update, antivirus flags its executable while raw calibration records remain critical to production. The technician does not disable protection forever or destroy evidence. Follow site policy, preserve recovery points, confirm hash and publisher, gather detection details, and refer to security/OEM as appropriate before any allowlisting or quarantine decision. Electrical line surges require different countermeasures, such as correctly rated surge protective devices and compliant grounding; antimalware software does not protect from physical overvoltage."],["B-085 independent check","Independent check: an unsigned program downloaded from an unverified mirror has altered executable hash and begins unauthorized network calls. Is absence of a signature by itself proof of malware? No, but the combined evidence justifies approved investigation and containment. Contrast that response with an instrument that reboots after lightning-related transient events: inspect appropriate site electrical surge controls rather than running an antivirus scan as the sole safeguard."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-087","module":"M25","page":"L19-0-10","reason":"Teach phototransistor current-to-voltage behavior with a pull-up load and its limits.","paragraphs":[["B-087 prerequisite model","A phototransistor is a light-sensitive transistor in which incident photons generate carriers equivalent to base drive. In a common NPN collector-output connection, the emitter is tied to the intended reference and the collector connects through a resistor to positive supply. Increasing light generally increases collector current until circuit/saturation limits. The pull-up resistor then drops more voltage, so collector output voltage usually FALLS with additional illumination. This inversion surprises learners who assume every light sensor emits a proportionally higher output voltage. Wavelength sensitivity, temperature, supply/load and bias condition all matter."],["B-087 worked example","Worked: with VCC=5.0 V and RC=10 kilo-ohms, a collector output of 4.8 V implies approximate collector current (5.0-4.8)/10000=0.020 mA. Under brighter light output falls to 1.2 V, implying (5.0-1.2)/10000=0.380 mA. Current rises while collector voltage falls. These calculations assume the resistor connects directly to a stable 5 V rail, emitter is at 0 V and external measurement loading is negligible."],["B-087 independent check","Independent check: replace the pull-up with 5 kilo-ohms while assuming collector current remains at 0.30 mA, and determine Vcollector approximately 5-0.0003*5000 = 3.5 V. Why can the output saturate near ground under very bright light? The circuit and device enter limiting regions, so current no longer scales linearly with illumination. Explain when a photodiode might be preferred for speed instead of the higher effective gain of a phototransistor."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-089","module":"M02","page":"L04-0-03","reason":"Connect SI symbol case to official NIST SP811 9.3 unit-name spelling exceptions.","paragraphs":[["B-089 prerequisite model","An SI prefix combines with a unit name to form a single word, generally without a space or hyphen. NIST SP 811 section 9.3 highlights customary spelling contractions when an SI prefix ending with a vowel is attached to ohm: kilo + ohm is spelled kilohm and mega + ohm is spelled megohm, not kiloohm or megaohm. Written symbol forms remain kΩ and MΩ, which use case-sensitive prefixes and the uppercase omega symbol for ohm. Unit names in ordinary prose are generally lowercase, even when their symbols derive from a person."],["B-089 worked example","Worked: a schematic identifies R1=1 kΩ and R2=1 MΩ. In a written narrative, R1 is one kilohm and R2 is one megohm. Their numerical values are 1000 Ω and 1000000 Ω, respectively; confusion between k and M represents a factor of 1000 here. Writing one kiloohm as the unit name misses the NIST spelling exception even if the numerical resistance is understood."],["B-089 independent check","Independent check: rewrite 4.7 kΩ and 2.2 MΩ as numerical ohms and spelled-out unit names, then distinguish the names from symbols: 4700 ohms or 4.7 kilohms, and 2200000 ohms or 2.2 megohms. Explain why the uppercase M versus lowercase m changes the meaning of a symbol, while the letter case used for the ordinary spelled-out unit name does not follow the same prefix-symbol convention."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-096","module":"M24","page":"L19-0-01","reason":"Provide a worked last-good-stage audio routing example without duplicating the revised assessment case.","paragraphs":[["B-096 prerequisite model","Audio inter-unit tracing depends on known level and reference expectations. A powered indicator establishes electrical power, not correct signal flow. A mixer can have a normal channel input meter while its main output is silent because the channel is not assigned to the mix bus, the output is muted, an insert return is missing, or the meter monitors a pre-fader point. Measure the last known-good and first missing signals using approved low-voltage methods, and verify format/impedance/cable before condemning hardware."],["B-096 worked example","Worked: a test tone plays through a power amplifier when directly connected but goes silent through a patch panel. A scope or proper audio tester sees line-level tone at patch input but not its output. Inspect patch routing, connector contact and patch wiring, while leaving functioning source and amplifier unchanged. If tone is present at the patch output but absent after a long cable, investigate that cable next; each measurement changes the likely fault boundary."],["B-096 independent check","Independent check: a player works through input A of a receiver, but not input B; an external reference tone also works through A and fails through B. Which path is most suspect? The receiver's B input selection, gain/routing or input circuitry rather than the player. Explain why swapping the player before tracing the known-good tone is less discriminating and how to document test signal level and receiving selection."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-097","module":"M26","page":"L20-0-03","reason":"Teach an ideal linear polarization overlap calculation and distinguish it from practical multipath effects.","paragraphs":[["B-097 prerequisite model","For two ideal linearly polarized antennas in free-space line of sight, orientation loss depends on the angle theta between their electric-field polarization axes. The aligned component of electric-field amplitude is proportional to cos(theta), so the received power fraction is cos²(theta). At 0 degrees the factor is one; at 45 degrees it is one-half, a 3.01 dB power reduction; at 90 degrees ideal crossed polarization gives zero coupled power. Real antenna cross-polar discrimination, reflections, circular/elliptical components and scattering complicate this simple relation; never claim infinite isolation in a real building."],["B-097 worked example","Worked: a reference receiver sees -50 dBm with antennas aligned. In a controlled direct path, rotating the receive linear antenna to 45 degrees predicts a 3.01 dB reduction to about -53 dBm, if every other component and path stays unchanged. A measurement of -55 dBm could include connector or propagation changes rather than purely polarization. A full polarization mismatch does not change transmit carrier frequency; it changes the field component the receiving antenna can couple."],["B-097 independent check","Independent check: for an ideal angle of 60 degrees, cos²(60 degrees)=0.25, or approximately -6 dB power loss. A manufacturer measures -2 dB in a multipath warehouse at the same nominal angle. Explain why the ideal model cannot uniquely predict the indoor measurement and why antenna orientation alone is not evidence of transmitter failure."]]},{"id":"ALF52-B-100","module":"M13","page":"L08-0-00","reason":"Differentiate L, T and pi low-pass filters by series/shunt connections and physical output location.","paragraphs":[["B-100 prerequisite model","A low-pass choke-input L section can be drawn as source -> series L -> output with capacitor C shunting that output node to return. An L-C-L T two-port adds another series choke after the intermediate shunt capacitor; its conventional load output is connected after the second choke. By contrast, a C-L-C pi section has a first capacitor shunting the input, one series choke, then a second capacitor shunting the output. These are topological definitions; performance depends on loading, parasitic resistance, saturation, resonance and component ratings. The official ETA objective 10.4.2 has terse output-across-capacitor wording; read the published wording alongside the actual two-port circuit rather than silently substituting an inconsistent drawing."],["B-100 worked example","Worked: given input rectifier node -> L1 (series) -> node A -> L2 (series) -> load, with C from node A to return, draw the circuit and label a T-section L1-C-L2. If the output probe is at node A, it observes the first L-C stage; the load after L2 sees the complete T filter. If a second capacitor were instead added at the load after the original series choke, the network could become a different C-L-C or L-C-C arrangement depending on the original input topology; do not classify from capacitor count alone."],["B-100 independent check","Independent check: identify input -> C1 to return -> series L -> C2 to return -> load as a pi section, not a T section. Identify input -> L1 series -> C to return -> load as an L section. If a second series choke is inserted between the capacitor node and load, show why the topology now becomes L-C-L T and why the load connection should not be confused with the shunt-capacitor midpoint."]]}];
  const INSTRUCTION_ONLY = [{"id":"ALF52-A-022","finding":"CV3-007","action":"Keep original exam stem, technically correct key and plausible choices; add missing Boolean distributive-law worked teaching at M16/L11-0-03."},{"id":"ALF52-A-030","finding":"AV-030","action":"Keep the original exam stem, mathematically correct key (36 Wh) and plausible options; existing Batch02 teaching addition at M03/L01-0-02 explicitly derives E=P×t and converts Wh to joules. Historical disposition register omission fixed in Checkpoint06; teaching unchanged."},{"id":"ALF52-A-098","finding":"AV-098 / CV3-006","action":"Keep existing mathematically sound item and plausible exponent traps, keyed index 3. Already drafted CV3-006 at M02/L04-0-03 explicitly derives 10^3 × 10^(−2) = 10, verifies as 1000 × 0.01, and includes independent multiplication of negative powers. Teaching-only disposition; no new answer distractors."}];
  const DOCUMENTED_EXCEPTIONS = [{"id":"ALF52-A-049","decision":"JUSTIFIED_NO_EDIT_ALREADY_REMEDIED_IN_FROZEN_SOURCE","expectedPrompt":"A Class B push-pull output stage exhibits a dead zone around the zero crossing. Which cause is most plausible?","expectedAnswerIndex":0,"expectedChoices":["Insufficient bias to overcome device conduction thresholds creates crossover distortion","Excessive idle bias that keeps both output devices conducting through the signal’s zero crossing","Clipping at the supply rails when the amplifier is driven close to its maximum output swing","Low-frequency attenuation caused by an undersized input coupling capacitor and load resistance"],"objectiveId":"11.3","sourceModuleId":"M14","sourcePageId":"L10-0-07","evidence":"Read-only frozen course.js plus existing final production overlays contain three meaningful alternate amplifier mechanisms. Ledger A-049 lists outdated patently irrelevant distractors. Key index 0 is correct; lesson explicitly teaches crossover vs clipping. Further editing would violate do-not-change-functioning-material rule."}];

  const fail = (why) => {
    window.ALFRED_CETA_CLOSURE_STATUS = {ok:false,revision:REVISION,error:String(why)};
    if (typeof console !== 'undefined') console.error('Alfred CETa academic closure did not apply: '+why);
  };
  try {
    const data = window.ALFRED_CETA_DATA;
    if (!data || !Array.isArray(data.course)) throw Error('Missing course data');
    if (window.ALFRED_ACADEMIC_PATCH_STATUS?.ok !== true ||
        window.ALFRED_FINAL_ASSESSMENT_STATUS?.ok !== true)
      throw Error('Frozen upstream academic overlays must apply first');
    if (window.ALFRED_CETA_CLOSURE_STATUS?.ok === true)
      throw Error('Academic closure already installed; duplicate script prevented');
    const modules = new Map(data.course.map(m => [m.id,m]));
    const pages = new Map();
    for (const p of GAP_PATCHES) {
      if (p.paragraphs.length !== 3 || !/^CV3-\d{3}$/.test(p.finding))
        throw Error('Malformed teaching entry '+p.finding);
      const mod = modules.get(p.module);
      const page = mod?.pages?.find(x => x.id === p.page);
      if (!page || !Array.isArray(page.goldTeaching?.paragraphs))
        throw Error('Target teaching page not found '+p.module+'/'+p.page);
      if (page.goldTeaching.paragraphs.some(e=>e[0]===p.paragraphs[0][0]))
        throw Error('Target teaching already applied '+p.finding);
      pages.set(p.finding,page);
    }
    const extraPages = new Map();
    for (const p of ITEM_TEACHING) {
      const mod = modules.get(p.module);
      const page = mod?.pages?.find(x => x.id === p.page);
      if (!page || !Array.isArray(page.goldTeaching?.paragraphs) ||
          !Array.isArray(p.paragraphs) || p.paragraphs.length !== 3)
        throw Error('Missing or malformed targeted teaching '+p.id);
      if (page.goldTeaching.paragraphs.some(e => e[0] === p.paragraphs[0][0]))
        throw Error('Targeted teaching already applied '+p.id);
      extraPages.set(p.id,page);
    }
    const radio = modules.get('M27')?.pages?.find(x=>x.id==='L20-0-07');
    const wrong = 'Simplex communication typically alternates transmit and receive on one shared channel';
    if (!radio || !radio.text.includes(wrong) ||
        !radio.goldTeaching.paragraphs[0][1].includes(wrong))
      throw Error('Known radio simplex wording changed; correction needs source review');
    const questions = new Map();
    for (const m of ['M29','M30']) {
      const form = modules.get(m)?.freshExamQuestionsV52;
      if (!Array.isArray(form) || form.length!==100) throw Error('Current mock bank missing '+m);
      for (const q of form) {
        if (questions.has(q.id)) throw Error('Duplicate exam ID '+q.id);
        questions.set(q.id,q);
      }
    }
    if (questions.size !== 200) throw Error('Expected 200 current mock items');
    for (const p of ITEM_TEACHING) {
      if (!questions.has(p.id)) throw Error('Targeted teaching exam reference missing '+p.id);
    }
    for (const p of ITEM_PATCHES) {
      const q = questions.get(p.id);
      if (!q || q.prompt !== p.expectedPrompt || q.answer !== p.expectedAnswerIndex ||
          JSON.stringify(q.choices)!==JSON.stringify(p.expectedChoices) ||
          (p.expectedObjectiveIds && JSON.stringify(q.objectiveIds)!==JSON.stringify(p.expectedObjectiveIds)) ||
          (p.expectedFocusObjectiveId && q.focusObjectiveId!==p.expectedFocusObjectiveId))
        throw Error('Effective post-overlay item mismatch '+p.id);
      if (p.newObjectiveIds && (!Array.isArray(p.newObjectiveIds) ||
          p.newObjectiveIds.length!==1 || p.newFocusObjectiveId!==p.newObjectiveIds[0] ||
          !modules.get(p.sourceModuleId)?.objectives?.some(o=>o.id===p.newFocusObjectiveId)))
        throw Error('Objective mapping correction invalid '+p.id);
      if (p.newChoices.length !== 4 ||
          new Set(p.newChoices).size !== 4 ||
          !p.newChoices.every(x=>typeof x==='string'&&x.trim().length>0))
        throw Error('Invalid replacement choices '+p.id);
      if (p.sourceModuleId && !modules.get(p.sourceModuleId)?.pages?.some(x=>x.id===p.sourcePageId))
        throw Error('Replacement teaching link missing '+p.id);
    }
    for (const p of INSTRUCTION_ONLY) {
      if (!questions.has(p.id)) throw Error('Instruction-only exam reference missing '+p.id);
    }
    for (const p of DOCUMENTED_EXCEPTIONS) {
      const q=questions.get(p.id);
      if (!q || q.prompt!==p.expectedPrompt || q.answer!==p.expectedAnswerIndex ||
          JSON.stringify(q.choices)!==JSON.stringify(p.expectedChoices) ||
          q.focusObjectiveId!==p.objectiveId || q.sourceModuleId!==p.sourceModuleId ||
          q.sourcePageId!==p.sourcePageId)
        throw Error('Source-backed no-edit exception mismatch '+p.id);
    }
    // All preconditions have passed: apply in a deterministic, idempotence-guarded pass.
    radio.text = radio.text.replace(wrong,'Shared-channel push-to-talk communication typically alternates transmit and receive and is half-duplex');
    radio.goldTeaching.paragraphs[0][1] = radio.goldTeaching.paragraphs[0][1].replace(wrong,'Shared-channel push-to-talk communication typically alternates transmit and receive and is half-duplex');
    for (const p of GAP_PATCHES) {
      pages.get(p.finding).goldTeaching.paragraphs.push(...p.paragraphs.map(a=>[a[0],a[1]]));
    }
    for (const p of ITEM_TEACHING) {
      extraPages.get(p.id).goldTeaching.paragraphs.push(...p.paragraphs.map(a=>[a[0],a[1]]));
    }
    for (const p of ITEM_PATCHES) {
      const q = questions.get(p.id);
      if (p.newPrompt !== undefined) q.prompt=p.newPrompt;
      q.choices=[...p.newChoices];
      if (p.sourceModuleId) {
        q.sourceModuleId=p.sourceModuleId; q.sourcePageId=p.sourcePageId;
        if (q.reviewedRemediationModuleId!==undefined) q.reviewedRemediationModuleId=p.sourceModuleId;
        if (q.reviewedRemediationPageId!==undefined) q.reviewedRemediationPageId=p.sourcePageId;
        if (q.remediationLinkEvidence && typeof q.remediationLinkEvidence==='object') {
          const pg=modules.get(p.sourceModuleId).pages.find(x=>x.id===p.sourcePageId);
          if (q.remediationLinkEvidence.pageTitle!==undefined) q.remediationLinkEvidence.pageTitle=pg.title;
          if (p.newFocusObjectiveId && q.remediationLinkEvidence.targetObjective!==undefined)
            q.remediationLinkEvidence.targetObjective=p.newFocusObjectiveId;
        }
      }
      if (p.newObjectiveIds) {
        q.objectiveIds=[...p.newObjectiveIds]; q.focusObjectiveId=p.newFocusObjectiveId;
      }
    }
    window.ALFRED_CETA_CLOSURE_STATUS = {
      ok:true,revision:REVISION,teachingFindingsAddressed:GAP_PATCHES.length,
      itemChanges:ITEM_PATCHES.length,targetedTeachingRepairs:ITEM_TEACHING.length,itemsAlignedByNewTeaching:INSTRUCTION_ONLY.length,documentedNoEditExceptions:DOCUMENTED_EXCEPTIONS.length,
      currentExamCount:questions.size,readinessValidated:false
    };
  } catch (e) {fail(e?.message||e);}
})();
